  }
  Action bd_ludd_read(actor: group, site: place) "Ask to read the Joke's part opposite him, cold, from the page" {
    Speaker ludd. Time 6.
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(ludd, site) and bd_ludd_told(actor) and not bd_written_in(actor) and not bd_ludd_leashed(actor) and not bd_ludd_exposed(actor).
    Resolve check(actor, performance, dc_hard).
    On success { Assert bd_written_in(actor). Increase bd_ludd_regard(actor) by 3. }
    Result "He gives you the page with both hands, his sleeves pulled down over his thumbs. You take it, and you stand where the door is, and you are wet, and small, and have been cut. 'I was cut at eleven this morning.' He answers, and for a moment he forgets to look at the page. It goes through the whole of it, and when you get to 'I keep it warm,' it is his line and he says it, and the house, the empty dark house, gives it back to him a half-beat late. 'There,' says Master Orlick, when it is over. He has to stop and begin again. 'There. That is what it is for.' And then, since he is who he is: 'The Joke would not have said ‘dripping’ in the second speech, it would have said ‘dribbling’, but I let it go.'".
    Otherwise "You read the Joke's part as if it were a bill of lading. He takes the page back, with great courtesy, and thanks you, and says that it is not the sort of part that can be got by reading. He means it kindly. It is not the sort of kindness a player can bear.".
