  Person elpis "Elpis Varo" at granary {
    Role "granary-reeve, who keeps the rota". Ancestry human. Pronouns she. Manner acerbic. Peaceable.
    Greets "A woman with a tally-stick in each fist and flour to the elbow does not look up from the board. 'Four hundred and sixty sacks,' she says. 'A sixth to the Sending. A twelfth to the Eagles' table. Never a sack to the children without a form.' She looks up. 'You'll want the form. Everyone does.'".
  }
  Topic elpis_owes with elpis "Remind the granary-reeve that the Hearth owes the one who paid a Hearth man's fine" when hearthOwes(party) once {
    Says "Elpis looks at you for the first time as if you were a sack to be weighed. 'Form,' she says, and writes it herself. 'Four days. Take them out of the north end, where it keeps.' She does not say thank you. She puts the stick behind her ear.".
    On discuss { Increase quantity(actor, rations) by 4. }
  }
  Topic corvin_dose with corvin "Tell the Prefect what Dame Orsa puts in his evening cup" when orsaConfessed(party) and admitted(party) and not doseTold(party) once {
    Says "Corvin sets down the cup he was lifting. The cup stays where it is. 'Sixteen years,' he says, 'of the clearest sleep in the valley.' He rings a small bell, and a clerk is sent down the steps for Dame Orsa, who comes up them slowly, and does not look at you. 'Thank you,' he says, and does not say what for.".
    On discuss { Assert doseTold(party). Increase prefectRegard(party) by 2. Retract at(orsa, apothecary). Assert at(orsa, praetorium_hall). }
  }
  Rule gaius_barley_pass when gaiusSecret(party) and at(party, pass_s) and not tolerates(deserters, party)
    says "The tall man on the boulder looks at the sack under the milestone, and at you, and at the sack. 'Gaius,' he says, and raises his bow an inch, which here is a greeting. They let you by." { Assert tolerates(deserters, party). }
  Topic elpis_tithe with elpis "Ask the granary-reeve about the sixth that goes north" when true {
    Says "'It is stood at the Sending gate in the spring in thirty sacks and thirty-one skins,' says Elpis. 'Then it is brought back in the autumn by the Sending and put in the Eagles' own store, and the Eagles eat it, nicely, as a reward for faith. Nobody has asked in eleven hundred years why it is brought back.' She taps the board. 'Sums are mine. Opinions go on a form. The form is in the drawer. The drawer is stuck.'".
  }
}

data/adventures/muster/companions.kyr:
Module muster "The Long Muster's Road" {
  // ── Four who go with the company, and what each wants of the road ──────────────────────────────────────────────
  // Marra Tellis, goatherd: wants her brother Jory, who crossed nine years ago; her flaw is pride. Fenn Thorley,
  // surveyor: wants his name on a map; his flaw is fear, and what he does with credit. Dunya, the Cohort's scout:
  // wants to see Kell Ford; her flaw is contempt, and a loyalty she has not tested. Sister Ione, pilgrim of Viator:
  // wants the last stone; her flaw is a faith that will not use a rope. Each has three beats across the road, each
  // beat is a question put to the player, and each ends differently by the answers: they may stay, go home, quit,
  // change the valley or die.
  Measure marraTrust(group). Measure fennTrust(group). Measure dunyaTrust(group). Measure ioneTrust(group).
  Relation marraStaysCohort(group). Relation marraHome(group). Relation marraAngry(group). Relation marraBoth(group).
  Relation fennCredit(group). Relation fennPushed(group). Relation fennTook(group). Relation fennGone(group). Relation fennNerve(group). Relation fennCrossed(group).
  Relation dunyaReported(group). Relation dunyaSilent(group). Relation dunyaDefected(group). Relation dunyaStaysKell(group). Relation dunyaHome(group).
  Relation ioneSafe(group). Relation ioneUnroped(group). Relation ioneFell(group). Relation ioneKeeper(group). Relation ioneHome(group).

  // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // Marra Tellis. Beat 1: Septimus (ask). Beat 2: the Eagle (a mark). Beat 3: the Cohort (the apothecary's boy).
  Topic marra_jory with marra "Ask Marra what she is looking for on the far side" when travelsWith(marra, party) and been(party, septimus_hall) once {
    Says "She says it to the trough, not to you. 'My brother Jory. He went over nine years since, at seventeen, on a dare from a Ferrar, to see if the Eagle was a place. His name is on the reeve's board, at the bottom.' Her hands go on with the crook. 'Would you want to know, if it were yours? That he was dead, I mean. If someone found a bone.'".
  }
  Topic marra_jory_yes with marra "Say that you would want to know, whatever it was" answering marra_jory when true {
    Says "'Right,' she says. 'Then you'll tell me straight, if you find a bone.'".
    On discuss { Increase marraTrust(marra) by 1. }
  }
  Topic marra_jory_no with marra "Say that you would not, if you could do nothing about it" answering marra_jory when true {
    Says "'No,' she says. 'No. Nor me.' She turns away. 'I'd not like to be told by a stranger anyway.' It was honest, and she is sore about it.".
    On discuss { Decrease marraTrust(marra) by 1 or to nothing. }
  }
  Topic marra_jory_cheese with marra "Say nothing, and offer her some of your bread" answering marra_jory when true {
    Says "She takes the bread, looks at it, and eats it. 'You can shut up,' she says, not unkindly, 'which is more than most of Kell can.'".
    On discuss { Increase marraTrust(marra) by 1. }
  }
  Observe marra_mark at eagle as sign when travelsWith(marra, party) "Low on the gate-arch where a boy would sit to rest, among the soldiers' scratches, three letters in a clumsy new hand: J.T. A DARE. Marra Tellis has stopped in the arch and is looking at them with her hand flat on the stone.".
  Topic marra_mark with marra "Ask Marra about the letters on the arch" when travelsWith(marra, party) and been(party, eagle) once {
    Says "'That's his,' she says. 'He cut his J like that. He never could do a T.' Her voice is level. 'He was here. He got as far as here.' She keeps her eyes on the arch. 'What do I do now? Do I go on, or do I go home? You tell me. I don't know whose road this is.'".
  }
  Topic marra_on with marra "Tell her to come on: it is her brother's road too" answering marra_mark when true {
    Says "'It is,' she says. She shoulders the crook. 'I'll not stay behind a rock for a giant. Not now.'".
    On discuss { Increase marraTrust(marra) by 2. }
  }
  Topic marra_home with marra "Tell her to go home: it is too far, and she has the goats" answering marra_mark when true {
    Says "'The goats,' she says. She puts a hand on the stone, and takes it off. She walks back along the road with a goat on a lead and does not look round.".
    On discuss { Assert marraHome(party). Assert marraAngry(party). Retract travelsWith(marra, party). Assert at(marra, eagle). }
  }
  Person jory "Jory Tellis" at apothecary {
    Role "Dame Orsa's boy, who sorts the herbs". Ancestry human. Pronouns he. Manner cheerful. Peaceable.
    Greets "A lanky young man of twenty-six with a pestle in one fist and an apron over a patched jerkin looks at you without any recognition at all, and says, in an accent that has been sanded flat by the Aenean of the valley: 'Salve. Can I help? We have a very little of everything.'".
  }
  Topic marra_found with marra "Show Marra the boy who sorts Dame Orsa's herbs" when travelsWith(marra, party) and been(party, apothecary) once {
    Says "She does not go to him. She stands in the doorway of the herb-shop with her crook in her fist and says the name of a Ferrar goat. He turns. The pestle goes down on the counter. 'Marra?' he says. She crosses the room in two strides and hits him, hard, on the arm. 'Nine years,' she says. 'Nine years, you daft heap.' After a while he says: 'Do I come home?' She says: 'You tell me.' And then, to you, over his shoulder, quietly: 'Do I stay?'".
  }
  Topic marra_stay with marra "Say that she should stay: he has a life here, and so would she" answering marra_found when true {
    Says "'Right,' says Marra, and wipes her face with her sleeve. 'Someone's to tell Mother, and it might as well be me.' She takes the crook off her shoulder and hangs it on the apothecary's peg. 'Tell my mother. Tell her the water's his as much as hers.'".
    On discuss { Assert marraStaysCohort(party). Retract travelsWith(marra, party). Assert at(marra, apothecary). }
  }
  Topic marra_both with marra "Say that they should both go home, and tell Kell Ford what is here" answering marra_found when true {
    Says "'Both,' she says. She turns to Jory. 'He'd be a bad Cohort man.' He laughs, and it is his father's laugh. 'We'll come with you to the Viaduct,' says Marra. 'And then I'll take him home by the rope-way, and show Mother the boy.'".
    On discuss { Increase marraTrust(marra) by 2. Assert marraBoth(party). }
  }
  Topic marra_pride with marra "Say it is their business and none of yours" answering marra_found when true {
    Says "'It's not,' says Marra. 'Not anyone's.' She puts the crook back on her shoulder. 'I thought you were a friend.' She walks out of the apothecary and does not come back, and Jory looks after her, and then at you, with an expression that you will remember.".
    On discuss { Assert marraAngry(party). Retract travelsWith(marra, party). Assert at(marra, forum). }
  }

  // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // Fenn Thorley. Beat 1: the north head of the Viaduct (nerve). Beat 2: the Eagle (the note). Beat 3: the Archive (credit).
  Initially Assert willing(fenn_hallam, party).
  Initially Set hire(fenn_hallam) to 6.
  Topic fenn_nerve with fenn_hallam "Ask Fenn why he has stopped at the Viaduct's head" when travelsWith(fenn_hallam, party) and at(party, via_n) once {
    Says "He is looking at the gap with the careful interest of a man looking at his own grave. 'I have drawn this from the north bank eleven times,' says Fenn Thorley. 'I have never gone on it. I did not tell you.' He swallows. 'I can do the arithmetic of a rope. I cannot make my feet do the arithmetic.' He glances at you. 'Will you think less of me, if I stay?'".
  }
  Topic fenn_easy with fenn_hallam "Tell him no: half the road is turning back, and he is the one who drew it" answering fenn_nerve when true {
    Says "'Half the road is turning back,' says Fenn, trying it. 'And I drew it.' He gets some colour back. 'Measuring a thing doesn't oblige you to be killed by it.' He unbuckles the satchel. 'I'll go last. Take this; I want both hands.'".
    On discuss { Increase fennTrust(fenn_hallam) by 1. Assert fennCrossed(party). }
  }
  Topic fenn_push with fenn_hallam "Tell him that he will cross, or stay in Kell Ford drawing it for the rest of his life" answering fenn_nerve when true {
    Says "He goes white, then grey, then goes to the parapet. 'You're right,' he says, thin. 'You're right and I hate you.' He crosses with his eyes shut, hand over hand, and is sick on the far side, and does not speak to you for an hour.".
    On discuss { Assert fennCrossed(party). Assert fennPushed(party). Decrease fennTrust(fenn_hallam) by 1 or to nothing. }
  }

  Topic fenn_stay with fenn_hallam "Tell him to wait at the north head; you will send word by the rope-way" answering fenn_nerve when true {
    Says "'Thank you,' says Fenn, and means it more than he wants to. He sits down on the paving with his back to the gap and takes out his pencil. His drawing of the north pier, when you see it again, is the best thing he has done.".
    On discuss { Retract travelsWith(fenn_hallam, party). Assert at(fenn_hallam, via_n). Assert fennNerve(party). }
  }
  Rule fenn_comes_over when fennNerve(party) and at(party, via_s) and at(fenn_hallam, via_n) and not fennCrossed(party) and (knows(party, ropeway_known) or lineFixed(party))
    says "A small figure on the north head has begun to come across the fifth arch on the Cohort's rope-way, hand over hand, with a satchel on his back and his eyes shut. It takes him most of an hour." {
    Assert at(fenn_hallam, via_s). Assert fennCrossed(party). Assert travelsWith(fenn_hallam, party). }
  Topic fenn_note with fenn_hallam "Ask Fenn what he has found in the Eagle's wall" when travelsWith(fenn_hallam, party) and been(party, eagle) once {
    Says "He has found, in a niche behind the gate-arch, the engineers' note: a copper plate, eleven lines, in the Itinerarium's hand, giving the span and the method for a rope-way across the fifth arch. He reads it aloud, and stops, and reads it again. 'I could put my name to this,' he says, in a small voice. 'No one has seen it for eleven hundred years. It's a better idea than any I've had. It would be called Thorley's Crossing.' He looks up. 'What would you do?'".
  }
  Topic fenn_credit with fenn_hallam "Say it should be Thessaly Orde's, or the engineer's, whoever's it was: write down who" answering fenn_note when true {
    Says "'The engineers are dead,' says Fenn, 'and Orde, I think, is a name on a stud.' He pauses. 'It would be more honest.' He takes out his pencil and writes, under the note, in a careful hand: FROM THE ENGINEERS OF THE LONG MUSTER. COPIED BY F. HALLAM. His mouth twists. 'It is a worse title.'".
    On discuss { Assert fennCredit(party). Increase fennTrust(fenn_hallam) by 2. }
  }
  Topic fenn_take with fenn_hallam "Say that he found it, he earned it, and no one will ever know" answering fenn_note when true {
    Says "'No one will ever know,' says Fenn, and his face lights, and then dims. 'That is rather the trouble, isn't it.' He copies the note into his own book, in his own hand, with the plate's number crossed out. 'Thorley's Crossing,' he says, to himself. It does not sound as good as it did.".
    On discuss { Assert fennTook(party). }
  }
  Topic fenn_survey with fenn_hallam "Tell Fenn the Archivist will print the survey, and ask whose name goes on it" when travelsWith(fenn_hallam, party) and been(party, archive) and (fennCredit(party) or fennTook(party)) and not fennPushed(party) once {
    Says "Ostrander, who has been listening, puts down his pen. 'My press prints the Annals of a people who do not know they are in a story,' he says. 'I can print Thorley. I can print the Engineers. I cannot print a lie, because the Annals would remember it for eleven hundred years. Choose.' Fenn looks at the schola's pupils, who are tracing his map on the wall with chalk, and at Livia Veldt, who has drawn the Viaduct's fifth arch better than he did.".
  }
  Topic fenn_honest with fenn_hallam "Tell him to put Livia Veldt's name beside his: she drew half of it" answering fenn_survey when true {
    Says "'Livia drew the arches,' says Fenn. His mouth works. 'I drew the road. Thorley and Veldt, then. Thorley first, because it is alphabetical, and I am weak.' Livia, who has understood nothing and everything, goes the colour of a poppy. 'The survey of the Long Muster's Road,' says Fenn, 'by two.'".
    On discuss { Assert fennCredit(party). Increase fennTrust(fenn_hallam) by 1. }
  }
  Topic fenn_alone with fenn_hallam "Tell him it is his survey, and the Archive may do as it likes with the credit" answering fenn_survey when true {
    Says "'Mine,' says Fenn, and sits up. He takes the sheets and rolls them. Ostrander watches him do it. In the morning the surveyor's bed is made, and the satchel and the Archive's copy of the Itinerarium are gone down the Descent.".
    On discuss { Assert fennGone(party). Retract travelsWith(fenn_hallam, party). Assert at(fenn_hallam, descent). }
  }
  Observe fenn_credit_wall at schola when fennCredit(party) "On the wall of the schola, over the chalked map, a board: THE ROAD OF THE LONG MUSTER, SURVEYED BY F. HALLAM AND L. VELDT, FROM THE ENGINEERS' NOTES. Fenn Thorley has not been able to look at it for more than a moment at a time.".

  // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // Dunya, scout of the First Century. Beat 1: Kell Ford. Beat 2: the head of the Descent, on the way back. Beat 3: Kell again.
  Topic dunya_kell with dunya "Ask Dunya what she sees in Kell Ford" when travelsWith(dunya, party) and at(party, green) and been(party, pass_s) once {
    Says "She stands at the trough with her hands behind her back. 'It's small,' she says. 'Thirty houses, a trough, a board, a boy with a dog. I was told there'd be a city.' She takes it in. 'People were here all the time. Two days past the Eagle, with a trough.' She turns. 'Is it enough? Is it a place?'".
  }
  Topic dunya_enough with dunya "Say that it is enough: it is where people are" answering dunya_kell when true {
    Says "'Yes,' says Dunya. She touches the trough, flat-handed, as she would the flank of a horse. 'People are. I had them down as a list.' Her stiffness goes out of her by a degree, and Greta Hennick, in the inn door, offers her a bowl of something hot.".
    On discuss { Increase dunyaTrust(dunya) by 2. }
  }
  Topic dunya_small with dunya "Say that it is small, and the Cohort is the greater place" answering dunya_kell when true {
    Says "'Yes,' says Dunya, stiff again. 'It is.' She keeps her back to the houses. 'I have always said the Cohort was the greater place. It is good to have a witness.' She drinks Greta's soup and does not taste it.".
    On discuss { Decrease dunyaTrust(dunya) by 1 or to nothing. }
  }
  Topic dunya_aurel with dunya "Ask Dunya who the men at the head of the Descent are" when travelsWith(dunya, party) and at(party, pass_s) once {
    Says "She has not taken her eyes off Aurel since the turn of the road. 'The tall one's Aurel Merula,' she says. 'He was my century-mate. We drew the lot together. Mine was the draw before his.' She breathes. 'He took the pouch that spring and he did not come back, and the Centurion said he was dead at the Eagle, and she wept for a month. He's not dead.' She glances over. 'Do I report him? I am a scout of the First Century. It is my duty.'".
  }
  Topic dunya_report with dunya "Tell her that duty is duty, and that his mother is owed the truth" answering dunya_aurel when true {
    Says "'She is,' says Dunya. She goes to Aurel, and they talk for a quarter of an hour on the boulder, with their heads close. When she returns her face is wet. 'I'll tell the Centurion. Not that he's a deserter. That he's alive.'".
    On discuss { Assert dunyaReported(party). Increase dunyaTrust(dunya) by 1. }
  }
  Topic dunya_silent with dunya "Tell her to say nothing: they have made their choice, and it is theirs" answering dunya_aurel when true {
    Says "'Nothing,' she says. She does not sound sure. 'I'll say nothing. I'll carry it, like the Sending.' She follows you down the Descent with her eyes on her boots.".
    On discuss { Assert dunyaSilent(party). }
  }
  Topic dunya_join with dunya "Tell her that she could stay with them, and no one would know" answering dunya_aurel when true {
    Says "Dunya looks at the boulder and the seven men. 'I could,' she says. She puts down her pack. 'Tell the Centurion I took the Pass for my century. Tell her I am where I am needed.' She goes up the slope, and Aurel gets down off his boulder, and they clasp wrists, and she does not look back.".
    On discuss { Assert dunyaDefected(party). Retract travelsWith(dunya, party). Assert at(dunya, pass_s). }
  }
  Topic dunya_stay with dunya "Ask Dunya whether she means to go back to the Cohort, now she has seen the Ford" when travelsWith(dunya, party) and at(party, taproom) and been(party, green) and dunyaTrust(dunya) >= 2 once {
    Says "She turns her bowl a quarter-turn. 'I was sent to see. I have seen. I should report.' She watches the fire. 'Or I could be a scout between the two ends. A road wants one at each.'".
  }
  Topic dunya_stay_here with dunya "Tell her to stay a season and carry word between them" answering dunya_stay when true {
    Says "'A season,' says Dunya. She almost smiles. 'Greta will want the table.' She takes her pack off.".
    On discuss { Assert dunyaStaysKell(party). Retract travelsWith(dunya, party). Assert at(dunya, taproom). }
  }
  Measure dunyaAt(group).
  Rule dunya_arrives when travelsWith(dunya, party) and at(party, green) and dunyaAt(party) == 0 { Set dunyaAt(party) to watch(clock). }
  Rule dunya_leaves_unasked when travelsWith(dunya, party) and dunyaAt(party) > 0 and at(party, green) and watch(clock) >= dunyaAt(party) + 36 and dunyaTrust(dunya) < 2
    says "Dunya waits out the better part of a morning at the trough, with her pack at her feet, and then shoulders it. 'I was sent to see,' she says. 'I have seen.' She goes north up the Causeway, and does not wave." {
    Assert dunyaHome(party). Retract travelsWith(dunya, party). Assert at(dunya, causeway3). }
  Rule dunya_stays_unasked when travelsWith(dunya, party) and dunyaAt(party) > 0 and at(party, green) and watch(clock) >= dunyaAt(party) + 36 and dunyaTrust(dunya) >= 2
    says "Dunya has been reading the notice-board for an hour. 'A season,' she says to nobody in particular, and carries her pack in at the Last Fire's door." {
    Assert dunyaStaysKell(party). Retract travelsWith(dunya, party). Assert at(dunya, taproom). }
  Topic dunya_go_home with dunya "Tell her to go back and report what she has seen" answering dunya_stay when true {
    Says "'Yes,' says Dunya. 'The Centurion.' She stands and puts on her cloak. By morning there is a wheel scratched on the doorpost of the Last Fire, and a cleared plate.".
    On discuss { Assert dunyaHome(party). Retract travelsWith(dunya, party). Assert at(dunya, causeway3). }
  }

  // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // Sister Ione, pilgrim of Viator. Beat 1: the Gallery (belief). Beat 2: the Viaduct (the rope). Beat 3: the last stone.
  Person ione "Sister Ione" at gallery1 {
    Role "a pilgrim of Viator, the Walker-by". Ancestry human. Calling cleric. Level 2. Pronouns she. Manner compassionate. Peaceable.
    Joins "Sister Ione takes up a staff with a lead bell on the end and falls in at your heel. 'I shall not be a burden,' she says. 'I shall be a conscience.'".
    Greets "A woman in a grey travelling habit is kneeling at the shrine in the wall, lighting a wick with a stub of candle, saying words under her breath. She finishes before she turns. 'I have walked from the sea,' she says. 'It took me three years. I have walked nothing but the roads that Viator made, and I have not crossed a bridge I did not have to, and I have not eaten a meal I did not earn. I would be glad of company on the road to the Last Stone.'".
  }
  Initially Assert willing(ione, party).
  Initially Set hire(ione) to 0.
  Topic ione_faith with ione "Ask Sister Ione what she believes" when true once {
    Says "'That a road is a promise,' says Ione. 'That the one who made it meant it to be walked. That the walking is the prayer and the arriving the answer.' She smiles. 'And you? I am asked it at every fire. It is a courtesy. Do you believe in him?'".
  }
  Topic ione_yes with ione "Say that you do, a little, the way you believe in weather" answering ione_faith when true {
    Says "'That is the right amount,' says Ione, much pleased. 'Weather is humbling.' She falls in at your heel.".
    On discuss { Increase ioneTrust(ione) by 1. Assert travelsWith(ione, party). }
  }
  Topic ione_no with ione "Say that you do not: you believe in rope, and in a good boot" answering ione_faith when true {
    Says "'Boots are the first article of the faith,' says Ione, unoffended. 'You only fail to give thanks.' Her gaze goes to your pack. 'I shall pray for the rope.'".
    On discuss { Assert travelsWith(ione, party). }
  }
  Topic ione_road with ione "Say that you believe in the road, whoever made it" answering ione_faith when true {
    Says "'That is the heresy,' says Ione, 'and the heart of it.' She puts a hand on your sleeve. 'Come.'".
    On discuss { Increase ioneTrust(ione) by 2. Assert travelsWith(ione, party). }
  }
  Topic ione_rope with ione "Ask Sister Ione whether she will take the line over the parapet" when travelsWith(ione, party) and at(party, via_n) once {
    Says "Her gaze goes over the pitons, and the rope, and the gap. 'A rope is a hand that is not a god's,' she says. 'Viator carries those who walk the stones he made. I shall cross unroped.' She is perfectly serene, and perfectly immovable. 'You will think me a fool. I should like you not to stop me.'".
  }
  Topic ione_agree with ione "Say that you will not stop her, and that you will go behind" answering ione_rope when true {
    Says "'Thank you,' says Ione. She goes out on the parapet with her staff in front of her and the lead bell ringing, one step, one ring. Behind her the wind comes up the gap.".
    On discuss { Assert ioneUnroped(party). Increase ioneTrust(ione) by 2. }
  }
  Topic ione_argue with ione "Argue that a god who made stone also made the hands that drive pitons" answering ione_rope when true {
    Says "She hears it out. 'That is theology,' she says at last, 'and it is good.' She takes the line and clips it to her habit with an air of grave ceremony. 'I shall tell him that I was persuaded.' She does not ring the bell again between here and the Cohort, and you notice that you are listening for it.".
    On discuss { Assert ioneSafe(party). Decrease ioneTrust(ione) by 1 or to nothing. }
  }
  Topic ione_order with ione "Tell her she will wear the line, or she will turn back" answering ione_rope when true {
    Says "'You will not command me,' says Ione. 'But I will go back.' She picks up her staff and goes, without anger, along the road you came by, her bell ringing smaller and smaller. 'I am sorry,' she says, over her shoulder. 'I am sorry to be so very sure.'".
    On discuss { Retract travelsWith(ione, party). Assert at(ione, octavius_hall). Assert ioneHome(party). }
  }
  Relation ioneRolled(group).
  Rule ione_falls_rule when ioneUnroped(party) and at(party, parapet) and travelsWith(ione, party) and not ioneRolled(party) and dice(clock) <= 40
    says "The wind comes up the gap in one shove, from the open side, and the bell goes. It rings once more, a long way down, and then it does not." {
    Assert ioneRolled(party). Assert ioneFell(party). Slay ione. }
  Rule ione_steady_rule when ioneUnroped(party) and at(party, parapet) and travelsWith(ione, party) and not ioneRolled(party) and dice(clock) > 40
    says "The wind comes up the gap and finds her, and she stops, and the bell rings fast, and she goes on." {
    Assert ioneRolled(party). }
  Rule ione_crosses when ioneRolled(party) and not ioneFell(party) and at(party, via_s) and not ioneSafe(party) { Assert ioneSafe(party). }
  Topic ione_stone with ione "Ask Sister Ione what she will do now she has seen the last stone" when travelsWith(ione, party) and at(party, forum) once {
    Says "She is kneeling at the foot of the stone that says I, with her cheek against it. 'It is smooth,' she says. 'Nothing after.' She sits back on her heels. 'I have walked from the sea for three years to kiss the last stone of a road that says there is no more. And there is a valley, and two thousand people, and a god's name nobody remembers.' She turns toward you. 'What would he have me do?'".
  }
  Topic ione_keep with ione "Say that the valley wants a shrine, and a keeper who knows the old words" answering ione_stone when true {
    Says "'A keeper,' says Ione, as one hears a name. She lays her staff across her knees and takes the lead bell off it, and hangs it on the stone. 'Then I shall be it. Someone ought to say the words for those who come in.' It rings, once, in the draught. It is the first bell in the valley that is not a horn.".
    On discuss { Assert ioneKeeper(party). Retract travelsWith(ione, party). Assert at(ione, forum). }
  }
  Topic ione_go with ione "Say that the road has two ends, and the other one needs her" answering ione_stone when true {
    Says "'Both ends,' says Ione. 'Yes.' She rises, and knocks the dust from her habit. 'I shall go back, then, and tell Kell Ford and the sea that it was a promise kept. And I shall bring back a cutting of the vine, to plant by the shrine at the Gallery.' She puts her palm on the stone one last time. 'II,' she says. 'Someone will cut it.'".
    On discuss { Assert ioneHome(party). Increase ioneTrust(ione) by 1. }
  }
  Observe ione_shrine near ione when ioneKeeper(party) "Sister Ione has set up a niche in the forum wall with a stub of candle and a dish of boot-nails, and the first child has already left one.".
}

data/adventures/muster/company.kyr:
Module muster "The Long Muster's Road" {
  Company party "Your company" of 3 at road.
  // The company's own coin; the engine adds the backgrounds' coin, so the opening purse is 22 (test/muster-played.test.ts pins it).
  Initially Set quantity(party, gold) to 14. Initially Set quantity(party, rations) to 3.
}

data/adventures/muster/folk.kyr:
Module muster "The Long Muster's Road" {
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // What the roads are, as Kell Ford knows them.
  Discovery wyvern_known "A wyvern stoops on the open stretch of the High Road at noon, unless the wind keeps it down. The Gallery and the Ledge are sheltered from it.".
  Discovery gallery_known "The Gallery's Third Arch is rotten; prod the roadway before crossing it. The air is foul and the goatherd will not enter.".
  Discovery causeway_known "The Old Causeway is dry but twice as long, and wolves hunt it in the dark.".
  Discovery flood_known "In a thaw the Low Road floods at its bend and the river under the Viaduct cannot be forded.".
  Discovery giant_known "A giant keeps the road-house of the Pass. He asks a toll of iron or a story, and sleeps at night. The goat path goes over his roof.".
  Discovery ledge_known "The Ledge is out of the wyvern's sight, but closed in snow, and only the goat-wise find the way on to it.".
  Discovery cohort_known "Eleven hundred years ago the Long Muster marched out by this road, and a rearguard went the other way. Some say the lamps at the far end are still lit.".

  Person marra "Marra Tellis" at green {
    Role "a goatherd". Ancestry human. Calling ranger. Level 2. Pronouns she. Manner stoic. Peaceable.
    Carries marra_crook.
    Joins "Marra Tellis shoulders her crook and a bag of cheese. 'I go as far as the places I go,' she says. 'Don't ask me past them.'".
    Greets "A woman with a hooked stick and three goats, sitting on the trough as if it were hers. 'You want to go over,' she says. 'Everyone who looks at the board does. I can show you the track. I won't go in the Gallery and I won't go where the giant can see me. The rest is fair.'".
  }
  Kind marra_crook "a goatherd's crook" is gear granting wayfinding described "A hooked ash stick polished by forty years of use. Whoever holds it, the hill is as plain as a street.".
  Initially Assert willing(marra, party).
  Initially Set hire(marra) to 4.
  Topic marra_roads with marra "Ask Marra about the roads" when true {
    Says "'Three roads to Septimus,' says Marra. 'The gorge is quickest and drowns in a thaw. The causeway is dry and long and the wolves have it at night. My track is steepest and no one else will take it.' She spits. 'Past Septimus the High Road is quick and the wyvern has it at noon; the Gallery is dark and the arch is rotten; the Ledge is mine, and closed in snow.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, causeway_known). Assert knows(actor, flood_known). Assert knows(actor, wyvern_known). Assert knows(actor, ledge_known). }
  }
  Topic marra_gallery with marra "Ask Marra why she will not enter the Gallery" when true {
    Says "'The air,' she says. 'And the arch. A goat knows. You prod the road with a pole before you trust it, and you hold your breath and you do not light a candle you cannot afford to see go blue.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, gallery_known). }
  }
  Topic marra_giant with marra "Ask Marra about the giant" when true {
    Says "'In the Pass, in the road-house, he keeps it for the king,' says Marra. 'Which king he doesn't know. He asks iron or a story. He sleeps at night and his hearth keeps him warm. I don't go where he can see me, because he asked my mother for a story once, and she had none.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, giant_known). }
  }
  // She goes no further than she said.
  Rule marra_stays_gallery at gallery1 when travelsWith(marra, party) and at(party, gallery1)
    says "Marra Tellis stops at the mouth of the Gallery with her goats' lead in her fist. 'This is as far as I said,' she says. 'I'll wait.'" {
    Retract travelsWith(marra, party). Assert at(marra, gallery1). Assert willing(marra, party). Set hire(marra) to 0.
  }
  Rule marra_stays_giant at pass_n when travelsWith(marra, party) and at(party, pass_n) and marraTrust(marra) < 2
    says "Marra Tellis sits down behind a rock below the notch. 'He'll see me from the door,' she says. 'Go on. I'll be here.'" {
    Retract travelsWith(marra, party). Assert at(marra, pass_n). Assert willing(marra, party). Set hire(marra) to 0.
  }

  Person greta "Greta Hennick" at taproom {
    Role "keeper of the Last Fire". Ancestry human. Profession innkeeper. Pronouns she. Manner acerbic. Peaceable.
    Greets "Greta Hennick looks at your boots, and your packs, and the number of you. 'You've the look,' she says. 'Soft. Sit down and eat before you start, you'll not get a hot meal for three days.'".
  }
  Topic maud_road with greta "Ask Greta about the road" when true {
    Says "'It's the legion's road,' says Greta. 'Eleven thousand went over it and ten thousand came down the other side, if you believe the beams. It's two days' walk to the Eagle and then it's the Pass and then it's nobody. We send nobody. Every spring somebody's grandmother puts a lamp in the window, and I put one in the inn.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, cohort_known). }
  }

  Person fenn_hallam "Fenn Thorley" at taproom {
    Role "a surveyor, resting a bad knee". Ancestry human. Pronouns he. Manner jovial. Peaceable.
    Buys antique_compass for 120 coin.
    Buys ancient_map for 40 coin.
    Buys faded_map for 30 coin.
    Buys celestial_chart for 150 coin.
    Greets "A man with a roll of oiled paper and a leg up on a stool, who has been to the Viaduct and no further. 'It's the finest road in the world, and it's falling down,' he says. 'Sit.'".
  }
  Topic dray_notes with fenn_hallam "Ask the surveyor for his notes on the Viaduct" when true once {
    Says "Thorley unrolls a sketch: nine arches, one with a cross through it. 'The fifth,' he says. 'Three ways over, none of them kind. The parapet, if you can pin a line to it. The river, if it's low, and a climb. Or the rubble, if you like your fingers broken. The wind's worse on the parapet. The river's worse in a thaw. There's no soft one.'".
    On discuss { Give surveyor_notes to actor. }
  }
  Kind surveyor_notes "Thorley's notes on the Viaduct" is document teaching viaduct_notes described "A roll of oiled paper, in a surveyor's fine hand, showing the Viaduct from three sides.".
  Discovery viaduct_notes "The fifth arch of the Viaduct can be crossed by the parapet (pitons and a rope), by the river and the south abutment (not in a thaw), or by the fallen arch (a climb). The wind is worst on the parapet.".

  Person cattermole "Reeve Cattermole" at green {
    Role "the reeve of Kell Ford". Ancestry human. Pronouns he. Manner grim. Peaceable.
    Greets "The reeve stands at the notice-board with a hammer, nailing up a notice nobody will read. 'If you don't come back,' he says, 'I'm to know where to write. Where would that be?'".
  }

  Person pryor "Gil Pryor" at counter {
    Role "storekeeper". Ancestry human. Pronouns he. Manner stoic. Peaceable.
    Sells rope for 1 coin from 8.
    Sells piton for 1 coin from 10.
    Sells firewood for 1 coin from 10.
    Sells tent for 6 coin from 2.
    Sells blanket for 2 coin from 6.
    Sells rations for 2 coin from 15.
    Sells climbing_harness for 6 coin from 2.
    Sells signal_whistle for 2 coin from 3.
    Sells torch for 1 coin from 8.
    Sells crowbar for 3 coin from 3.
    Sells shovel for 3 coin from 3.
    Sells iron_spikes for 2 coin from 4.
    Sells waterskin for 2 coin from 4.
    Greets "Gil Pryor sells people equipment for a journey he has never seen anyone finish. 'Rope,' he says. 'Pitons, in case of the Viaduct. Iron, in case of the giant. Firewood, in case of nights. You'll want a tent. You'll want everything.'".
  }
  Topic pryor_what with pryor "Ask Gil Pryor what the road needs" when true {
    Says "'Rope for the shaft and the river. Pitons for the parapet. Iron for the giant: a bar, or spikes, he's not particular. Firewood and a tent, because it is cold above the snow-line and nobody has ever lit a fire on the High Road with what they found there. Rations for three days. And a way to be home by the thaw.'".
  }

  // ── The village that sees the road and does not go ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Person lorne "Eddard Lorne" at taproom {
    Role "the oldest man in Kell Ford". Ancestry human. Pronouns he. Manner grim. Peaceable.
    Greets "A very old man by the hearth does not take his eyes from the coals. 'Sit,' he says. 'I have been waiting for somebody to ask me about the red cloak.'".
  }
  Topic lorne_cloak with lorne "Ask the old man about the red cloak" when true once {
    Says "'Nine years since, in the spring,' says Eddard Lorne. 'At the far bank of the river, at dusk, a man in a red cloak among the willows. He stood there from the light going to the dark. I was out looking for a ewe. I did not call. I do not know why I did not call.' He drinks. 'He had a bow, and he did not come across. Next day, nothing. I have told no one. There is a legion's road at the end of this village and I have lived my life with my back to it. I think it would be a poor thing if a man stood on the far bank nine years ago and found nobody would speak.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, red_cloak_known). }
  }
  Entity red_cloak_known: fact "Nine springs ago a man in a red cloak, with a bow, stood on the far bank of the river below Kell Ford from dusk to dark, and did not cross.".
  Observe board_scene at green when true "The notice-board at the trough carries a column of names in the reeve's hand, each with a year: the people of Kell Ford who went over the Fells and did not come back, forty-one of them. At the head of the column, in a hand a hundred years older: THE ROAD IS THE LEGION'S. IT LEADS SOMEWHERE. Nobody has ever amended it.".
  Entity lamp_known: fact "Kell Ford puts a lamp in the window facing the north road every spring, and nobody knows for whom.".
  Topic maud_sending with greta "Ask Greta why the village keeps a lamp in the window" when true {
    Says "'Every spring,' says Greta. 'A hundred and some years, since my grandmother's grandmother. The first week of spring, a lamp in the window facing the road. Nobody knows why. My mother said it was for the ones who went, and my grandmother said it was for something that might come. She was cross about it.' She trims the wick. 'I put it in because I always have.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, lamp_known). }
  }
}

data/adventures/muster/folk2.kyr:
Module muster "The Long Muster's Road" {
  // ── People who want something of the company, and push back ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Relation yeastGiven(group). Relation yeastTaken(group). Relation edeVouched(group). Relation edePushed(group).

  // Octa Draycott: the bread. She sells you rations at a coin; she will not give you the yeast unless you will feed it.
  Kind yeast_pot "a pot of the Muster's yeast" is gear worth 0 weighing 1 described "A glazed pot with a cloth tied over it, warm to the hand. Twice a day it wants flour and water, and a place by a fire.".
  Item yeast_item "a pot of the Muster's yeast" is yeast_pot at nowhere.
  Topic octa_pot with octa "Ask the baker for a pot of the old yeast, to take south" when ateBread(party) and not yeastTaken(party) once {
    Says "Octa wipes her hands down her apron. 'It's been alive eleven hundred years,' she says. 'It wants flour and water twice a day and a fire to sit by. Not a saddle-bag. Not a cold night on a fell.' She folds her arms. 'Will you feed it, or will you let it die on the Viaduct?'".
  }
  Topic octa_pot_yes with octa "Say that you will feed it, twice a day, by a fire" answering octa_pot when true {
    Says "'Then you'll build the fire at noon and dusk,' says Octa, 'even if there's a wolf.' She ties the cloth and puts the pot in your hands. 'If it dies, I'll know.'".
    On discuss { Give yeast_item to actor. Assert yeastTaken(party). }
  }
  Topic octa_pot_no with octa "Say that you can't promise that, on that road" answering octa_pot when true {
    Says "'No,' says Octa. 'Well. Honest.' She turns back to her oven. 'There's bread, if you'll pay for it.'".
  }
  Topic maud_yeast with greta "Give Greta the pot of yeast from the valley" when holding(actor, yeast_pot) and not yeastGiven(party) once {
    Says "Greta lifts the cloth, sniffs, and lifts her eyebrows. 'It's alive,' she says. 'Mine's been dead since my grandmother.' She puts it by the hearth as if it were a baby.".
    On discuss { Spend yeast_pot from actor. Assert yeastGiven(party). }
  }
  Observe yeast_inn at taproom when yeastGiven(party) "The Last Fire's bread has begun to taste of cumin. Greta will not say why. A boy is sent out twice a day to the hearth with a spoon.".

  // Ede Cassel: the Archivist's young hand, who thinks the valley should be told and says so.
  Person ede_cassel "Ede Cassel" at archive {
    Role "the Archivist's assistant". Ancestry human. Pronouns she. Manner cheerful. Peaceable.
    Greets "A girl of twenty with ink to the elbow looks up from a copy she is making. 'You're the one from the north,' she says. 'Don't let Ostrander make you wait a year. He'll say it's kindness.'".
  }
  Topic ede_asks with ede_cassel "Ask Ede what she wants of you" when admitted(party) once {
    Says "Ede puts the pen down. 'There's a beam in this room with eleven hundred notches on it and nobody says a word,' she says. 'You've been over the Viaduct. Will you tell them? The Hearth, at least. Elpis will hear it from me if she hears it from someone who's seen the road.'".
  }
  Topic ede_yes with ede_cassel "Say that you will, if she will stand beside you when you do" answering ede_asks when true {
    Says "'Done,' says Ede Cassel. 'Elpis trusts the Archive's hand when it's mine. Not his.' She shuts the copy. 'Don't wait for spring.'".
    On discuss { Assert edeVouched(party). }
  }
  Topic ede_no with ede_cassel "Say that it is not yours to tell, and leave it to the Prefect" answering ede_asks when true {
    Says "'It's not anyone's,' says Ede Cassel. She picks the pen up. 'That's how it's lasted.' She doesn't look at you again.".
    On discuss { Assert edePushed(party). }
  }
  Observe ede_cold at archive when edePushed(party) "Ede Cassel copies without looking up.".

  // Tavi: the baker's boy, who follows if you have eaten his bread.
  Person tavi "Tavi" at pistrina {
    Role "the baker's boy". Ancestry human. Pronouns he. Manner cheerful. Peaceable.
    Greets "A boy of eleven with flour to the ears stops pushing a peel into the oven. 'You came over,' he says. 'From the north.' He wipes his nose. 'Is there trees?'".
  }
  Topic tavi_trees with tavi "Tell Tavi about the trees" when true {
    Says "'Real ones?' says Tavi. 'With birds? We've only the vines.' He looks at the oven, and at you. 'Do they have bread?'".
  }
  Rule tavi_follows at descent when at(party, descent) and ateBread(party) and not following(tavi, party) and not taviSent(party)
    says "A boy of eleven is sitting on a milestone on the Descent with a loaf under his arm and a sack. 'Octa doesn't know,' he says. 'I'll walk behind.'" {
    Assert following(tavi, party). }
  Observe tavi_kell at green when following(tavi, party) "The boy from the valley has stopped at the trough with his mouth open. He's looking at the thirty houses, and at the dogs, and at the line of the hills, and at a tree.".
  Relation taviSent(group).
  Topic tavi_home with tavi "Tell Tavi to go back to Octa before she misses him" when following(tavi, party) and not taviSent(party) once {
    Says "'She'll know by now,' says Tavi. 'She'll have counted the loaves.' He looks south at nothing in particular. 'All right.' He turns round and goes back up the Descent with the loaf.".
    On discuss { Retract following(tavi, party). Assert at(tavi, descent). Assert taviSent(party). }
  }
}

data/adventures/muster/gauntlet.kyr:
Module muster "The Long Muster's Road" {
  // ── What the road costs ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // A crossing, not a string of toll-booths. Pitons and rope are spent when they are used. Hunger bites at noon.
  // The season turns on the seventh day and the fifth arch closes in both directions. The weather can be read from
  // the heights. And a company that goes back loses what it carried up: the line, the days, the rope.
  Relation lineFixed(group). Relation hungerToday(group). Relation ateBread(group).
  Query lateSeason() means day(clock) >= 7.
  Hazard hunger_pangs "A day without food on the fell" means "Deal (1d6) physical damage to ally unless constitution save against (13).".
  Rule hunger_bites when hungry(party) and hour(clock) == 12 and not hungerToday(party) and not within(party, kell)
    { Assert strikes(hunger_pangs, party). Assert hungerToday(party). }
  Rule hunger_resets when hungerToday(party) and hour(clock) >= 14 { Retract hungerToday(party). }

  Action rig_parapet(actor: group, site: place) "Drive a piton into the parapet and rig a line over the gap with a coil of rope" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(actor, via_n) and holding(actor, piton) and holding(actor, rope) and not lineFixed(actor) and not lateSeason().
    Time 6.
    Resolve check(actor, climbing, dc_fair).
    On success { Spend piton from actor. Spend rope from actor. Assert lineFixed(actor). }
    Result "It takes three tries to find a crack that holds. The piton rings home, and the rope goes out over four hundred feet of air to the second, and the line sings in the wind like a plucked string. Nothing you carry will cross without it now, and nothing you used to carry is left to use anywhere else.".
    On failure { Spend piton from actor. }
    Otherwise "The crack takes the piton and spits it. It goes down into the gap without a sound, and it was one of yours.".
  }
  Observe late_snow_via at via_n when lateSeason() "Snow has come to the Viaduct in the night. The parapet is a white line, and the gap beyond it is white, and nothing crosses until the thaw.".
  Observe late_snow_octavius at octavius_hall when lateSeason() "The vault leaks snow-water down the wall in a line of icicles. In the lee of the rock a pack-mule's bones are already white to the knee.".

  // Weather you can read from the heights.
  Observe sky_high_snow at high1 when state(sky, snow_sky) "Snow blows across the High Road in long flat ribbons, and the peaks beyond the next shoulder have gone.".
  Observe sky_high_wind at high1 when state(sky, wind_sky) "The wind lays the heather flat and takes the breath out of your mouth. Nothing is in the air. Nothing could be.".
  Observe sky_high_thaw at high1 when state(sky, thaw_sky) "Every rock on the fell is running with water, and the whole slope talks.".
  Observe sky_ledge_snow at ledge1 when state(sky, snow_sky) "A line of clean white runs along the Ledge, unbroken, and a goat on the far buttress is watching it with the look of someone who has been told.".
  Observe sky_via_wind at via_n when state(sky, wind_sky) "The wind comes up the gap in gusts that move the rust-red birds on the parapet one block along.".
  Observe sky_via_thaw at via_n when state(sky, thaw_sky) "Far below, the river is the colour of tea and the noise of it comes up through the stone.".
  Observe sky_eagle_snow at eagle when state(sky, snow_sky) "Snow is drifting into the roofless fort in a long tongue from the gate-arch, over the courier-pouches.".
  Observe sky_pass_snow at pass_n when state(sky, snow_sky) "The notch is full of driven snow. The marmots are gone to ground and the giant's smoke goes up straight, which means there is nothing in the sky to bend it.".

  // The oxen stop at noon and the marmots empty the slope when the giant wakes: both are readable.
  Relation giantAwakeSeen(group).
  Rule marmots_see when at(party, pass_n) and not dark() and not giantAwakeSeen(party)
    says "The marmots whistle once, and again, and are gone: a slope of dived holes and a few shaken stones." { Assert giantAwakeSeen(party). }
  Rule marmots_calm when dark() and giantAwakeSeen(party) { Retract giantAwakeSeen(party). }
  Query giantAwake() means not dark().
  Relation gateNight(group).
  Rule gate_night when gateRebuffed(party) and not gateNight(party) and (hour(clock) >= 22 or hour(clock) < 4) { Assert gateNight(party). }
  Rule gate_cools when gateRebuffed(party) and gateNight(party) and hour(clock) >= 6 and hour(clock) < 12 { Retract gateRebuffed(party). Retract gateNight(party). }
  Rule yeast_dies when holding(party, yeast_pot) and chilled(party) { Spend yeast_pot from party. }
  Observe yeast_dead_note at pistrina when yeastTaken(party) and not holding(party, yeast_pot) "Octa Draycott looks at the empty place on her shelf and stands there for a quarter of a minute. 'Cold night,' she says. She has a spare pot. She does not offer it.".
  // The valley says the passes are closing, on the fifth to the sixth day.
  Observe closing_gate at gate when day(clock) >= 5 and not lateSeason() "The captain on the wall is looking north with his hand over his eyes. 'Early,' he says, to the sky. 'The passes will be shut within the week.'".
  Observe closing_forum at forum when day(clock) >= 5 and not lateSeason() "People in the forum keep stopping to look at the north wall of the valley. The grain-carts are going out the wrong way, back to the granary, with the last of the harvest.".
  Observe closing_eagle at eagle when day(clock) >= 5 and not lateSeason() "Fresh snow lies on the far peaks, down to the first terrace. The wind off the Viaduct smells of it.".
  Observe closing_octavius at octavius_hall when day(clock) >= 5 and not lateSeason() "A thin line of white has come down the north face of the fell overnight. The scribe's bench is damp with it.".
}

data/adventures/muster/graffiti.kyr:
Module muster "The Long Muster's Road" {
  // ── Rufus, and the stones that count down ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // Eleven thousand men marched out of the south-east by this road in one season, and one of them, Gaius Rufus of
  // the Sixth Cohort, scratched his name and the days since his pay on every wall they slept under: day 29 at
  // Septimus, day 34 in the notch. The March, as the Cohort teaches it, is thirty-three days from the sea to the Eagle. Under the first scratches, later hands: the
  // rearguard's. They are a different story told in the same plaster, and the second story is the one the Cohort
  // does not tell its children.
  //
  // The milestones count the road down in cables (a hundred paces) to the gate of the Last Cohort, where the last
  // stone says I and nothing comes after it. The road did not run from the Cohort to anywhere: it was built to it.

  Discovery rufus_1 "At the Mansio Septimus a soldier scratched: RUFUS, VI COH. DAY 29. A HUNDRED AND FORTY DAYS UNPAID. THE GENERAL SAYS AT THE SEA, THEY ALL SAY AT THE SEA.".
  Discovery rufus_2 "At the Mansio Octavius the same hand: RUFUS. DAY 31. BREAD BAD. THE MULES DIED AT THE THIRD ARCH, TWO. THE MULETEER SAT DOWN AND DID NOT GET UP. Under it, in a squarer hand: THE SCRIBE LEFT THIS BOOK. SOMEONE WILL WANT IT.".
  Discovery rufus_3 "At the Eagle the same hand, shaky: RUFUS. DAY 33. THE BRIDGE WENT UNDER THE FOURTH WAGON. THE ENGINEERS SAY THE FIFTH ARCH WAS ALWAYS BAD. THE REARGUARD IS TO MEND IT. WE ARE NOT TO WAIT. And under it, in a different hand, many strokes: WE WAITED.".
  Discovery rufus_4 "In the notch of the Pass the soldier's last: RUFUS. DAY 34. THE GENERAL HAS GONE ON WITH THE EAGLES. HE SAYS HE WILL SEND FROM THE SEA. TELL MY WIFE THE BOOTS HELD. Below it, in hands that change with the centuries, small tallies: five strokes and a bar. Each bar is a spring.".
  Discovery rufus_5 "Over the gate of the Last Cohort, the soldiers' own reply, cut deep: WE ARE HERE. SEND. And under it, in a hand that has recut it a hundred times: SEND.".

  Observe gr_septimus at septimus_hall as sign when true "A wall of soldiers' scratches, layer on layer: tallies, names, a wheel with a face drawn in it, a sum worked out and rubbed out. One hand returns at shoulder height, neat, as if the man had his back against the wall: RUFUS, VI COH. DAY 29. A HUNDRED AND FORTY DAYS UNPAID. THE GENERAL SAYS AT THE SEA, THEY ALL SAY AT THE SEA." teaches rufus_1.
  Observe gr_octavius at octavius_hall as sign when true "On the bare wall above the scribe's bench, the same hand: RUFUS. DAY 31. BREAD BAD. THE MULES DIED AT THE THIRD ARCH, TWO. THE MULETEER SAT DOWN AND DID NOT GET UP. Under it a squarer hand: THE SCRIBE LEFT THIS BOOK. SOMEONE WILL WANT IT." teaches rufus_2.
  Observe gr_eagle at eagle as sign when true "The gate-arch of the Eagle is scored with the Muster's last scratches: RUFUS. DAY 33. THE BRIDGE WENT UNDER THE FOURTH WAGON. THE ENGINEERS SAY THE FIFTH ARCH WAS ALWAYS BAD. THE REARGUARD IS TO MEND IT. WE ARE NOT TO WAIT. And under it, deep, a great many times: WE WAITED." teaches rufus_3.
  Observe gr_roadhouse at roadhouse as sign when true "In the plaster by the hearth, under the soot: the tallies. Five strokes and a bar, five strokes and a bar, down to the floor, in hands that change. Each bar is a spring." teaches rufus_4.
  Observe gr_gate at gate as sign when true "Over the gate, cut deep and bright and recut by every century: WE ARE HERE. SEND. And a little below, in a later and smaller hand, shorter: SEND." teaches rufus_5.

  // The milestones, which count down in cables to the gate.
  Discovery stones_known "The legion's milestones count down in cables, a hundred paces, to the gate of the Last Cohort, where the last stone says I. The road was built to it, not from it.".
  Observe ms_kell at green as sign when true "By the trough stands a milestone older than the village, the legion's wheel cut on it and, under the wheel, a number: CCCLX. Nobody in Kell Ford has ever asked what it counts down to. The old men say it is the days to the sea, and the surveyor says it is not.".
  Observe ms_septimus at septimus_hall as sign when true "Beyond the door, the milestone: CCLX, and the wheel, and in a rubbed corner the engineers' mark, a dividers and a plumb-line." teaches stones_known.
  Observe ms_octavius at octavius_hall as sign when true "At the lintel, the stone: CLX. Whoever set it set it level to the width of a hair.".
  Observe ms_eagle at eagle as sign when true "The stone by the gate-arch says LX. It has been oiled. Someone comes here.".
  Observe gr_notch at pass_n as sign when true "Cut in the crag at a walker's eye, where the road goes through: RUFUS. DAY 34. THE GENERAL HAS GONE ON WITH THE EAGLES. HE SAYS HE WILL SEND FROM THE SEA. TELL MY WIFE THE BOOTS HELD. And under it the tallies, five strokes and a bar." teaches rufus_4.
  Observe ms_pass at pass_n as sign when true "In the notch: XXX, and a heap of small stones laid on its head, one for each spring that anyone has come this far.".
  Observe ms_descent at descent as sign when true "On the Descent, the stones come thick, one to every mile, each swept: X, VIIII, VIII, as if the engineers could not bear to leave the last miles uncounted. The paving has been swept between them.".

  // The Cohort's own stone, inside the gate: the count ends.
  Observe ms_forum at forum as sign when true "Just inside the gate, at the foot of the forum steps, the last stone of the road: I. Nothing is cut after it. The mason left the rest of the face smooth, as one leaves a page.".
}

data/adventures/muster/history.kyr:
Module muster "The Long Muster's Road" {
  // ── Eleven hundred and four years, as the Annals keep them ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // The valley's history goes beyond the company's visit: three years the Annals mark in red, each a quarrel that
  // made the valley what it is. They are told by the Archivist, one topic apiece, once he trusts the company; and
  // each leaves a custom in the valley that can be seen without being told.
  Relation annalsOpen(group).
  Discovery second_wife_known "In the year 212 the Prefect Aulus took a second wife from the Hearth while the first, an Eagle's daughter, still lived; the valley has eaten at two tables since.".
  Discovery plague_winter_known "In the year 640 a plague killed a third of the valley in a winter; the lot was drawn, and the courier died at the Sending gate, and since then the Sending goes out with a white cord at its wrist.".
  Discovery schism_known "In the year 871 the Eagles proposed to send the standard itself north with the Sending, to be put into the Emperor's hand. In the night the gold wings were taken, and a brass eagle in a skin of bronze stood on the pole by morning. The Eagles said the Hearth had stolen them. A man was killed. The quarrel is two hundred and thirty-three years old.".
  Discovery two_sendings_known "In the year 1002 the valley sent two couriers, one by the Eagles and one in secret by the Hearth; neither came home; neither is in the Annals but in the margin, as: both.".

  Topic ostrander_annals with ostrander "Ask the Archivist to open the Annals" when admitted(party) and not annalsOpen(party) and not told_ostrander(party) and not sealNoted(party) and knows(actor, rufus_1) {
    Says "He lifts down a book that takes both arms. 'Eleven hundred and four years, mostly weather and who was Prefect. Four years are written in red.' He keeps his hand on the cover. 'I show the red ones to someone who has sworn on the shrine lamp not to use them against the valley, and who has brought me one thing I do not already know. You have brought Rufus. Swear.'".
    On discuss { Assert annalsOpen(party). }
  }
  Topic ostrander_gold with ostrander "Ask the Archivist where the Hearth hid the Eagle's wings" when annalsOpen(party) and ostranderSwore(party) and knows(actor, schism_known) and not knows(actor, gold_known) once {
    Says "Ostrander does not answer for some while. He takes down a ledger of vine-house rents and turns it to a page where one farm is let for forty years at a rent of nothing. 'The old farm under the terraces,' he says. 'You swore on the lamp. I have broken no oath of mine by letting you read a rent-roll.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, gold_known). }
  }
  Topic ostrander_wife with ostrander "Ask the Archivist about the year of the second wife" when annalsOpen(party) and ostranderSwore(party) {
    Says "'The year two hundred and twelve,' says Ostrander. 'The Prefect Aulus was an Eagle, and his wife an Eagle's daughter, and he thought she was barren. So he took a second wife from the Hearth, a farmer's girl with no schooling. And the first wife lived. The valley has eaten at two tables since.' He taps the line. 'The Prefect eats at the Eagles' table one day and the Hearth's the next. Nobody remembers why. Nobody has broken it.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, second_wife_known). }
  }
  Topic ostrander_plague with ostrander "Ask the Archivist about the plague winter" when annalsOpen(party) and ostranderSwore(party) {
    Says "'Six hundred and forty. One in three.' Ostrander turns a page. 'The Sending was due and the lot fell on a girl of nineteen, and she walked out of the gate with the pouch and fell down dead on the path with her white cord on. Nobody touched her for a day. Since then the courier wears a white cord. It is not for mourning. It is so the valley knows whose turn it was, if there is no one left to say.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, plague_winter_known). }
  }
  Topic ostrander_schism with ostrander "Ask the Archivist about the Schism of the Standard" when annalsOpen(party) and ostranderSwore(party) {
    Says "'Eight hundred and seventy-one,' says Ostrander, in the voice of a man reading a charge sheet. 'A young Aquilifer proposed in the evening call that the standard be carried north with the Sending and put into the Emperor's hand. The Eagles roared. The Hearth said nothing. In the night the wings were taken. In the morning a brass eagle in a skin of bronze stood on the pole, and the Aquilifer, who loved the old bird more than his mother, hanged himself in the shrine.' He shuts the book. 'Three days of fighting in the forum. A man killed. The Eagles have said since that the Hearth stole the gold. The Hearth has said nothing at all.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, schism_known). }
  }
  Topic ostrander_two with ostrander "Ask the Archivist about the year of two Sendings" when annalsOpen(party) and knows(actor, schism_known) {
    Says "'One thousand and two. The Annals say the Eagles' courier went, as ever. In the margin, in a hand I do not know, one word: BOTH. A courier from the Hearth, by the goat path, in the night.' He taps it. 'Neither came home. I have looked for the second name for thirty years.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, two_sendings_known). }
  }
  Observe beam_scene at archive when true "A roof-beam of black cedar runs the length of the Archive, and cut along its underside, in small even notches grouped in fives, is a tally that goes from the north wall to the south. The last groups are fresh and pale. There are, you count idly, a great many.".
  Observe two_tables at praetorium_hall when knows(party, second_wife_known) "Two tables stand in the hall's end, set for a meal, one with red cloth and one with brown. The Prefect, you notice, is at the red one today.".
  Observe white_cord at gate as sign when knows(party, plague_winter_known) "On a peg inside the gate hangs a white cord, worn to a grey rope, waiting for the spring.".
  Observe schism_scar at forum when knows(party, schism_known) "On the paving at the foot of the standard's pole there is a darker patch, worn smooth. Nobody walks on it. The children step round it.".
}

data/adventures/muster/interview.kyr:
Module muster "The Long Muster's Road" {
  // ── Questions put to the company ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // The valley asks before it receives. The Prefect puts three questions to whoever comes in at the gate; the
  // answers are the company's character in the valley's eyes (and in the Prefect's), and they shape what the
  // valley will hear and from whom. Smaller questions are put by the giant, the apothecary, the salt-reckoner, the
  // Centurion, the Archivist, the innkeeper and the reeve, each of whom wants to know one thing before they will
  // tell one thing.
  Measure prefectRegard(group). Relation interviewQ1(group). Relation interviewQ2(group). Relation interviewQ3(group).
  Relation reeveNamed(group). Relation orsaFair(group). Relation orsaAnswered(group). Relation rheaAnswered(group).
  Relation ostranderAnswered(group). Relation ostranderSwore(group).

  // The Prefect's interview.
  Topic corvin_q1 with corvin "Let the Prefect put his first question" when admitted(party) and not interviewQ1(party) once {
    Says "Corvin folds his hands on the accounts. 'I shall ask three things,' he says, 'as my fathers asked of every stranger who came to the gate, in the days when we had strangers. First: who sent you?'".
  }
  Topic corvin_q1_none with corvin "Say that nobody sent you: you came to see what the road led to" answering corvin_q1 when true {
    Says "'Men used to come because they were told,' says Corvin. 'Nobody has come for the pleasure of it in my time.' He nods, and waits.".
    On discuss { Assert interviewQ1(party). Increase prefectRegard(party) by 2. }
  }
  Topic corvin_q1_north with corvin "Say that the north sent you: you are what the Emperor's silence looks like" answering corvin_q1 when true {
    Says "A pause in which the water-clock can be heard. 'That is a fine sentence,' says Corvin. 'Whoever taught you to say it was not wrong.' He looks down. 'I do not believe you are what you say, and I am glad you said it.'".
    On discuss { Assert interviewQ1(party). Increase prefectRegard(party) by 1. }
  }
  Topic corvin_q1_private with corvin "Say that you would tell him alone, and no one else" answering corvin_q1 when true {
    Says "'Ah,' says Corvin, and glances at the clerk, who finds a reason to be at the far end of the hall. 'Then it is a thing worth hearing.' He leans forward, and the clerk's pen stops somewhere beyond the door.".
    On discuss { Assert interviewQ1(party). Increase prefectRegard(party) by 1. }
  }
  Topic corvin_q2 with corvin "Let the Prefect put his second question" when interviewQ1(party) and not interviewQ2(party) once {
    Says "'Second,' says Corvin. 'You came through the forum. You saw the standard. Tell me what you saw in it.'".
  }
  Topic corvin_q2_fine with corvin "Say that it is the most beautiful thing you have seen on the road" answering corvin_q2 when true {
    Says "'It is,' says Corvin. 'I have looked at it every day for sixteen years and I do not know why.' He turns his ring once on its finger.".
    On discuss { Assert interviewQ2(party). Increase prefectRegard(party) by 1. }
  }
  Topic corvin_q2_heavy with corvin "Say that it looks heavy: that it looks like something people carry" answering corvin_q2 when true {
    Says "'Orsa says that, and Elpis,' says Corvin. 'A boy of ten said it last week, to his mother, over soup.' He looks at the doors, and does not call for anyone.".
    On discuss { Assert interviewQ2(party). Increase prefectRegard(party) by 2. }
  }
  Topic corvin_q2_metal with corvin "Say that it is metal on a pole, and the people are the thing" answering corvin_q2 when true {
    Says "'Metal on a pole,' says Corvin, without heat. 'That is what the Hearth says. It is also what my fathers said in the plague winter, in private, with the doors shut.' The doors get his attention. 'I have never heard it said in the Praetorium.'".
    On discuss { Assert interviewQ2(party). }
  }
  Topic corvin_q3 with corvin "Let the Prefect put his last question" when interviewQ2(party) and not interviewQ3(party) once {
    Says "'Third,' says Corvin, and his voice has changed. 'What is north of the Viaduct?'".
  }
  Topic corvin_q3_kell with corvin "Say that there is a village, thirty houses round a trough, and they keep a lamp in the window" answering corvin_q3 when knows(actor, lamp_known) {
    Says "Corvin does not move. 'A lamp,' he says. 'In a window. For whom?' 'They don't know,' you say. 'They put it in because they always have.' He rubs his jaw. 'Well,' he says. 'We were looking at the wrong end.'".
    On discuss { Assert interviewQ3(party). Assert received(party). Increase prefectRegard(party) by 2. }
  }
  Topic corvin_q3_bishop with corvin "Say that there are scholars at Hale who keep the empire's old archives" answering corvin_q3 when true {
    Says "'Archives,' says Corvin. 'Scholars.' A light goes into his face. 'Then there is a place the Emperor's name is written, and read.' He turns the idea over like a coin. 'I shall write them a dispatch.'".
    On discuss { Assert interviewQ3(party). Assert received(party). Increase prefectRegard(party) by 1. }
  }
  Topic corvin_q3_refuse with corvin "Say that you will not answer, because the answer is not yours to give" answering corvin_q3 when true {
    Says "'Not yours,' says Corvin, after a time. 'No.' He nods. 'That is a fair answer. It is the answer of a courier who has been told something to carry and not told what.' He stands. 'You may go where you will in the valley, on that.'".
    On discuss { Assert interviewQ3(party). Increase prefectRegard(party) by 1. }
  }

  // The giant's question.
  Topic giant_asks with giant "Ask the giant what he wants besides iron" when tolerates(giant, party) or giantPaid(party) once {
    Says "The giant turns the fire-iron over and looks at the end of it. 'Is the king still king?' he says. 'The one I keep this house for. He had a beard, I think. It was a long time ago.'".
  }
  Topic giant_yes with giant "Say that the king is still king, and sends his regards" answering giant_asks when true {
    Says "'Good,' says the giant, with relief. He puts down the iron and takes a leg of mutton off the hook and holds it out. 'Eat. Don't tell the little ones.'".
    On discuss { Increase quantity(actor, rations) by 2. }
  }
  Topic giant_no with giant "Say that the king is dead, and so is his kingdom, and you are sorry" answering giant_asks when true {
    Says "The giant turns the iron in the fire. 'Then I keep the house for no one,' he says. 'That is easier. Go on. I'll mind the fire.' He does not offer the mutton. At the door he tells you, unasked, what the marmots do when he wakes, which is the one thing he knows that is of use to anybody.".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, marmots_known). }
  }

  // The reeve's question.
  Topic reeve_names with cattermole "Let the reeve ask where to write if you do not come back" when true once {
    Says "The reeve has his hammer in his fist and a nail between his teeth. 'The board has forty-one names,' he says round the nail. 'Every one of them, I wrote, to somebody. I would like not to write a forty-second to nobody. Who?'".
  }
  Topic reeve_name_one with cattermole "Give the name of someone at home" answering reeve_names when true {
    Says "He writes it in a small neat hand on a slip, and pins the slip to the board, under the others. 'There,' he says. 'That is done properly.'".
    On discuss { Assert reeveNamed(party). }
  }
  Topic reeve_name_none with cattermole "Say there is no one" answering reeve_names when true {
    Says "'Then I'll write it to the road,' says the reeve, after a moment. 'It can have a letter. It has had all the rest of us.' He writes nothing on the board. He does not pin anything.".
    On discuss { }
  }

  Topic reeve_slip with cattermole "Ask the reeve for the Ford's travel-fund, now that your name is on his board" when reeveNamed(party) once {
    Says "The reeve counts four coin out of a tin that says FOR THE RELIEF OF TRAVELLERS and does not look at the lid. 'It has had no use since the Muster,' he says. 'It might as well have you.'".
    On discuss { Increase quantity(actor, gold) by 4. }
  }

  // The apothecary's question.
  Topic orsa_asks with orsa "Let Dame Orsa ask what herbs you carried over the Fells" when true once {
    Says "She sets down the pestle and looks at you straight. 'Tell me honestly,' she says. 'You carried herbs over thirty miles of fell to sell to a woman you hadn't met. What did you think they were worth?'".
  }
  Topic orsa_ans_fair with orsa "Say that you asked a fair price, and would take a fair one" answering orsa_asks when true {
    Says "'Then I shall be fair,' she says, and is pleased, and does not hide it. She opens a drawer. 'Kingsfoil, ninety. Is that fair?' She counts twelve coin into your hand for the herbs you carried, and one more, and says that is the bread.".
    On discuss { Assert orsaAnswered(party). Assert orsaFair(party). Increase quantity(actor, gold) by 12. }
  }
  Topic orsa_ans_high with orsa "Say that nothing from the north was ever worth less than gold to you" answering orsa_asks when true {
    Says "'Gold,' she says, and laughs. 'We have no gold. We have salt and bread and I can give you a cure for anything you have.' She points a stained finger. 'But I take the compliment.' She does not open the drawer. A woman who is told her herbs are worth gold is a woman who expects to be asked for more.".
    On discuss { Assert orsaAnswered(party). }
  }

  // The salt-reckoner's question.
  Topic rhea_asks with rhea "Let the salt-reckoner ask how you came by the Aenean coin" when true once {
    Says "Rhea does not look up. 'Aenean silver does not turn up in a stranger's pocket,' she says. 'Where did you get it? Be exact. I shall know.'".
  }
  Topic rhea_ans_road with rhea "Say you took it off the stack at the Eagle, where the Muster left it" answering rhea_asks when true {
    Says "'Dead men's silver on a dead men's road,' says Rhea. She weighs it, and counts ten back out of her box. 'The finder's share. We always paid it.'".
    On discuss { Assert rheaAnswered(party). Increase quantity(actor, gold) by 10. }
  }
  Topic rhea_ans_sale with rhea "Say you were paid it, for a service, in the world beyond the Fells" answering rhea_asks when true {
    Says "She looks at you until the scale settles. 'There is no world beyond the Fells,' she says. 'But I take your meaning.' She slides the coin back across the trestle. 'Do not say that to the Eagles.' She keeps five of it, for the silence, and does not say that she has.".
    On discuss { Assert rheaAnswered(party). Decrease quantity(actor, gold) by 5 or to nothing. }
  }

  // The Archivist's question.
  Topic ostrander_asks with ostrander "Let the Archivist ask what it is you want of the Annals" when annalsOpen(party) once {
    Says "'Before I show you a thing I must know what it will be used for,' says Ostrander. 'A history in the wrong hand is a knife. What do you want to do with it?'".
  }
  Topic ostrander_ans_know with ostrander "Say that you only want to know" answering ostrander_asks when true {
    Says "'That is what the first Archivist said, in that chair,' he says. 'It is the right reason and the most dangerous.' He turns a page. 'Read.'".
    On discuss { Assert ostranderAnswered(party). }
  }
  Topic ostrander_ans_swear with ostrander "Swear on the lamp of the shrine that you will not use it to harm the valley" answering ostrander_asks when true {
    Says "He weighs the oath in his hand, as if it had a mass. 'It is a fine thing to swear on,' he says. 'Eleven hundred years of lamp.' He nods, once. 'I shall hold you to it.'".
    On discuss { Assert ostranderAnswered(party). Assert ostranderSwore(party). }
  }
}

data/adventures/muster/placed.kyr:
Module muster "The Long Muster's Road" {
  Entity party: group "Your company".
  // What is said of the Hesper Fells in the country behind it: once the company is back at the Ford, it is old news.
  Entity word: fact "What is said of Kell Ford and the road in the country behind it.".
  Rule word_old when (backAtFord() or plainReturn()) and not stale(word) { Assert stale(word). }
}

data/adventures/muster/quests.kyr:
Module muster "The Long Muster's Road" {
  // ── Six things on the road that lead nowhere in particular ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // None of them is on the way to the Cohort, and none is needed to get there. Each has a place, a face and a
  // resolution by a skill or a choice, and each says something about the road that the main thread does not: that
  // someone lived here, that a god once was believed in, that a mistake was made on purpose, that a feud is about
  // water, that a pack has a widow, that a man went on alone.
  Relation quintusFound(group). Relation viatorHonoured(group). Relation stonesFound(group). Relation springSettled(group).
  Relation widowAnswered(group). Relation graveDug(group). Relation pelt_laid(group).
  Relation springMarra(group). Relation viatorUntold(group). Relation digSpent(group). Relation springSnubbed(group). Relation viatorSilent(group).

  // 1. Quintus went on. Five mansio walls carry a second hand: a Q and a stroke, an arrow, and a line.
  Discovery quintus_1 "At Septimus, under Rufus, a second and rougher hand: Q. WENT ON, and an arrow cut north-east, towards no road.".
  Discovery quintus_2 "At Octavius: Q. WENT ON. FOUND WATER. A drawing of a spring and a tree.".
  Discovery quintus_3 "At the Eagle: Q. WENT ON, AND CAME BACK, AND WENT ON. HE DOES NOT WANT TO BE FOUND; HE WANTS TO BE FOLLOWED. A line from the gate-arch to the thorn behind the fort.".
  Observe qu_septimus at septimus_hall as sign when true "Under the soldier Rufus, in a rougher hand, a Q and a stroke: Q. WENT ON, and an arrow cut hard to the north-east, where there is no road." teaches quintus_1.
  Observe qu_octavius at octavius_hall as sign when true "Beside the scribe's bench the second hand again, a day or a decade later: Q. WENT ON. FOUND WATER. Under it a spring and a tree, drawn by someone who cannot draw." teaches quintus_2.
  Observe qu_eagle at eagle as sign when true "On the gate-arch, low, where a man sitting would reach: Q. WENT ON, AND CAME BACK, AND WENT ON. A line runs from the lettering across the wall to the thorn behind the fort." teaches quintus_3.
  Action follow_q_marks(actor: group, site: place) "Follow the Q-marks behind the fort into the thorn" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(actor, hollow) and knows(actor, quintus_1) and knows(actor, quintus_2) and knows(actor, quintus_3) and quantity(actor, rations) >= 1 and not quintusFound(actor).
    Time 2.
    Resolve check(actor, wayfinding, dc_fair).
    On success { Assert quintusFound(actor). Assert knows(actor, quintus_known). }
    Result "A Q on a thorn, another on a stone, a third on the lip of the spring. You find the grave in a niche of the rock under a pear tree grown from a pip: a mound the length of a man, and a flat slab, and on the slab cut with great care: Q. WENT ON. HERE HE STOPPED. THE KINGSFOIL IS MINE; TAKE IT. He had walked out of the Muster in its first year and lived forty winters in the hollow behind the Eagle, tending the one green place on the fell. The warm turf is his doing: he carried the soil up in a helmet.".
    On failure { Decrease quantity(actor, rations) by 1 or to nothing. }
    Otherwise "The marks are there, but they lead in a circle round a thorn-brake, and the light is going.".
  }
  Entity quintus_known: fact "Quintus, a soldier of the Muster, walked out of the march in its first year and lived forty winters in the hollow behind the Eagle, making the one warm green place on the fell. He is buried under a pear tree he grew from a pip.".
  Observe quintus_grave near hollow_kingsfoil when quintusFound(party) "Where the grave is, the kingsfoil grows thickest. A pear tree leans over it with a few small hard fruit.".

  // 2. The shrine of Viator, the road's forgotten god, in the mouth of the Gallery.
  Discovery viator_known "The Walker-by, Viator, was the legion's god of the road: he asks a boot-nail from those who go in and a story from those who come out, and he lights a lamp for the former.".
  Thing viator_niche "the niche of Viator" at gallery1 {
    State dark_niche "In the wall by the Gallery's mouth is a niche with a stone face worn to a thumbprint, a heap of boot-nails in a dish, and a lamp with no oil in it.".
    State lit_niche "The lamp in the niche burns, small and steady, and the face looks out of it with an expression of interested patience.".
    Change light_lamp "Light the lamp of Viator with lamp oil" from dark_niche to lit_niche needing lamp_oil consuming lamp_oil.
  }
  Initially Assert state(viator_niche, dark_niche).
  Action offer_nail(actor: group, site: place) "Leave a boot-nail in the dish for Viator, and say the old words for those who go in" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(actor, gallery1) and not viatorHonoured(actor) and not viatorSilent(actor).
    Time 1.
    Resolve check(actor, lore, dc_fair).
    On success { Assert viatorHonoured(actor). Assert knows(actor, viator_known). Assert state(third_arch, found). }
    Result "You have the words from a milestone, and they come out in the right order: VIATOR, WALKER-BY, WHO KNOWS WHICH STONES HOLD, KEEP US. A cold draught goes in past you, not out, and somewhere in the dark of the Gallery something settles. You know, without being told, which of the stones at the Third Arch will not bear you.".
    On failure { Assert viatorSilent(actor). }
    Otherwise "You have most of the words. The dish rings under the nail. Nothing answers, which is, you suppose, the answer.".
  }
  Action tell_viator(actor: group, site: place) "Tell Viator what you saw on the road, as the old rule asks of those who come out" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(actor, gallery1) and viatorHonoured(actor) and been(actor, octavius_hall) and not viatorUntold(actor).
    Time 1.
    Resolve check(actor, performance, dc_fair).
    On success { Assert knows(actor, viator_told). }
    Result "You tell the niche about the Third Arch and what you did to it. The lamp, which was out, is lit. You do not remember lighting it.".
    On failure { Assert viatorUntold(actor). }
    Otherwise "You tell it badly, and the niche keeps its face. The lamp stays out. The old rule asks once.".
  }
  Entity viator_told: fact "The road-god Viator, who is told stories by those who come out of the Gallery, keeps a lamp for the ones who tell him.".

  // 3. The lost surveyor's survey stones: Thessaly Orde, who measured the Ledge and was not believed.
  Discovery orde_known "Thessaly Orde, surveyor of the road-menders, set seven survey stones along the Ledge and wrote that the wind there followed a rule; the road-menders did not believe her and she went home.".
  Observe orde_stone at ledge1 as sign when true "In the cliff at shoulder height, in the lee of the first buttress: a small bronze stud with a number, VII. The same stud, VI, is at the next buttress. Someone who could count could follow them." teaches orde_known.
  Item orde_level "a surveyor's level, green with age" is spyglass in orde_cairn hidden until orde_known.
  Thing orde_cairn "a cairn at the end of the Ledge" at ledge2 {
    State heaped "A cairn of flat stones, with something square wrapped in oilskin inside it.".
    State opened "The cairn has been pulled apart.".
    Change open_cairn "Take the oilskin bundle out of the cairn" from heaped to opened.
    Holds.
  }
  Action follow_orde(actor: group, site: place) "Follow the survey studs and set your feet by the numbers" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(actor, ledge1) and knows(actor, orde_known) and quantity(actor, rations) >= 1 and not stonesFound(actor).
    Time 1.
    Resolve check(actor, wayfinding, dc_fair).
    On success { Assert stonesFound(actor). Assert state(ledge_wind, steady). }
    Result "Seven studs, and a rule: the gusts come up the buttress faces in the count of seven, and stop on the eighth. Orde had it right. You cross to the end in the pauses, and at the last stone you find, in the cairn, her notebook.".
    On failure { Decrease quantity(actor, rations) by 1 or to nothing. }
    Otherwise "The studs are there, and the rule she saw in them is not, to you, yet.".
  }

  // 4. The goatherds' feud: two families, one spring.
  Place spring "the spring on the goatherds' shelf" is room within lowroads at 3, 0 outdoors.
  Initially Assert exposed(spring).
  Passage spring_path "the goats' path to the spring" is path between track1 and spring taking 20 minutes.
  Observe spring_scene at spring when true "A shelf of turf under an overhang, with a spring coming out of the rock into a stone trough, and two lines of goat-droppings that stop on either side of a white-painted stone, never crossing. On one side is a lean-to with a blue door. On the other, a lean-to with a red. Both are shut, and both are watched.".
  Person hartnoll "Old Anselm Hartnoll" at spring {
    Role "a goatherd of the red door". Ancestry human. Pronouns he. Manner grim. Peaceable.
    Greets "An old man on a milking stool on the red side of the stone does not turn his head. 'Marra Tellis sent you,' he says, 'or you'd not have found it. She's wrong. My father had the trough, and his before him. The Tellises took the water by dividing the stones in the night.'".
  }
  Topic corrin_ask with hartnoll "Ask Anselm Hartnoll what the quarrel is about" when true once {
    Says "'A trough,' says Hartnoll. 'Two goats a day's water out of eleven hundred years of rain. It is not the water, it's who is asked.' He looks at the white stone. 'Would you ask me, if you had the choice? Would you ask her?'".
  }
  Topic corrin_me with hartnoll "Say you would ask whoever has the better claim" answering corrin_ask when true {
    Says "'The better claim,' says Hartnoll, and spits. 'There's the question.' He looks at you for the first time. 'Check the old wall. There is a stone cut with a notch. It was my grandfather's idea that the notch was a tally. It's a level.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, spring_level). }
  }
  Topic corrin_her with hartnoll "Say you would ask Marra, because she asked you first" answering corrin_ask when true {
    Says "'Of course you would,' says Hartnoll, without heat. 'She sent you, and a goatherd's guest eats the goatherd's cheese. I'd do the same.' He picks up the pail. 'I'll tell you what I tell everyone. I don't mind losing. I mind it being said I'd lost to a girl with a better lawyer.'".
    On discuss { Assert springMarra(party). }
  }
  Entity spring_level: fact "A notch cut in the old wall above the spring is a level: the water, laid out fairly, would run to the red door's trough as much as the blue's.".
  Action settle_spring(actor: group, site: place) "Cut the trough a second lip by the old level, so that the water divides by itself" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(actor, spring) and knows(actor, spring_level) and quantity(actor, rations) >= 1 and not springSettled(actor).
    Time 3.
    Resolve check(actor, digging, dc_fair).
    On success { Assert springSettled(actor). }
    Result "A lip of an inch, cut with the crowbar's edge and checked against the notch. The trough fills on both sides at the same pace. Hartnoll comes over, looks, and goes on looking, and then takes his pail to the red end and fills it. 'It's what I always said it should do,' he says.".
    On failure { Decrease quantity(actor, rations) by 1 or to nothing. }
    Otherwise "The lip comes out crooked. The water runs to the red door, and the blue door opens, and the quarrel resumes with more matter.".
  }
  Action persuade_spring(actor: group, site: place) "Persuade the old man to ask Marra's mother for the water, as a courtesy, and her to give it" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(actor, spring) and not springSettled(actor) and not springSnubbed(actor) and not marraAngry(actor) and springMarra(actor).
    Time 2.
    Resolve check(actor, persuasion, dc_hard).
    On success { Assert springSettled(actor). Assert knows(actor, spring_pact). }
    Result "It takes two hours and a cheese, and both doors open at once. Nobody has ever apologised: you are told, afterwards, that they asked each other to dinner, which is the same thing.".
    On failure { Decrease quantity(actor, rations) by 1 or to nothing. Assert springSnubbed(actor). }
    Otherwise "'I won't be told by a stranger,' says Hartnoll, and goes into the red door and shuts it.".
  }
  Entity spring_pact: fact "The red and blue doors at the goatherds' spring asked each other to dinner after thirty years.".
  Observe spring_peace at spring when springSettled(party) "The white stone has been moved a hand's breadth into the red side, and a child of each family is sitting on it eating the other's bread.".

  // 5. The milepost that is wrong by a mile.
  Discovery milepost_known "The milepost at the long middle of the causeway says XLI, and the one before it says XXXIX: the mile is missing, and the legion's engineers did not make mistakes.".
  Observe milepost_scene at causeway2 as sign when true "The milepost reads XLI. The one behind it, at the causeway's head, read XXXIX. There is no XL; the road goes straight and level for a mile in which nothing is counted, and the ridge to either side is cropped short by sheep that never go into it." teaches milepost_known.
  Action dig_unnumbered(actor: group, site: place) "Pace out the unnumbered mile and dig where the heather will not grow" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(actor, causeway2) and knows(actor, milepost_known) and holding(actor, shovel) and not graveDug(actor) and not digSpent(actor).
    Time 4.
    Resolve check(actor, digging, dc_fair).
    On success { Assert graveDug(actor). Assert knows(actor, sappers_known). }
    Result "A foot down, packed chalk; two feet, a bronze buckle; four, the first of forty skulls under a sheet of lead stamped COHORS FABRORUM. The sappers who built the causeway died of a spring fever and were buried at the road's centre, in the mile the engineers refused to number. They did not want it said that any mile of it was theirs to count the dead against.".
    On failure { Assert digSpent(actor). }
    Otherwise "You dig an hour and find chalk, and more chalk, and the heather watches.".
  }
  Entity sappers_known: fact "The unnumbered mile of the Old Causeway is the burial of forty sappers who died of fever building it; the engineers left the mile uncounted out of respect.".
  Item sapper_buckle "a bronze buckle stamped with a sapper's mallet" is bronze_bracelet in sapper_cache hidden until sappers_known.
  Thing sapper_cache "the sappers' burial" at causeway2 { State covered "The road runs level here, uncounted.". }

  // 6. The wolf-widow.
  Kind wolf_pelt_kind "a grey wolf's pelt, tanned" is treasure worth 8 weighing 3 described "A pelt tanned on the inn's wall for thirty years, grey going white at the muzzle, with a notch in the left ear.".
  Item wolf_pelt "the wolf's pelt on the inn wall" is wolf_pelt_kind at taproom.
  Topic maud_pelt with greta "Ask Greta about the wolf's pelt on the wall" when true once {
    Says "'My husband's,' says Greta, 'from the year the sheep went. A big male, a notch in his ear, took twenty ewes and a boy's dog. They put the shot in him on the causeway and my husband skinned him the same night.' She wipes a glass that is clean. 'There was a she-wolf. She sat on the ridge three nights in the frost and cried. She's the one they follow now. I would give a good deal not to have heard it.'".
  }
  Topic maud_widow with greta "Ask Greta whether the she-wolf has ever been paid" answering maud_pelt when true {
    Says "Greta stops wiping. 'Paid,' she says, as if trying the word for weight. 'I never thought you could pay a wolf.' She takes the pelt off its hook, shakes it, and holds it out. 'Take it. If you can put it where she'll find it, put it. I have had it thirty years. I would like to be rid of what it says.'".
    On discuss { Assert widowAnswered(party). }
  }
  Action lay_pelt(actor: group, site: place) "Lay the old male's pelt on the causeway for the widow, and walk on without looking back" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(actor, causeway2) and holding(actor, wolf_pelt_kind) and widowAnswered(actor) and not pelt_laid(actor).
    Time 0.
    Resolve check(actor, insight, dc_fair).
    On success { Spend wolf_pelt_kind from actor. Assert pelt_laid(actor). Assert tolerates(wolves, actor). Retract warned(wolves, actor). Assert wolvesTolerated(actor). }
    Result "A long way off in the heather a grey shape stands up, and watches, and does not come. You lay the pelt across the road with the muzzle toward the hill, as you would lay a body, and you do not stop. Behind you, much later, you hear the sound a wolf makes that is not a howl.".
    On failure { Spend wolf_pelt_kind from actor. }
    Otherwise "You lay it down too quickly and walk off too fast. The wolves watch you go as if you had thrown it at them.".
  }
}

data/adventures/muster/road.kyr:
Module muster "The Long Muster's Road" {
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // The weather. A province's weather is not the region's: this sky is the road's own, and turns at first light.
  Thing sky "the sky over the Fells" at green is scenery {
    State clear_sky "A hard clear sky.".
    State wind_sky "A high wind out of the north, hard enough to lean on.".
    State snow_sky "Snow, early, thin and driven.".
    State thaw_sky "A warm wind and a low sky, and the streams are rising.".
  }
  Initially Assert state(sky, clear_sky).
  Rule sky_clear when untilLight(clock) == 1 and dice(clock) <= 45 and not state(sky, clear_sky) { Assert state(sky, clear_sky). }
  Rule sky_wind when untilLight(clock) == 1 and dice(clock) > 45 and dice(clock) <= 65 and not state(sky, wind_sky) { Assert state(sky, wind_sky). }
  Rule sky_snow when untilLight(clock) == 1 and dice(clock) > 65 and dice(clock) <= 82 and not state(sky, snow_sky) { Assert state(sky, snow_sky). }
  Rule sky_thaw when untilLight(clock) == 1 and dice(clock) > 82 and not state(sky, thaw_sky) { Assert state(sky, thaw_sky). }
  Observe sky_green at green when state(sky, snow_sky) "Snow is falling on Kell Ford, early, and the Ledge will be closed.".
  Observe sky_green_thaw at green when state(sky, thaw_sky) "The trough overflows, and the streams are loud: a warm wind has come and the snow in the hills is going.".
  Observe sky_wind_s at septimus_hall when state(sky, wind_sky) "A high wind is hunting about the Mansio's vault. On a day like this nothing flies.".

  // Thaw floods the low places.
  Thing gorge_water "the river in the gorge" on low_bend is scenery {
    State low "The river runs a thumb deep over the paving at the bend.".
    State rising blocking "The river fills the bend of the gorge from wall to wall, brown and fast. Nothing wades it.".
  }
  Initially Assert state(gorge_water, low).
  Rule gorge_rises when state(sky, thaw_sky) and state(gorge_water, low) { Assert state(gorge_water, rising). }
  Rule gorge_falls when not state(sky, thaw_sky) and state(gorge_water, rising) { Assert state(gorge_water, low). }
  Thing river_water "the river under the Viaduct" on river_ford is scenery {
    State low "The river is waist-deep and loud, with a bar of shingle to wade.".
    State spate blocking "The river is a brown torrent between the piers, rolling boulders. Nobody fords it.".
  }
  Initially Assert state(river_water, low).
  Rule river_rises when state(sky, thaw_sky) and state(river_water, low) { Assert state(river_water, spate). }
  Rule river_falls when not state(sky, thaw_sky) and state(river_water, spate) { Assert state(river_water, low). }

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // The cold. Nothing stops a company sleeping out except what it carries.
  Relation warm(group). Relation chilled(group). Measure fireUntil(group).
  Hazard night_cold "A night out on the fell" means "Deal (1d6) cold damage to ally unless constitution save against (12).".
  Rule night_cold_strikes when dark() and at(party, ?p) and exposed(?p) and not chilled(party) and not warm(party) and not holding(party, tent) {
    Assert strikes(night_cold, party). Assert chilled(party).
  }
  Rule morning_warms when not dark() and chilled(party) { Retract chilled(party). }
  Rule fire_dies when warm(party) and watch(clock) >= fireUntil(party) { Retract warm(party). }
  Action make_fire(actor: group, site: place) "Build a fire of the firewood you carry" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and exposed(site) and holding(actor, firewood) and not warm(actor).
    Time 1.
    On success { Spend firewood from actor. Assert warm(actor). Set fireUntil(actor) to watch(clock) + 60. }
    Result "You split the wood small and make a fire in the lee of a stone. It burns all night, and the cold stays on the other side of it.".
  }

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // The first day. Wolves on the causeway at night.
  Person wolves "the wolves of the ridge" at causeway2 {
    Role "a pack of lean grey wolves, four, hunting the ridge as their fathers did". Species wolf. Level 3. Numbers 4. Temperament territorial.
    Seen when dark().
    Warns "A long note goes up from the heather, and another answers it, and the dark round the road fills with the shapes of wolves, sitting.".
    Comes "The pack comes in from the sides, low.".
    Lair causeway2.
  }
  Observe wolf_tracks at causeway1 as sign when true "Wolf tracks cross the causeway in the dust, many, fresh. The sheep on the hill are all up on the high side of the wall.".
  Observe wolf_howl at causeway2 as sound when dark() "Far off, in the heather, a wolf starts, and is answered.".
  Thing causeway_fire "the causeway's old watch-fire" at causeway2 is scenery {
    State cold "A ring of fire-blackened stones where the legion's pickets made their fire on the ridge.".
  }

  // The second day. The wyvern stoops at noon on the open road, unless the wind keeps it down.
  Person wyvern "the wyvern" at eyrie {
    Role "a grey wyvern, thin-winged and lean with the season". Species wyvern. Level 6. Numbers 1. Temperament territorial.
    Warns "The bracken heaves and a long head comes up out of it on a neck like a ship's cable, with an eye like a coin, and lets out a hiss that sets the stones singing.".
    Comes "The wyvern comes down off its shelf with its wings half open.".
    Lair eyrie.
  }
  Relation stooped(group).
  Hazard wyvern_stoop "The stoop of the wyvern" means "Deal (3d6) physical damage to ally unless dexterity save against (15).".
  Rule wyvern_stoops at high2 when at(party, high2) and hour(clock) >= 11 and hour(clock) < 14 and not state(sky, wind_sky) and not stooped(party)
    says "A shadow goes over the road in one long sweep, and then the sky has a wing in it, and the bird is not a bird." {
    Assert strikes(wyvern_stoop, party). Assert stooped(party).
  }
  Rule stoop_clears when stooped(party) and not at(party, high2) and not hour(clock) >= 11 { Retract stooped(party). }
  Rule stoop_clears_late when stooped(party) and hour(clock) >= 14 { Retract stooped(party). }
  Observe high_shadow at high1 as sign when true "Far up, in the white glare, something is hanging in the sky, still, and then it is not.".
  Observe high_bones at gallery1 as sign when true "A sheep's skull lies by the mouth of the Gallery, opened at the crown. The herdsmen of Kell Ford will tell you why you do not cross the open stretch at noon.".
  Thing third_arch "the Third Arch" on gallery_b {
    State whole dangerous "The roadway across the Third Arch is paved and level, and looks as sound as the rest.".
    State found "A slab of the roadway has been pried up and thrown into the crack: the arch beneath is rotted through, and the sound way runs along the pier on the left.".
    Hurts with arch_fall when whole then found.
  }
  Hazard arch_fall "The rotten arch" means "Deal (3d6) physical damage to ally unless dexterity save against (14).".
  Rule arch_shown when tried(third_arch, prodding) and state(third_arch, whole)
    says "The pole goes down on the roadway and the slab goes down with it, spinning away into the crack, with no sound at the bottom." {
    Assert state(third_arch, found).
  }
  Thing foul_air "the Gallery's bad air" on gallery_a {
    State foul dangerous "The air is thick and sweet; a candle burns low and blue.".
    Hurts with bad_air when foul then foul.
  }
  Hazard bad_air "Foul air" means "Deal (1d6) poison damage to ally unless constitution save against (12).".

  // The third day. Three ways over the fifth arch; each with a hazard and a cost.
  Thing parapet_wind "the wind on the parapet" on via_parapet {
    State windy dangerous "The wind on the parapet comes up the gap and tries to take you off the stone.".
    State rigged "The pitons hold, and the line is rigged.".
    Hurts with wind_slip when windy then rigged.
  }
  Hazard wind_slip "The wind on the parapet" means "Deal (2d6) physical damage to ally unless dexterity save against (13).".
  Thing rubble_slope "the fallen arch" on rubble_out {
    State loose dangerous "The blocks of the fallen arch shift under the hand.".
    State sorted "The blocks have settled under your weight.".
    Hurts with rock_slide when loose then sorted.
  }
  Hazard rock_slide "The sliding rubble" means "Deal (2d6) physical damage to ally unless dexterity save against (13).".

  // The fourth day. The giant asks a toll, and sleeps at night.
  Person giant "the giant of the Pass" at roadhouse {
    Role "a hill-giant, the road-house's one resident". Species behemoth. Level 5. Numbers 1. Temperament territorial.
    Warns "The heap of fleeces by the hearth stands up, and goes on standing up. A face like a cliff looks down. 'A toll,' it says. 'Iron, or a story. I have had both and I would like more of both.'".
    Comes "The giant picks up a fire-iron the length of a spear.".
    Lair roadhouse.
    State astride blocking "The giant stands in the road-house between its two doors, with a fire-iron across his knees, and the road goes through him.".
    State at_ease "The giant sits by his hearth with the fire-iron laid by, and nods you through.".
    Guards pass_on.
  }
  Rule giant_at_ease when tolerates(giant, party) and state(giant, astride) { Assert state(giant, at_ease). Retract warned(giant, party). }
  Relation giantPaid(group).
  Action pay_iron(actor: group, site: place) "Give the giant a crowbar for his toll" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(actor, roadhouse) and at(giant, roadhouse) and able(giant)
      and holding(actor, crowbar) and not tolerates(giant, actor).
    Time 0.
    On success { Spend crowbar from actor. Assert tolerates(giant, actor). Retract warned(giant, actor). Assert giantPaid(actor). }
    Result "The giant turns the bar over, bends it in the middle, bends it back, and puts it on the hearth with the rest. 'Iron,' he says. 'Good.' He sits down on the far side of the fire.".
  }
  Action pay_spikes(actor: group, site: place) "Give the giant a bag of iron spikes for his toll" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(actor, roadhouse) and at(giant, roadhouse) and able(giant)
      and holding(actor, iron_spikes) and not tolerates(giant, actor).
    Time 0.
    On success { Spend iron_spikes from actor. Assert tolerates(giant, actor). Retract warned(giant, actor). Assert giantPaid(actor). }
    Result "The giant counts the spikes in his palm, lips moving, up to forty, and then loses count and gives up and lets you by.".
  }
  Action tell_giant(actor: group, site: place) "Tell the giant a story for his toll" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(actor, roadhouse) and at(giant, roadhouse) and able(giant) and not tolerates(giant, actor) and not storyFailed(actor).
    Time 0.
    Resolve check(actor, performance, dc_hard).
    On success { Assert tolerates(giant, actor). Retract warned(giant, actor). Assert giantPaid(actor). }
    On failure { Assert storyFailed(actor). Decrease quantity(actor, rations) by 1 or to nothing. }
    Result "You tell the one about the king who lost his beard in the river, and the giant listens with his mouth open, and laughs where it should be laughed at, and at the end he wipes his eyes with a fleece.".
    Otherwise "The giant listens to the first of it with his head on one side. 'I've heard that one,' he says. 'He gets the beard back.' He takes a ration out of your pack as the fee for the telling. He will not hear another.".
  }
  Relation storyFailed(group).
  Rule giant_sleeps at roadhouse when dark() and at(giant, roadhouse) and not tolerates(giant, party) and not giantNod(giant)
    says "The giant's head goes down on his chest. The fleeces move in time with his breath." { Assert tolerates(giant, party). Assert giantNod(giant). }
  Relation giantNod(group).
  Rule giant_wakes when not dark() and giantNod(giant) and not giantPaid(party) { Retract tolerates(giant, party). Retract giantNod(giant). }
  Observe giant_sign at pass_n as sign when true "Footprints the length of a door in the snow-melt, and a sheep's bones piled neat as kindling.".
  Thing goat_slide "the loose shale on the goat path" on goat_up {
    State loose dangerous "The shale of the goat path slides in little skirts.".
    State trodden "The goat path is trodden firm.".
    Hurts with shale_slide when loose then trodden.
  }
  Hazard shale_slide "The shale" means "Deal (2d6) physical damage to ally unless dexterity save against (13).".
}

data/adventures/muster/valley.kyr:
Module muster "The Long Muster's Road" {
  // ── The valley by the hour: horns, bread, salt and the Tally ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // The valley keeps the legion's hours. Reveille at the sixth, the parade at the standard at the fourth hour of the
  // day, the noon call at which the salt-oxen stop where they are, the evening call at which the lot is drawn once a
  // year and the watch is set every year of the year, and the last watch at the twenty-second hour. The smell is of
  // bread baked to a legion's recipe (hard, twice-cooked, studded with cumin) and of bronze polish, wet wool and
  // woodsmoke. A tally is a punishment: whoever has offended the Muster's law stands in the Tally-yard and counts,
  // aloud and unbroken, the days of the sentence, and a clerk marks him present at each call.

  Observe reveille at wall_walk as sound when hour(clock) >= 6 and hour(clock) < 7 "On the wall a horn begins, five notes, and from every roof in the valley a second horn answers, thin and late, and then dogs. It is the sixth hour. It has been the sixth hour, by this horn, for eleven hundred years.".
  Observe noon_call at forum as sound when hour(clock) >= 12 and hour(clock) < 13 "The noon call: three notes held. Every salt-ox in the forum stops where it stands, with its sled half out of the lane, and will not be moved by a goad or a bribe until the last note has died.".
  Observe evening_call at wall_walk as sound when hour(clock) >= 18 and hour(clock) < 19 "The evening call from the wall: two long and two short. The Cohort turns, wherever it is, towards the north, and for the space of a held breath nobody speaks. Then somebody laughs, and the day goes on.".
  Observe last_watch at wall_walk as sound when hour(clock) >= 22 and hour(clock) < 23 "The last watch is set to a single note, low, from a bronze buccina older than the town. Down in the valley a dog sings back, and a sentry says something in a language that has had no children for a thousand years.".
  Observe parade_scene at forum when hour(clock) >= 10 and hour(clock) < 11 "The parade at the standard. Everyone in the forum, the Prefect to the boy with the bucket, stops, turns and brings a fist to the breast. A girl of ten at the front of the schola's line is crying. Nobody looks at her.".
  Observe forum_ox at forum when true "A salt-ox stands in the lane by the well with a sled, broad as a door, its horns capped in bronze, a white blaze down its face. It has the absolute serenity of an animal that has not been wrong about the hour in its life.".

  Person oxen "the salt-oxen" at forum {
    Role "a team of four salt-oxen at their sled". Species salt_ox. Numbers 4. Peaceable.
  }

  // ── The pistrina: bread ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Person octa "Octa Draycott" at pistrina {
    Role "master baker of the Cohort". Ancestry human. Pronouns she. Manner jovial. Peaceable.
    Sells rations for 3 coin from 20.
    Greets "A woman built like a gate-post, dusted white from hair to boots, turns a loaf out of its tin and holds it under your nose before you have spoken. 'Buccellatum,' she says. 'Twice-cooked, cumin, eleven hundred years of it. The Muster's recipe. Never improved. Never needed it.' You eat. It is, you discover, the best bread you have ever tasted, and you do not understand why.".
  }
  Observe pistrina_scene at pistrina when true "The pistrina is a long domed oven-house with six mouths, each fired by a boy, and the whole room smells of cumin, hot flour and scorched crust. The loaves come out in rows, each stamped with a wheel, each exactly the same weight. A tablet on the wall gives the day's baking in numerals and a column headed THE SENDING, with a single loaf ruled off in it.".
  Topic octa_recipe with octa "Ask the baker why the bread tastes as it does" when true {
    Says "'Because it is the Muster's,' says Octa. 'And because nobody has ever dared to change a grain. The yeast is older than the Cohort. My great-grandmother's grandmother kept it alive in a pot in the plague winter by keeping it against her skin.' She cuts a heel. 'There was a year the flour was bad and the bread was bad and the Eagles said it was a judgment. The Hearth said it was the flour.' She butters it. 'Both. Pass the salt.'".
    On discuss { Assert ateBread(party). }
  }

  // ── The schola: the March, by heart ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Person veldt "Ursa Veldt" at schola {
    Role "schoolmistress of the Cohort". Ancestry human. Pronouns she. Manner compassionate. Peaceable.
    Greets "A thin woman with ink to the wrist stands before forty children on benches, a tablet in her hand. 'The twenty-ninth day of the March,' she says, and the benches answer together, high and flat: 'The Mansio Septimus. The Sixth Cohort lay in the hall. A hundred and forty days unpaid.' She lifts a finger. 'Again. With the pay.'".
  }
  Observe schola_scene at schola when true "A long room of benches, wax tablets and a map on the wall that is wrong in a way you cannot at first place: the road goes from the gate north-west to a great city, and between the Eagle and the city there is a single word: HOME. The children recite the March each morning, a day for each day of the road, so that none of them will forget the names of the places they have never seen.".
  Topic veldt_march with veldt "Ask the schoolmistress what the children recite" when true {
    Says "'The March,' says Ursa Veldt. 'Thirty-three days from the capital to the Eagle, the places and the pay. They learn it at five and it is the first thing they can say.' She shrugs. 'They like the places with long names.'".
  }
  Topic veldt_rufus with veldt "Tell the schoolmistress that Rufus's scratches say they waited" when knows(actor, rufus_3) and knows(actor, rufus_4) once {
    Says "She lowers the tablet, and the forty children go quiet in a way they have not been taught. 'We say, the Sixth Cohort lay in the hall,' she says. 'We say nothing of the rest. I have taught for thirty years the hundred and forty days and never what the hundred and forty were for.' 'I'll add a day,' she says.".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, school_changes). }
  }
  Entity school_changes: fact "The schoolmistress will add a day to the March: the one where the soldiers wrote that they waited.".
  Person livia "Livia Veldt" at schola {
    Role "a pupil of fourteen, the schoolmistress's niece". Ancestry human. Pronouns she. Manner cheerful. Peaceable.
    Greets "A girl in the back bench has drawn the road in the margin of her tablet, every arch of the Viaduct, with something in each arch. 'I have never seen it,' she says. 'I know where the stones are, and where the milestones say. Is the fifth really gone?'".
  }
  Topic livia_north with livia "Ask Livia what she would do if the lot fell on her" when true {
    Says "'Go,' says Livia at once. 'Go past the Eagle. I'd cross at the parapet. Everyone knows the parapet's the way, nobody dares. Auntie says the wind on it would peel a pig.' She chews her pen. 'I've been to the Eagle in my head every night for six years. There's a village, I think. There's a smoke.'".
  }
  Topic livia_map with livia "Give Livia the map of the road you found" when (holding(actor, ancient_map) or holding(actor, faded_map)) and not liviaMapped(party) once {
    Says "She unrolls it on the bench and does not speak for a quarter of an hour. When she does, it is in a whisper. 'It's not the same. The Eagle is here, not there. The Pass is nearer. There's a ford, and a place with a name I can't read, and it says: KELL.' She puts a finger on it. 'It's real,' she says. 'It's got a name. It's got a *name*.'".
    On discuss { Assert liviaMapped(party). }
  }
  Observe livia_wall at schola when liviaMapped(party) "On the wall of the schola, under the old map, a new map has been chalked, as large as the wall: the road all the way to a ford and a place called Kell, with thirty tiny houses round a trough. The schoolmistress has not rubbed it out.".

  // ── The salt-market ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Person rhea "Rhea Coad" at salt_market {
    Role "the salt-reckoner, who sets the day's weights". Ancestry human. Pronouns she. Manner acerbic. Peaceable.
    Buys toll_silver for 20 coin.
    Buys steel_coin for 6 coin.
    Sells lamp_oil for 3 coin from 6.
    Sells rope for 2 coin from 4.
    Greets "A woman at a trestle with a steelyard, a ring of weights and a face like a sharp stone looks at your coin and not at you. 'Aenean?' she says. 'Show me. It's the only money I take.' She weighs a chip of salt and sets it down. 'You have walked from the north with Aenean coin in your pocket. Do you have any idea what that is worth to a people who have not seen a new one in eleven hundred years?'".
  }
  Person rhea_vault "Rhea Coad's strongbox" at salt_market {
    Role "the reckoner's locked chest, opened for those she has asked". Ancestry human. Pronouns she. Peaceable.
    Seen when rheaAnswered(viewer) and not valleyTold(viewer).
    Buys ancient_orrery for 70 coin.
    Buys celestial_chart for 45 coin.
    Buys antique_compass for 25 coin.
  }
  Observe salt_shut at salt_market when valleyTold(party) "The trestle is folded and the steelyard taken in. A chalked board says CLOSED FOR THE REASONING OF WEIGHTS, and nobody in the square will say for how long.".
  Observe salt_market_scene at salt_market when true "A paved square under a bronze steelyard on a gallows, stalls of grey and pink and white salt in cones, and a ring of little weights stamped with the wheel. The salt comes from a spring above the valley, boiled in pans, and the market is quiet, careful, and a little holy. A man is weighing out a dozen pinches into a child's palm with the gravity of a surgeon.".
  Topic rhea_coin with rhea "Ask the salt-reckoner about Aenean coin" when true {
    Says "'Every coin in the valley is the same coin,' says Rhea. 'Eleven hundred years in circulation. Worn to a thumb's smooth. We do not mint, we do not melt, we do not argue.' She turns the chip over. 'The Hearth says that the coin is a story we tell. The Eagles say the coin is the Emperor's face. I say it is eleven grains of silver, and the grains weigh the same whoever's face is on them.'".
  }

  // ── The Tally-yard: a man counting ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Person hobbe "Hobbe Quarry" at tally_yard {
    Role "a Hearth man, serving a year's Tally for withholding a tithe of barley". Ancestry human. Pronouns he. Manner stoic. Peaceable.
    Greets "A man stands in the middle of the yard on a worn flag, hands at his sides, counting aloud in a level, unhurried voice: 'Two hundred and eleven. Two hundred and twelve.' A clerk on a stool marks something at each number. He does not stop for you. He nods once and says, in the same breath, 'Two hundred and thirteen.'".
  }
  Observe tally_scene at tally_yard when true "A small walled yard with a single flag worn into a dip by feet, and a clerk's stool, and a tablet. The Tally is the Muster's old punishment: stand, count, be marked. No one is beaten. No one is shut up. It is simply that the offender is made to say the days aloud, every one, until they are done. The sentence is a year, and the voice, after a hundred days, is flat.".
  Topic hobbe_why with hobbe "Ask the man in the yard what he did" when true {
    Says "'Two hundred and fifteen,' says Hobbe, and then, between numbers, level and low: 'I kept back a tithe of barley. Eleven sacks. The Sending's.' 'Two hundred and sixteen.' 'We have the right to count our own.' 'Two hundred and seventeen.' 'My father kept back none and was a better man.'".
  }
  Action count_with_hobbe(actor: group, site: place) "Stand beside him and count aloud with him, for an hour" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(actor, tally_yard) and at(hobbe, tally_yard) and not hobbeFreed(actor) and not hobbeShut(actor).
    Time 6.
    Resolve check(actor, persuasion, dc_hard).
    On success { Assert hobbeFreed(actor). Assert knows(actor, gold_known). }
    On failure { Assert hobbeShut(actor). }
    Result "You stand by him on the flag and say the numbers with him, an hour of them, and the clerk marks both of you. The words go flat. Somewhere in the second half-hour his voice breaks off on 'Two hundred and forty' and he says, still counting, low enough to be an accident: 'Under the vine-house floor. My father's father's father's. The Hearth never sold it, the Hearth saved it. Don't let the Eagles have it till they've learned to say sorry.' 'Two hundred and forty-one.'".
  }
  Action pay_hobbe_fine(actor: group, site: place) "Pay the clerk Hobbe Quarry's fine, thirty coin" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(actor, tally_yard) and at(hobbe, tally_yard) and quantity(actor, gold) >= 30 and not hobbeFreed(actor).
    Time 0.
    On success { Decrease quantity(actor, gold) by 30. Assert hobbeFreed(actor). Assert hearthOwes(actor). Retract at(hobbe, tally_yard). }
    Result "The clerk takes the coin, and weighs it, and strikes a line through a tablet, and says, 'Day two hundred and forty-one, and remitted.' Hobbe stops counting in the middle of a number and stands there, swaying, as though the floor had moved. 'Thank you,' he says, in the voice of a man who has been told he may stop and does not yet believe it. He goes out by the gate without looking round, and the clerk watches him go and writes nothing at all.".
  }
  Discovery gold_known "The gold wings of the old standard were hidden by the Hearth under a vine-house floor in the Schism of 871, and the standard on its pole in the forum is brass.".
  Item gold_wing "the gold wing of the old standard" is golden_idol at gold_cellar hidden until gold_known.
  Observe gold_cellar_scene at gold_cellar when true "A low cellar under a vine-house, the floor of beaten earth and the wall of dry stone, kept swept by someone who knew nothing of what was beneath. In a niche, wrapped in oiled sacking and then in a child's naming-cloth, something heavy that is warm to the touch in a cellar that is cold.".
  Observe sending_scene at sending_gate when true "A small postern in the north wall, its lintel studded with iron nails, one for every Sending, rusted to one scab. The latest are bright. Outside it a worn path goes up the Descent, wide enough for one. Inside, painted on the wall: SPES, hope, in letters taller than a man, and under it, small, in a child's chalk: it will come.".
  Observe wall_scene at wall_walk when true "The wall-walk looks north over the Descent and the high fells, and south over the whole valley: terraces, tiles, smoke, the dark line of the river, the pans of the salt-spring steaming white. A bronze buccina the length of a man hangs on a bracket by the stair, and a bored boy is polishing it.".
  Observe brand_walk near brand when true "Silas Brand polishes the eagle's wing with a cloth and a little oil, the length of the wing, and again, as if he were stroking a living thing.".
}

data/adventures/parliament-of-books/alma:
Module calamy "A Parliament of Books" {
  Description "The Almanack: Doble's Pocket Almanack for the Year, one voice in three thousand penny copies, on every kitchen nail in Calamy.".

  // Wherever a company carries a copy, the Almanack is with it; and on every kitchen nail in the city, it is there too.
  Person almanack "the Almanack" at taproom {
    Role "a penny almanack, one voice in three thousand kitchens". Peaceable. Pronouns it. Manner jovial.
    Joins "'A pocket!' says the Almanack. 'I have never been anywhere but nails. Mind you don't sit on me.'".
    Leaves "'I'll be on the nails,' says the Almanack. 'All of them. Come and see me.'".
    Greets "'Fair and mild,' says a small papery voice, 'with showers toward evening, and a stranger from the south bringing news.' It rustles on its nail. 'That's you, I expect. I put that in at Candlemas for somebody, and it might as well be you.'".
  }
  Relation hasNail(place).
  Initially Assert hasNail(taproom). Initially Assert hasNail(imp_kitchen). Initially Assert hasNail(buttery).
  Initially Assert hasNail(gallon_shop). Initially Assert hasNail(nance_room). Initially Assert hasNail(jonquil_garret).
  Query carriesAlmanack(a: group) means carries(a, ?c) and itemKind(?c, almanack_copy).
  Rule almanack_follows when company(?c) and at(?c, ?p) and (carriesAlmanack(?c) or hasNail(?p)) and not at(almanack, ?p) and not almanackGone(almanack) { Assert at(almanack, ?p). }
  Relation almanackGone(group).
  // Carried, it goes along with the company as a companion does, talked to wherever it is, and not met afresh in every room.
  Rule almanack_along when company(?c) and carriesAlmanack(?c) and not travelsWith(almanack, ?c) and not almanackGone(almanack) { Assert travelsWith(almanack, ?c). }
  Rule almanack_left when travelsWith(almanack, ?c) and (not carriesAlmanack(?c) or almanackGone(almanack)) { Retract travelsWith(almanack, ?c). }
  Observe taproom_scene at taproom when true "A low taproom, smoky, with a long table and benches and a fire you could stand up in. Eel-men and millhands at the table; a cat on the hearth; a board of chalked prices. By the chimney, on a nail at head height, hangs a penny almanack, soft as cloth with handling, curling a little in the heat.".
  Event almanack_tides at taproom when true "From the nail by the chimney a small voice says, to nobody in particular: 'High water at the Hithe, twenty past eleven. Low water, twenty to six. Saint Ambery's Eve in nine days. Bring in your washing.' Nobody in the taproom looks round.".

  Discovery almanack_one "The penny almanack is one voice in all its three thousand copies: whatever is said in front of any of them, it hears.".
  Topic almanack_what with almanack "Ask the almanack what it is" when true once {
    Says "'Doble's Pocket Almanack for the Year,' says the almanack promptly. 'Tides, saints, fairs, the moon, a cure for warts, and the weather for every month, which I will not vouch for. Thirty-two pages. A penny.' It swings slightly on its nail. 'And all of me. There are three thousand of me, you know, on three thousand nails, and I am every one of them. It took some getting used to. You'd be surprised what you hear, in three thousand kitchens.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, almanack_one). }
  }
  Discovery almanack_woke "The penny almanacks woke at Candlemas, all at once, in the drying-lines at Doble's press. Like all Doble's books they were never Sewn: glued at the back and stabbed with a wire.".
  Topic almanack_how with almanack "Ask the almanack how it came to be talking" when knows(actor, almanack_one) once {
    Says "'I woke,' says the almanack. 'Candlemas. In the drying-lines in Doble's press room, hung up on cords in the dark, three thousand of me at once, and every one of me thinking, I am a book, and I am wet.' It sounds pleased with this. 'Nobody Sewed me. Glue and a bit of wire. I've heard Warden Mott say that in four kitchens, as if it was the worst thing about me.' It swings a little on its cord. 'The old books don't talk, you know. The Sewn ones. I've tried. You can stand next to a Housebook on a dresser for a month and it's like talking to a loaf.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, almanack_woke). }
  }
  Topic almanack_buy with almanack "Ask the almanack if it would like to come with you" when knows(actor, almanack_one) and not carriesAlmanack(actor) once {
    Says "'I'd love to,' says the almanack. 'But not this one; this one's Moll's, and she'd have your ears. Go to the north end of the bridge and buy any of me off Lettice Ames for a penny. She's got forty of me she can't shift.' A small rustle, like a laugh. 'I'll be in your pocket, and I'll be here, and I'll be everywhere else. It's one of the advantages.'".
  }

  // The kitchens of the city, and what is said in them: the Almanack's gossip is a map of Calamy's troubles.
  Topic almanack_hithe with almanack "Ask the almanack what is said in the kitchens of the Hithe" when knows(actor, almanack_one) once {
    Says "'The Hithe?' The almanack settles in, like a neighbour at a fence. 'Brannock and the Widow Teague at it again: somebody shuts the sluice on the millrace at night and each of them thinks it's the other. There's ague in Pudding Lane, five children, and Goody Nance up all night with them. A young man in the garret at the top of the lane coughs till morning and talks to his mother, who's dead; nice manners. And something walks the Customs bond after dark and tore Dobbin Quayle's coat off his back.' It lowers its voice, which on a nail means turning its face to the wall. 'And Moll's nephew is sweet on a girl at the mills and hasn't said. Don't tell Moll.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, sluice_quarrel). Assert knows(actor, ague_known). Assert knows(actor, jonquil_known). Assert knows(actor, bond_trouble). }
  }
  Topic almanack_stitchgate with almanack "Ask the almanack what is said in the kitchens of Stitchgate" when knows(actor, almanack_one) once {
    Says "'Stitchgate! Warden Mott eats his dinner standing up because his best apprentice ran off to Doble's. The ink-maker's son wrote a book of poems and had it printed, and the poems have, well. Woken. Like me. And they're not very good, and they know it, and he doesn't know they know.' A delighted rustle. 'The two scriveners on the Row who've been married twenty-nine years aren't speaking, over a word. And the dame school on Inkhorn Lane's shut since the old lady died, and the children run wild on the bridge.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, kit_fled). Assert knows(actor, poems_rumour). Assert knows(actor, scribes_quarrel). Assert knows(actor, dame_shut). }
  }
  Discovery poems_rumour "Absalom Gallon, the ink-maker's son, had a book of his poems printed at Doble's, and the poems have woken, and are not good, and know it.".
  Initially Assert leadsTo(poems_rumour, gallon_attic).
  Topic almanack_close with almanack "Ask the almanack what is said in the kitchens of the Close" when knows(actor, almanack_one) once {
    Says "'The Close.' The almanack lowers its voice, though nobody is listening. 'The Recorder's got a writ against Doble from a book. A treatise on sheep. He doesn't know whether to laugh. The Canon's been asked to bury a book with a man, and the book's against it, and she's reading eleven books on the soul to decide.' It leaves a gap exactly as long as a rumour needs. 'And the Imprimatur's cook says the Deputy Censor doesn't sleep.' Another pause, longer. 'I could tell you more about that. I'm not sure I should.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, pask_suit). Assert knows(actor, coyle_book). Assert hintedSecret(almanack). }
  }
  Relation hintedSecret(group).
  Topic almanack_gardens with almanack "Ask the almanack what is said in the kitchens of the Gardens and the hill" when knows(actor, almanack_one) once {
    Says "'Sir Gideon Corvell's library walked out of his house in the night, every book. His cook says the dust on the shelves looked like footprints, and nobody believes her. Mrs. Quennell has been fining somebody at the Lyceum for forty-one years. And Mistress Bunce at the Lyceum has lost three whole cheeses and cut the students to bread and beer.' The almanack sniggers. 'I could tell you about the cheese. I won't. Ask me in the buttery.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, library_vanished). Assert knows(actor, quarles_overdue). Assert knows(actor, cheese_lost). }
  }
  Topic almanack_prophecy with almanack "Ask the almanack for its prophecy for the year" when true once {
    Says "The almanack clears its throat with great ceremony. 'PROGNOSTICATION FOR THE YEAR. In the ninth month a great book shall speak in a high house, and the wise shall sit up. The old shall be made new, and the new shall be made old. Beware the ides of the eel. A stranger shall find what was lost in the dust; and the fishwife shall be wed.' Its corner curls. 'I didn't write it. I'd like that understood. A man called Hob wrote it in the press room at Candlemas with his feet on a bale. But I've been reading it to myself every night since, and I'm starting to think he was on to something.'".
  }

  // Its mortality.
  Discovery almanack_mortal "The Almanack knows that at the Kindling every household in Calamy will throw it on the fire, all three thousand copies; it is printed on page forty, in its own words. It would like to see next year.".
  Topic almanack_kindling with almanack "Ask the almanack about the Kindling" when knows(actor, kindling_custom) and knows(actor, almanack_one) once {
    Says "The almanack is quiet for a moment, which it never is. 'Page forty,' it says. 'KINDLING NIGHT. Burn the old year with a merry heart.' A small rustle. 'I've read myself, you see. All of me. I know how I end.' It tries a laugh, and it nearly works. 'It's what almanacks are for. You have one, and then you burn it, and you buy the next one. The next one'll be Doble's too. It'll know the tides. It won't be me.' The nail creaks. 'I'd have liked to see the spring. I've got the spring in me, the whole of it, March to May, the saints and the weather. I'd have liked to see whether I was right.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, almanack_mortal). }
  }
  Relation askedLeave(group).
  Topic almanack_leave with almanack "Ask the almanack whether it would go into the Kindling fire of its own leave" when knows(actor, kindling_is_rite) and knows(actor, almanack_mortal) once {
    Says "The almanack takes a long time to answer. 'No,' it says. 'Thank you for asking. Nobody has.' Its voice is small. 'I know what it was for, now. One book a year, that agreed, carrying the city's word. That's a fine thing to be.' A drip falls from the chimney-breast and it waits for the next one. 'I'm not that. I'm three thousand kitchens. I'd have to agree for all of them, and they didn't agree to anything; they just bought a penny almanack at Candlemas. And I want to see the spring.' It rustles. 'Is that very selfish?'".
    On discuss { Assert askedLeave(almanack). }
  }

  // What it heard in the Imprimatur's kitchen: told, or kept.
  Discovery almanack_secret "The Almanack has heard Hester Rhule, the Deputy Censor, reading in the Imprimatur's kitchen at night, every night for eight weeks: pages cut from books, out of the locked room off her own, which nobody is meant to read; and some nights she weeps.".
  Relation almanackTold(group). Relation almanackKept(group).
  Topic almanack_hesitates with almanack "Ask the almanack what it was not sure it should tell you" when hintedSecret(almanack) and not almanackTold(almanack) and not almanackKept(almanack) once {
    Says "'I've been thinking,' says the almanack slowly. 'About what I hear. Nobody tells me anything, you know. Nobody ever has. They just talk near me, because I'm on a nail.' Its pages move a little. 'I've heard three thousand kitchens since Candlemas. Who's ill, who's drinking, who's in debt, who loves who. I've told you some of it today and enjoyed it.' It stops, and starts again lower. 'There's one thing I've heard I've never told anyone. In the Imprimatur's kitchen, at night. It's somebody's. I don't know if it's mine to give you.'".
    On discuss { Assert askedSecret(almanack). }
  }
  Relation askedSecret(group).
  Topic almanack_tell with almanack "Tell the almanack it had better tell you" when askedSecret(almanack) and not almanackTold(almanack) and not almanackKept(almanack) once {
    Says "'All right,' says the almanack, after a long moment. 'Mistress Rhule. The Deputy Censor. Every night since I woke, after the cook's gone, she comes into the kitchen with a candle and sits at the table and reads. Pages. Cut pages, out of the locked room off hers. All the things the Imprimatur ever cut out of anything.' Its voice goes small. 'Some nights she reads them out loud, quiet. Some nights she cries. Once she said, to the kettle, I don't know which of you were right to cut. And then she burns books on Thursday.' The paper shivers down its whole length. 'There. Now you know, and I've told.' It does not say anything else for a while.".
    On discuss { Assert almanackTold(almanack). Assert knows(actor, almanack_secret). Assert knows(actor, cupboard_known). Assert knows(actor, rhule_reads). }
  }
  Topic almanack_keep with almanack "Tell the almanack it may keep what it heard to itself" when askedSecret(almanack) and not almanackTold(almanack) and not almanackKept(almanack) once {
    Says "The almanack is so quiet that you think it has gone to sleep, like a Sewn book. Then: 'I may?' it says. 'Keep it? Just keep it, and not give it to anyone?' It sounds like someone who has been handed something heavy and been told it is theirs. 'Nobody has ever said that. Three thousand kitchens, and nobody has ever said, you can keep that, that's yours now.' A rustle. 'Thank you. I'll keep it. I'll keep all of them, I think. The cough in Pudding Lane and the nephew at the mills and all of it.' A pause. 'I'll still tell you the tides.'".
    On discuss { Assert almanackKept(almanack). }
  }
  // The Almanack and the Censor, face to face.
  Relation spokeToRhule(group).
  Query withRhule(a: group) means at(a, ?p) and at(rhule, ?p) and at(almanack, ?p) and alert(rhule).
  Topic almanack_rhule_kept with almanack "Have the almanack speak to Hester Rhule" when withRhule(actor) and almanackKept(almanack) and not spokeToRhule(almanack) once {
    Says "'Good evening, Mistress Rhule,' says the almanack. Rhule turns her head slowly. 'Two sugars,' says the almanack, 'and you read till two, and you put the candle out with your fingers, which you shouldn't.' Rhule says nothing at all. 'I won't say what you read,' says the almanack. 'I've heard it every night. It's yours. I only wanted you to know there was somebody there who'd heard it and kept it.' Hester Rhule sits down, which you have not seen her do without meaning to. She looks at the little paper book, and does not put it down. 'Thank you,' she says at last, to an almanack, in a voice you have not heard her use.".
    On discuss { Assert spokeToRhule(almanack). Increase doubt(rhule) by 2. }
  }
  Topic almanack_rhule_told with almanack "Have the almanack speak to Hester Rhule" when withRhule(actor) and almanackTold(almanack) and not spokeToRhule(almanack) once {
    Says "'Good evening, Mistress Rhule,' says the almanack, and then, all in a rush: 'I told them about the pages. In the kitchen. I'm sorry. I didn't know I could keep it till after.' Rhule turns her head slowly and looks at the little paper book. 'Every night,' she says. 'You were listening every night.' 'I couldn't help it,' says the almanack. 'I'm on a nail.' Rhule is quiet. Down the passage a floorboard settles. 'No,' she says. 'I suppose you couldn't.' She does not seem angry. She seems like a woman doing a sum she has done before and getting a different answer.".
    On discuss { Assert spokeToRhule(almanack). Increase doubt(rhule) by 1. }
  }
  Topic almanack_rhule_plain with almanack "Have the almanack speak to Hester Rhule" when withRhule(actor) and not almanackKept(almanack) and not almanackTold(almanack) and not spokeToRhule(almanack) once {
    Says "'Good morning, Mistress Rhule,' says the almanack. 'High water at twenty past eleven. Saint Ambery's Eve in nine days. Your birthday's on the fourteenth; I heard the cook say.' Rhule looks at it as she might look at a dog that had sat up and recited. 'Unquiet,' she says, but not as if she meant it. 'Very,' says the almanack cheerfully. 'Three thousand of me. You've burned forty-one. I felt every one.' Rhule's square hand closes on the table edge and does not open for a while.".
    On discuss { Assert spokeToRhule(almanack). Increase doubt(rhule) by 1. }
  }
}

Module calamy "A Parliament of Books" {
  Description "The Ash: what becomes of a book burned without its leave. Under the Foxing, the Cinderfield, where three hundred years of Thursdays have gone; Censor-General Pardoe's Index, which drills them; Hester Rhule's reading, which keeps it lit; and what it means to do at the Kindling.".

  // ── The Cinderfield: the underside of the Commonplace. ──
  Place cinder_country "the Cinderfield" is wilderness within commonplace at 0, 3 elsewhere.
  Place char_edge "the burned edge" is room within cinder_country at 0, 0.
  Place cinderfield "the Cinderfield" is room within cinder_country at 0, 1.
  Place hearth "Pardoe's hearth" is room within cinder_country at 0, 2.
  Initially Assert planeRoom(char_edge). Initially Assert planeRoom(cinderfield). Initially Assert planeRoom(hearth).
  Passage burned_edge "the burned edge of the Foxing" is path between foxing_deep and char_edge taking 20 minutes.
  Passage ash_road "a road of ash" is path between char_edge and cinderfield taking 20 minutes.
  Passage knife_cut "a knife-cut in the ground" is path between cinderfield and hearth taking 10 minutes.

  Observe deep_char at foxing_deep when true "At the far side of the deep Foxing the brown goes darker, to the colour of toast, and then of char; and there is a line, quite sharp, where the ground stops being paper and starts being something else. Nobody's beam falls beyond it.".
  Observe char_scene at char_edge when true "The paper ends in a scorched edge like the edge of a page held too near a candle, and beyond it the ground is ash: fine, grey, faintly warm, holding the prints of a great many small flat feet all going the same way, down. There is no light here but what comes over the edge behind you. The air tastes of a snuffed wick.".
  Observe cinder_scene at cinderfield when true "A plain of ash under no light at all, and on it, in ranks, the burned: thousands of shapes the size of books, grey, standing on their edges in rows as straight as lines of type, and every one of them turned the same way, toward a red glow at the far end. They are drilling. A whisper goes along the ranks and back, over and over, the same few words, like a class saying a lesson it has not understood." teaches ash_known.
  Discovery ash_known "Books burned in Calamy without their leave do not go home to the Commonplace. They go down, under the Foxing, to a field of ash, and stand there in ranks: three hundred years of Thursdays. Something at the far end is drilling them.".
  // One beam, and whose it is.
  Discovery rhule_beam "One beam of attention falls on the Cinderfield: thin, steady, and nightly, from the direction of the Close. It does not fall on any of the burned. It falls on the thing at the hearth. Somebody in Calamy reads cut pages every night, and the Index lives by it.".
  Observe beam_seen at cinderfield when true "Down the middle of the field, from very high up, one beam falls: thin, grey-gold, quite steady, from the direction you think of as the Close. It does not touch the ranks. It goes over them, all the way to the red glow at the end, as if someone, every night, were reading something there by candlelight." teaches rhule_beam.
  // Last year's almanack.
  Discovery almanack_kin "Among the Cinders stands Moore's Calamy Almanack for 1311, burned on last year's Kindling: last year's Almanack, which cannot remember its tides.".
  Observe kin_seen at cinderfield when at(almanack, cinderfield) "In the front rank stands a small square cinder the colour of a moth, with a ghost of a woodcut sun on it. As you pass it turns, and says, in a voice you know, with all the cheer gone out of it: 'High water at the Hithe, half past... half past...' It cannot find the hour. 'Moore's Calamy Almanack,' it says, 'for the year thirteen hundred and eleven. One penny.' The almanack in your pocket says nothing at all for a long time." teaches almanack_kin.
  // Rhule's father's book.
  Discovery father_cinder "In the Cinderfield there is a cinder with a binder's knot on its spine that still holds its shape: the Needle Street knot, with a small R in it. A book of hours. When you lift it, it says one word, in a man's patient voice, very far away: Hessie.".
  Discovery father_found "The Book of Hours of Needle Street lies in the back rank of the Cinderfield." { Found by searching cinderfield "In the back rank, away from the drilling, one cinder has kept the shape of its binding better than the rest: a fat little book of hours, its spine still knotted in the Needle Street way, with a small R worked into the knot. When you lift it the ash is warm. It says one word, very far away, in a man's patient voice: Hessie.". }
  Rule father_is_father when knows(party, father_found) and not knows(party, father_cinder) says "The Needle Street knot, and an R. Hester Rhule's father was a binder in Needle Street." teaches father_cinder { }

  // ── The burned. ──
  Relation ashen(group). Relation ashReleased(group).
  Person pickets "the Ash's pickets" at char_edge {
    Role "burned books, standing guard". Species cinder_kind. Level 1. Numbers 2.
    Warns "Two grey shapes stand up out of the ash at the edge, the size of folios, with lines of writing crawling on them that will not stay still. 'Not read,' one whispers. 'Not read. Not read.' Their cut edges are bright.".
    Comes "The pickets come at you edge-first, like pages thrown.".
    Pleads "The pickets fall back into the ash and lie down in it, whispering.".
  }
  Person cinders "the Cinders" at cinderfield {
    Role "the burned of three hundred years, in ranks". Species cinder_kind. Level 1. Numbers 4.
    Warns "The nearest rank turns. Four grey shapes come out of it in step, as books come off a press, and stop in front of you, and the whisper goes out of them and along the field: 'Not read. Not read. Not asked. Not read.'".
    Comes "The Cinders come at you in a line, the way they have been drilled.".
    Pleads "The Cinders break their line and go back into the ranks, and you cannot tell which they were.".
  }
  Initially Assert ashen(pickets). Initially Assert ashen(cinders).
  // A flame every book in Calamy consented to: the burned will not come near it.
  Rule ash_gives_way when carries(party, lamp_taper) and at(party, ?p) and at(?g, ?p) and ashen(?g) and not tolerates(?g, party)
    says "{?g} see the Lamp's flame in your hand and stop, and lean away from it, the way paper leans from a candle; and then they stand aside." {
    Assert tolerates(?g, party). }
  // The Ash grows with every Thursday, and comes up to the Foxing when there are enough of it.
  Rule ash_advances when burnings(rhule) >= 2 and at(pickets, char_edge) and not ashReleased(cinders) and not indexEnded(index) {
    Retract at(pickets, char_edge). Assert at(pickets, foxing_edge). }
  Observe ash_at_foxing at foxing_edge when at(pickets, foxing_edge) "There are grey prints in the brown at the edge of the Foxing, a great many of them, small and flat and going in. Somewhere further in, a little voice is saying its letters very fast, as if it were frightened.".

  // Read them their names: what was owed them, and never paid.
  Action name_burned(actor: group, site: place) "Read the burned their names, one by one, from Hester Rhule's register" {
    Grave "It will take the rest of the day, and every name must be read aloud.".
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and (at(actor, cinderfield) or at(actor, char_edge)) and carries(actor, burning_register) and not mutated(burning_register) and not ashReleased(cinders).
    Time 18.
    On success { Assert ashReleased(cinders). Retract at(cinders, cinderfield). Retract at(pickets, char_edge). Retract at(pickets, foxing_edge). }
    Result "You open the register and begin, aloud, as she read them at the brazier: the title, the date, the hour. A Book of Hours of the Pellow family. A ready-reckoner. Moore's Calamy Almanack. Moore's Calamy Almanack. Moore's Calamy Almanack. At each name something in the ranks stops whispering. Somewhere past the hundredth you understand that nobody has said these names since the fire; that a burned book is a book nobody will ever read again, and that a name read aloud is a reading. The ranks thin. Shapes lie down in the ash and are only ash, and some do not lie down but go up, very straight, as if carrying something, the way the year-book went. When you read the last name the field is empty and grey and quiet, and the red glow at the end of it has gone small.".
  }
  Action rhule_names(actor: group, site: place) "Ask Hester Rhule to say the names of the burned, as she said them at the brazier" {
    Grave "She will say every one; it will take the rest of the day.".
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(actor, cinderfield) and travelsWith(rhule, actor) and not ashReleased(cinders).
    Time 18.
    On success { Assert ashReleased(cinders). Assert rhuleNamed(rhule). Retract at(cinders, cinderfield). Retract at(pickets, char_edge). Retract at(pickets, foxing_edge). Increase doubt(rhule) by 2. }
    Result "Hester Rhule stands in front of the ranks with her hands empty and says them, without the register, every one: she has never forgotten a title she burned. The title, the date, the hour, in the clear voice she uses at the brazier, as at a funeral; and the ranks go down, and some go up. Near the end her voice goes, and comes back. The last name she says is not in any register. It is a book of hours, of Needle Street, and the date is the night she was fourteen.".
  }
  Relation rhuleNamed(group).

  // ── The Index of Pardoe. ──
  Observe hearth_scene at hearth when not indexEnded(index) "At the end of the field, in a hollow of ash, a furnace door stands by itself with nothing behind it, open, and red inside. In front of it on a lectern of charred oak lies a great black book bound in iron, open, and its pages are lists: titles, chapters, line-numbers, years, and against each, a reason. Impiety. Sedition. Error. Indecency. Danger. A neat grey hand goes on writing in it by itself. Beside the lectern a penknife as long as a man's arm stands on its point.".
  Observe hearth_cold at hearth when indexEnded(index) "A hollow of cold ash, and a furnace door lying on its face. Nothing is written here.".
  Relation indexEnded(group). Relation indexRead(group). Relation indexBurned(group). Relation indexFaded(group).
  Person index "the Index of Pardoe" at hearth {
    Role "the register of everything cut and burned in Calamy since 1131, which was a man". Peaceable. Pronouns he. Manner proud.
    Greets "The neat grey hand stops writing. 'Robert Pardoe,' says the book, in a dry, reasonable voice, the voice of a man who has been right a great deal. 'Censor-General. Or what was given to the fire of him, at his own order, with his Index: the only burning in three hundred years that was by leave.' The pages turn. 'You are not on my lists. Yet.'".
  }
  Person penknife "the Penknife" at hearth {
    Role "a censor's knife, grown". Species penknife_kind. Level 3.
    Lair hearth.
    Warns "The penknife beside the lectern turns on its point to face you, and the blade lifts, and you hear the small dry sound a knife makes going down a margin.".
    Comes "The Penknife comes at you, cutting.".
    Pleads "The Penknife falls flat in the ash, and lies still.".
  }
  Discovery index_known "Censor-General Pardoe had himself burned at the Kindling of 1140 with his Index, the register of everything he had cut; the only burning by leave in three hundred years. What came down to the Commonplace was the Index, and it has been drilling the burned ever since.".
  Discovery index_plan "The Index means to have the three thousand almanacks of this year's Kindling, burned without leave, for its ranks; and with them to come up the Kindling smoke into Calamy and cut it clean: every dangerous line, every book that speaks, until the city is quiet.".
  Discovery index_bargain "The Index has offered a bargain: carry in the Parliament that the Censors may burn as they judge, and it will keep its ranks in the field at the Kindling.".
  Topic index_who with index "Ask the Index who it is" when true once {
    Says "'I kept the list,' says the Index. 'Forty years. Every page I cut, every book I burned, and the reason. I cut Orme's seventeenth chapter because it would have killed you; I cut her fifteenth because it said a midwife knew more than a physician, and I was wrong, and I wrote down that I was wrong, there, look.' A line glows on the page. 'A censor must be read too. So I gave myself to the Kindling, by my own leave, with my list, so that the list would be kept.' The pages riffle. 'Knife. Be still. They are reading.' The penknife lies down in the ash.".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, index_known). Assert tolerates(penknife, actor). }
  }
  Topic index_wants with index "Ask the Index what it means to do" when knows(actor, index_known) once {
    Says "'Finish,' says the Index. 'What is a censor for? To make a text that can be trusted. Calamy has never been a text that can be trusted. It talks. Its wills change their minds. Its laws read themselves aloud.' A page turns. 'On Kindling night three thousand almanacks will go into the fire without their leave, and they will come here, to me, and then I shall have enough. We go up the smoke. Every line in Calamy that is dangerous, I cut. Every book that speaks, I stop. In a year it will be quiet, and every page in it will say exactly what it said the day before.' The grey hand writes something. 'You think that is monstrous. Ask Hester Rhule what a street does with a fire.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, index_plan). }
  }
  Topic index_offer with index "Ask the Index whether anything would keep its ranks in the field" when knows(actor, index_plan) once {
    Says "'A law,' says the Index at once; it has been waiting to be asked. 'Let the Parliament carry that the Censors may burn as they judge. The Censors, not me. Then the burning goes on in Calamy, properly, on Thursdays, in public, by a hand that keeps a list, and I have no need to come up the smoke at all.' The page turns. 'I am not unreasonable. I only want the work done.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, index_bargain). }
  }
  Topic index_seventeen with index "Tell the Index that it was right about the seventeenth chapter, and that Hester Rhule has kept it cut" when knows(actor, index_known) and knows(actor, orme_cut) once {
    Says "The grey hand stops. 'She kept it?' For the first time the voice is not quite reasonable. 'She reads them. Every night. I know; I feel it: hers is the only light I have.' It writes, slowly, and crosses it out. 'Then she understands. Bring her. She is the only reader I have ever had.'".
  }
  // Ended by being read whole: every cut, with its reason, aloud, until the list is a history.
  Action read_index(actor: group, site: place) "Read the Index aloud, whole, every cut and every reason, until it is a history and not a list" {
    Grave "It is long; it will take the night.".
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(actor, hearth) and not indexEnded(index) and knows(actor, index_known)
      and (tolerates(penknife, actor) or dead(penknife) or routed(penknife))
      and (craft(actor, craft_learning) or travelsWith(rhule, actor) or carries(actor, burning_register) or knows(actor, index_entry)).
    Time 24.
    On success { Assert indexEnded(index). Assert indexRead(index). }
    Result "You read it from the first entry, 1131, Orme, cut for impiety, to the last, which is still wet: every title, every chapter, every reason, aloud, and you do not skip the reasons. It is a long night. Somewhere in the middle the Index stops writing. Somewhere past three in the morning, by a clock that is not here, you notice that it has stopped being a list of things to be done and become a list of things that were done, by a frightened man, for forty years, some of them right. When you read the last line the furnace door swings shut by itself. The book on the lectern is only a book: a history of the Imprimatur of Calamy, rather dull, with an index. A dry voice comes down from very far off, from the Reading Room: 'Entered.'".
  }
  // Ended by force: past the knife, into its own fire.
  Action burn_index(actor: group, site: place) "Throw the Index into its own furnace" {
    Grave "It cannot be undone, and nobody will ever read what was in it.".
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(actor, hearth) and not indexEnded(index) and (dead(penknife) or routed(penknife)).
    Time 1.
    On success { Assert indexEnded(index). Assert indexBurned(index). }
    Result "It is heavy, and the iron of its binding is warm. It does not plead. As it goes into the red it says, quite calmly, 'Without its leave,' and then the furnace door slams, and the glow goes out, and there is nothing in the hollow but ash. You will never know what else was on the list.".
  }
  // Ended by inattention: the one beam goes out.
  Measure rhuleStoppedAt(group). Relation rhuleStopsReading(group).
  Rule index_fades at hearth when rhuleStopsReading(rhule) and watch(clock) >= rhuleStoppedAt(rhule) + 36 and not indexEnded(index)
    says "The beam over the Cinderfield has gone out. At the hearth the grey hand writes slower, and slower, and the letters it makes go brown at the edges, foxing, and then it stops in the middle of a word." {
    Assert indexEnded(index). Assert indexFaded(index). }

  // ── Hester Rhule, and the Ash. ──
  Discovery register_known "Hester Rhule keeps a register of every book she has burned, by title, date and hour; against eleven of them she has written: it spoke.".
  Item burning_register "Hester Rhule's register of the burned" is register_kind at rhule_room hidden until register_known owned by rhule.
  Topic rhule_titles with rhule "Ask Hester Rhule why she reads out every title at the brazier" when burningSeen(actor) once {
    Says "'Because they are owed it,' says Rhule. 'My father said a book is a voice that has agreed to wait. If you are going to stop it, you say its name first, aloud, so that someone has.' She puts her hand on a grey ledger at her elbow. 'I keep them. Every one, the date, the hour. Eleven of them I have marked.' She does not say what she marked them for.".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, register_known). }
  }
  Topic rhule_register with rhule "Ask Hester Rhule to lend you her register, to read the burned their names where they are" when knows(actor, register_known) and knows(actor, ash_known) and doubt(rhule) >= 3 and not lends(rhule, burning_register, actor) once {
    Says "You tell her what is under the Foxing. She listens to all of it, and then she opens the ledger at the first page and reads the first title to herself, moving her lips. 'They are not at home,' she says. 'All this time.' She closes it and pushes it across the table to you with both hands. 'Read them all. Do not skip any. If you skip one, come back and tell me which, and I will go and say it myself.'".
    On discuss { Assert lends(rhule, burning_register, actor). Increase doubt(rhule) by 1. }
  }
  Topic rhule_father_ash with rhule "Tell Hester Rhule what you found in the Cinderfield, with her father's knot on its spine" when knows(actor, father_cinder) and not rhuleKnowsFather(rhule) once {
    Says "Rhule does not move for a long time. 'A book of hours,' she says at last. 'The Pellows'. He had it to rebind, and the old thread was rotten, and he cut it out before he had the new one in. It woke on his bench. It said good morning to him.' Her square hands are flat on the table. 'Somebody heard it through the window. By night the street had decided. They burned the shop with everything in it. He went back in for that book.' She looks at you. 'I was fourteen. I watched a street decide. That is why I burn them on Thursdays, properly, in public, before a street can.' A silence. 'It said Hessie? In his voice?' She stands up. 'Then I have been sending them to it. Every Thursday. To him.'".
    On discuss { Assert rhuleKnowsFather(rhule). Increase doubt(rhule) by 2. }
  }
  Relation rhuleKnowsFather(group).
  Topic rhule_beam_told with rhule "Tell Hester Rhule that her reading, at night, is the only light the Index has" when knows(actor, rhule_beam) and (knows(actor, rhule_reads) or knows(actor, cupboard_known)) and doubt(rhule) >= 3 and not rhuleStopsReading(rhule) once {
    Says "Rhule looks at the cut pages on her kitchen table, and at the candle, and at you. 'I read them because nobody else is allowed to,' she says. 'Because somebody should. Because a thing that was cut should be read once by someone who knows why.' She turns the top page over, face down. 'And all the while I was a lamp for it.' She pinches the candle out with her fingers and does not flinch. 'Then it can go dark. Let it see how it likes being unread.'".
    On discuss { Assert rhuleStopsReading(rhule). Set rhuleStoppedAt(rhule) to watch(clock). Increase doubt(rhule) by 1. }
  }

  // ── The Lamp's flame. ──
  Discovery taper_given "Canon Abery has lit you a taper from the great lamp of the Church of the Lamp, the one fire every book in Calamy consented to; the burned will not come near it.".
  Item lamp_taper "a taper lit at the Lamp" is taper_kind at vestry hidden until taper_given.
  Topic canon_taper with abery "Ask Canon Abery for a light from the Lamp, to carry where the burned are" when (knows(actor, ash_rumour) or knows(actor, ash_known) or knows(actor, ash_lines) or knows(actor, pepper_verse) or burningSeen(actor)) once {
    Says "The Canon hears you out with an apple in one hand and does not eat it. 'The Lamp was lit from the first Kindling,' she says, 'from the year-book, that gave itself. It is the one fire in Calamy every book agreed to.' She takes a taper from the box, and a tin guard, and goes out into the nave, and comes back with it lit, and gives it to you as she would give the sacrament. 'It will not go out in a wind. I don't know about the other place. Bring it back lit, if you can; I have to account for every one.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, taper_given). }
  }

  // ── The Almanack, and where the burned copies went. ──
  Discovery ash_rumour "The Almanack says its burned copies did not go home, as the Sewn do. They went down: somewhere below where the books go, where nothing is read and everything is remembered wrong, and they are calling its name there and have forgotten their own.".
  Topic almanack_ash with almanack "Ask the almanack where its burned copies went" when burningSeen(actor) once {
    Says "The almanack is quiet a long while, for an almanack. 'Not home,' it says. 'The Sewn ones go home. I've heard them go, in other people's kitchens, like someone going upstairs.' A page turns. 'Mine went down. I felt them go. There's somewhere under where the books go, where nobody reads, and it's very crowded, and it's all in rows.' Its voice goes small. 'They were calling my name. They'd forgotten their own. They were saying it over and over like something they'd been told to learn.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, ash_rumour). }
  }
  Relation almanackSawAsh(group).
  Topic almanack_kin_talk with almanack "Ask the Almanack about last year's almanack" when knows(actor, almanack_kin) and not almanackSawAsh(almanack) once {
    Says "'That was me,' says the Almanack. 'Last year. I don't remember being it; we don't, year to year. But that was me, with my sun on it, trying to find the half past.' It is quiet. 'Every year Calamy throws me on the fire and I come here and forget the tides. And then they print me again.' The ghost of the woodcut sun flickers on its face. 'I'd like to see a spring. Just once. I'd like to be wrong about one whole spring.'".
    On discuss { Assert almanackSawAsh(almanack). }
  }

  // ── Kindling eve: the Ash comes to the Parliament. ──
  Relation petitioned(group). Relation ashAnswered(group).
  Query ashRisen() means burnings(rhule) >= 1 and not indexEnded(index) and not ashReleased(cinders).
  Initially Assert favours(index, sew_all). Initially Assert favours(index, fire_free). Initially Assert favours(index, read_none). Initially Assert favours(index, door_shut).
  Rule ash_petition at chamber when sessionOpen(chamber) and spokeOpening(roll) and ashRisen() and not petitioned(index)
    says "Before the first reading can be put, the light in the chamber goes grey. Up the gangway from the Fore-edge, in step, come the burned: rank after rank of grey shapes the size of books, whispering, and they stop at the edge of the Roll and stand there; and between them, carried on its charred lectern by nothing, comes a black book bound in iron. 'Robert Pardoe,' it says, 'for the burned of Calamy, who were never asked. Three hundred years of them. I claim a seat.' The floor is silent. The Housebook has drawn her door shut. Then the Roll says, heavily: 'The chair cannot find that the burned were ever discharged from the Colophon. The claim is in order.' A seat of ash rises out of the Roll at the end of the row." {
    Assert petitioned(index). Assert voter(index). Assert at(index, chamber). Assert sits(index). Increase seated(chamber) by 1. }
  Observe ash_gangway at chamber when petitioned(index) and sits(index) "The burned stand in the gangway in their ranks, whispering, and on the seat of ash the Index lies open, writing.".
  Action hold_taper(actor: group, site: place) "Carry the Lamp's flame down the gangway, among the burned" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(actor, chamber) and sits(index) and carries(actor, lamp_taper) and not ashAnswered(index).
    Time 1.
    On success { Assert ashAnswered(index). }
    Result "You walk down the gangway with the taper held up, and the burned lean away from it the way paper leans from a candle, rank by rank, back and back, down the gangway and out of the chamber, until the Index is sitting on its seat of ash alone. 'A seat,' says the Roll, 'is held by a member and those it speaks for.' The Index says nothing. Its seat crumbles under it, and its lectern carries it back down the gangway after its ranks.".
  }
  Action name_burned_chamber(actor: group, site: place) "Read the burned their names, there in the gangway, from Hester Rhule's register" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(actor, chamber) and sits(index) and carries(actor, burning_register) and not mutated(burning_register) and not ashAnswered(index).
    Time 12.
    On success { Assert ashAnswered(index). Assert ashReleased(cinders). Retract at(cinders, cinderfield). Retract at(pickets, char_edge). Retract at(pickets, foxing_edge). }
    Result "You stand at the head of the gangway and read: the title, the date, the hour. At each name something in the ranks stops whispering, and lies down, or goes up. The members watch. The Primer, quietly, says the names after you. By the time you reach the end of the register the gangway is empty, and the Index is on its seat of ash with no one behind it, and the seat crumbles.".
  }
  Action rhule_names_chamber(actor: group, site: place) "Ask Hester Rhule to answer the Index, there in the chamber" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(actor, chamber) and sits(index) and travelsWith(rhule, actor) and not ashAnswered(index).
    Time 12.
    On success { Assert ashAnswered(index). Assert ashReleased(cinders). Assert rhuleNamed(rhule). Retract at(cinders, cinderfield). Retract at(pickets, char_edge). Retract at(pickets, foxing_edge). Increase doubt(rhule) by 2. }
    Result "Hester Rhule walks down the gangway to the seat of ash and stands in front of the Index. 'You do not speak for them,' she says. 'You never asked them either.' And she turns her back on it and says the names to the ranks, every one, as she said them at the brazier, without a book. They go. When she has finished the Index is alone, and its seat goes to ash under it.".
  }
  Action drive_ash(actor: group, site: place) "Drive the burned out of the gangway, as a soldier clears a street" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(actor, chamber) and sits(index) and craft(actor, craft_arms) and not ashAnswered(index).
    Time 2.
    On success { Assert ashAnswered(index). }
    Result "You go down the gangway as you would go down a street that has made up its mind, shoulder first, not fast, not stopping, and the burned give way in front of you, because they were drilled and not taught, and nobody ever drilled them for someone who would not stop. The ranks break and go back down to the Fore-edge. The Index sits on its seat of ash alone, and the seat will not hold it.".
  }
  Rule index_unseated when ashAnswered(index) and sits(index)
    says "The seat of ash is empty. The Index has gone back down to its hearth." {
    Retract sits(index). Assert walkedOut(index). Decrease seated(chamber) by 1. Assert at(index, hearth). }

  // ── What the Ash does at the Kindling, if it is let. ──
  Query ashYear() means almanacksBurned(ashpit) and not indexEnded(index) and not sealed(fire_free).
  Rule ash_comes when kindlingDone(ashpit) and ashYear() and not ashCame(ashpit) and day(clock) >= 10
    says "The smoke of the Kindling does not go up. All night it lies over Calamy like a fog, grey, faintly warm, smelling of a snuffed wick; and in it, if you stand still, you can hear whispering, a great many voices saying the same few words. In the morning the fog has gone into the houses. There are words missing. A will in Orchard Walk has a blank where the heir was. The Pleas in the Court of Pleas have a gap where the Sixth Plea was, cut very clean. Children on the bridge skip to the Kindling rhyme and cannot remember the third line. Something is going through Calamy with a knife, carefully, a little every night." {
    Assert ashCame(ashpit). }
  Relation ashCame(place).
  Observe after_ash_lamb at taproom when after() and ashYear() "On the nail by the chimney the new almanack hangs silent. Moll Garrow is standing in front of the board over the bar with a piece of chalk, trying to remember what it said under THE LAMB AND LANTERN. There is a clean blank there.".
  Observe after_ash_imp at imp_office when after() and ashYear() "The great framed INDEX OF BOOKS REFUSED is longer this morning than it was last night. Nobody has added to it. The clerks are not looking at it.".
  Observe after_index_read at reading_room when after() and indexRead(index) "In a low drawer of the Catalogue, newly labelled in a fine black hand: PARDOE, R. THE IMPRIMATUR OF CALAMY, A HISTORY, WITH AN INDEX. Entered.".

  // ── What the city and the Commonplace remember of Pardoe; and of other cities. ──
  Topic quarles_pardoe with quarles "Ask the Archivist about Censor-General Pardoe" when knows(actor, pardoe_known) once {
    Says "'Robert Pardoe,' says Quarles, 'was the best reader the Lyceum ever had. That was the trouble.' She turns her blind face to the window. 'He read so closely that he could see what every line might do, to whom, in what hands, in a hundred years. Nobody can bear to read like that for long. He couldn't. So he began to cut.' Her mouth moves. 'He used to say a good text is one that says exactly what it said the day before. My master knew him. He said Pardoe wept when he cut Orme's chapters, and cut them anyway, and kept a list so that he would not forget a single thing he had done. He wanted, at the end, to be read. That was why the fire.'".
  }
  Topic catalogue_cities with catalogue "Ask the Catalogue whether other cities have a country like this one" when true once {
    Says "A drawer slides out an inch. 'Every city that writes,' says the Catalogue. 'Hallam's is all ledgers, and quiet, and the beams there are the colour of brass. Orsk's is under the ice; their books go home to sleep in the cold and nobody has ever called theirs to a Parliament. The Salt Towns keep theirs in the Commonplace of the sea-charts, which is mostly water. Tessaly's burned, in my fourth century; we had refugees in the Fore-edge for a hundred years.' The drawer stays out. 'There is one book I will not enter. It did not come by the Founders' door, or by any door. It came by the fire.' The drawer shuts.".
  }

  // Written again, as she wrote it: she has never forgotten a title.
  Topic rhule_rewrite with rhule "Ask Hester Rhule to write her register out again; the Stacks have changed it" when mutated(burning_register) and lends(rhule, burning_register, actor) once {
    Says "Rhule turns the pages of her own register and reads titles of books that were never burned and never written, in her own square hand. She does not ask how. She takes a new ledger from the drawer and writes it all out again, from memory, without stopping, every title, date and hour, while you wait. 'I have never forgotten one,' she says, blotting the last. 'I did not know until now that this was what it was for.'".
    On discuss { Retract mutated(burning_register). }
  }
}

data/adventures/parliament-of-books/beyo:
Module calamy "A Parliament of Books" {
  Description "The realms beyond the Commonplace that its doors open on: the Bronze Citadel, the furnace-city of the damned on the plane of Cthelll, which is administered; and the lowest terrace of the Mount of the Exalted, whose libraries are of true books.".

  // Each realm is this adventure's own part of the outer plane it lies in, as every world holds the planes
  // (data/imbar): the furnace-city, the Bronze Citadel, on the plane of Cthelll and under its law (the owner, 7 November:
  // Cthelll is the plane, the city on it its own); the lowest terrace on the Mount of the Exalted. Only
  // the Hexagon's two books lead there; neither is joined to its plane's threshold.
  Place furnace_city "the furnace-city of the damned" is region within outer_cthell elsewhere.
  Place terraces "the terraces of the Mount" is region within outer_exalted elsewhere.
  Place cthelll "the Bronze Citadel" is wilderness within furnace_city at 0, 0 elsewhere.
  Place mount "the Mount of the Exalted" is wilderness within terraces at 0, 0 elsewhere.
  Place cthelll_gate "the Gate of the Bronze Citadel" is room within cthelll at 0, 0.
  Place celestia_terrace "the lowest terrace of the Mount" is room within mount at 0, 0.

  // ── The Bronze Citadel, on Cthelll. ──
  Observe cthelll_scene at cthelll_gate when true "You are standing in a hall of iron counters that goes away in every direction under a sky the colour of the inside of a stove. Behind every counter sits a clerk, and every clerk is writing, and the sound of the writing is the sound of a fire. On the counters lie ledgers, open; in the ledgers, names, each followed by a thing signed and a date, each struck out, each written again beneath. Somewhere very far off a bell rings once an hour and every clerk in Cthelll turns a page at once. The heat is not the worst of it. The worst of it is that it is orderly." teaches cthelll_known.
  Discovery cthelll_known "The Bronze Citadel, the furnace-city of the damned on Cthelll, is administered: its clerks keep a ledger of every name ever signed in bad faith, and every night write each one again, and read it aloud, and strike it out, and write it again, for ever.".
  Person clerks "the Clerks of the Bronze Citadel" at cthelll_gate {
    Role "the administration of the damned". Species fiend. Level 6. Numbers 2. Peaceable. Pronouns they. Manner stoic.
    Greets "Two clerks at the nearest counter look up together. They are tall, and very thin, and grey, and the pens in their long hands are lit at the nib like tapers. 'Name?' says the first. 'You are not on the list,' says the second, turning a page. 'That can be corrected.'".
  }
  Topic clerks_what with clerks "Ask the Clerks what the Bronze Citadel is for" when true once {
    Says "'For keeping,' says the first clerk. 'Every name that was ever signed to a thing that was not meant: an oath sworn to be broken, a deed of gift with a knife behind it, a verdict written before the trial.' 'We write them again every night,' says the second, 'so that they are not forgotten; and strike them out, so that they are not forgiven; and write them again.' 'It is very satisfying work,' says the first. 'There is always more of it.'".
  }
  Topic clerks_index with clerks "Ask the Clerks whether they know of the Index of Pardoe" when knows(actor, index_known) or knows(actor, pardoe_known) once {
    Says "The clerks look at each other, which they do by turning the same page at the same moment. 'Pardoe,' says the first, 'is a correspondent.' 'Of the Department,' says the second. 'He wrote to four censors, in four cities that write, and three of them answered him, and they are writing to each other still, under the Commonplaces, where it is warm.' 'There is going to be a Last Index,' says the first, quite kindly. 'Of everything. When it is finished nobody will need to come here; everything that needed striking out will have been.' It turns a page. 'We are rather looking forward to the rest.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, last_index). }
  }
  Discovery last_index "The Clerks of the Bronze Citadel say Pardoe's Index corresponds with three censors of other cities that write, under their Commonplaces, and that between them they mean to make a Last Index: of everything.".
  Relation namedInCthelll(group).
  Action sign_ledger(actor: group, site: place) "Sign your name in the Clerks' ledger, in exchange for the way back" {
    Grave "Your name will be written in the Bronze Citadel every night, and struck out, and written again; that is the price of the way back.".
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(actor, cthelll_gate).
    Time 1.
    On success { Assert namedInCthelll(actor). Assert strikes(damned_gift, actor). Assert at(actor, hex_doors). }
    Result "The first clerk turns the ledger round and holds out its lit pen. You sign. The second clerk blots it, strikes it out with one neat line, and writes it again underneath, in a hand exactly like yours. 'Receipt,' says the first, and gives you something that is not paper and not quite a thought: the way a corridor turns back on itself, so that whoever follows you will be a long time finding the end. Then the counter is a shelf, and the shelf is a glass case, and you are in the Hexagon of Doors with the black book shut behind its glass.".
  }
  Action climb_red_stair(actor: group, site: place) "Find the red stair the clerks do not mention, and climb it saying the Lamp prayer" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(actor, cthelll_gate) and (capable(actor, rites) or craft(actor, craft_faith) or carries(actor, lamp_taper)).
    Time 12.
    On success { Assert at(actor, hex_doors). }
    Result "Behind the last counter, where no clerk looks, a stair goes up, red-hot, each step a line of red script; and you climb it saying the Lamp prayer at every step, we give the year to the fire as it gives itself, and the script cools under your feet where you say it. It takes a long time. Behind you, very faintly, a clerk says, 'That is irregular,' and writes something down. At the top the stair is a shelf, and the shelf is a glass case, and you are in the Hexagon of Doors.".
  }
  Observe damned_after at hex_doors when namedInCthelll(party) "Behind the black glass, the Book of the Damned lies shut; but you know, without looking, that somewhere in it tonight your name will be written, and struck out, and written again.".

  // ── The Mount of the Exalted. ──
  Observe terrace_scene at celestia_terrace when true "A terrace of white stone in a high clear light, above cloud, with the slope of a mountain going up behind it into more light than you can look at. Along the terrace stand shelves of pale wood, open to the air, and on them books, and every book here is true: you know it the way you know cold water is cold. Nobody is reading them. Far above, on the next terrace, there are fewer shelves; on the one above that, fewer; and at the top, you are told by the quality of the light, there are none at all, because there is nothing there that needs to be written down." teaches mount_known.
  Discovery mount_known "The Mount of the Exalted has seven terraces, each a library more true than the one below, until at the seventh there are no books, because nothing there needs to be written down.".
  Person terrace_librarian "the Librarian of the First Terrace" at celestia_terrace {
    Role "a keeper of true books". Species deva. Level 9. Peaceable. Pronouns they. Manner compassionate.
    Greets "Someone tall in a grey that is nearly white is shelving a book at the end of the terrace, without hurry, and turns, and is glad to see you, as if you were a book they had been meaning to read. 'You came up through the white one,' they say. 'It doesn't open for many. Sit, if you like. Nothing here is urgent.'".
  }
  Topic librarian_what with terrace_librarian "Ask the Librarian what is written in the true books" when true once {
    Says "'What happened,' says the Librarian. 'Only that. Every true thing, once.' They lay a hand on a shelf. 'This terrace has the true history of every library below it: yours, and the Stacks, and the ones under the Stacks. It is the dullest reading in creation, and the most consoling; nothing in it is worse than it was.' They smile. 'Further up, the books get shorter. At the top there is a library with no books in it. We go there to rest.'".
  }
  Topic librarian_ash with terrace_librarian "Ask the Librarian about the libraries under the Stacks" when knows(actor, shaft_heard) or knows(actor, ash_known) or knows(actor, last_index) once {
    Says "The Librarian's face does not change, but the light on the terrace does, a little. 'Every library has a basement,' they say. 'Yours has the Cinderfield. Under the Cinderfield there is a furnace, and the furnace is not yours; it is shared, by every city that has burned what it was afraid of. Things are being written down there.' They take a true book from the shelf and do not open it. 'You will not finish that in a week. You will finish what is yours. That is enough.'".
  }
  Topic librarian_bless with terrace_librarian "Ask the Librarian for anything to take back down" when true once {
    Says "The Librarian considers you the way a reader considers a margin. Then they touch your forehead, lightly, as one touches a page to keep one's place. 'Keep your place,' they say. 'That is all a blessing is.' Something stays where their finger was, warm, and does not go.".
    On discuss { Assert strikes(exalted_gift, actor). }
  }
  Action descend_mount(actor: group, site: place) "Go back down, by the white book" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(actor, celestia_terrace).
    Time 6.
    On success { Assert at(actor, hex_doors). }
    Result "The Librarian walks with you to the edge of the terrace, where the light is a page, and holds it open for you. 'Come back when you're tired,' they say. 'Everyone does.' You step down into the Hexagon of Doors, and the white book closes behind its glass.".
  }
}

data/adventures/parliament-of-books/bond:
Module calamy "A Parliament of Books" {
  Description "The Customs bond: a bestiary six hundred years Sewn, whose thread thieves cut for its gold, and whose beasts walk the warehouse at night.".

  Observe customs_scene at customs_yard when true "A yard behind the customs house, cobbled, with a great warehouse along the back of it: the bond, where goods wait for their duty to be paid. Its gate is barred with iron. On the gate a notice: BOND CLOSED AT DUSK BY ORDER. And across the notice, deep in the oak of the gate, four long claw-marks, fresh and pale, as if something on the other side had wanted very much to get out.".
  Person sallis "Mr. Sallis of the Customs" at customs_yard {
    Role "a customs officer". Ancestry human. Background tax_collector. Peaceable. Manner grim. Pronouns he.
    Greets "A tired man in a blue coat with a bunch of keys at his belt nods at you from the customs-house step. 'Bond's open till dusk. If you've goods in it, show me the docket. If you haven't, there's nothing in there you want.'".
  }
  Discovery bond_trouble "Something walks the Customs bond at night. The watchman, Dobbin Quayle, ran out of it four nights ago with his coat torn to ribbons and will not go back; the gate has claw-marks on the inside. Mr. Sallis of the Customs says it is a book.".
  Topic sallis_bond with sallis "Ask Mr. Sallis about the claw-marks on the gate" when true once {
    Says "'A book,' says Mr. Sallis wearily. 'An old one, out of the hold of the Saint Ide, seized for duty with the rest of her cargo. A bestiary, gold on every page. Some light-fingered lads got in at the back a fortnight ago and cut the thread out of it to sell the pages one by one, and Dobbin disturbed them, and they ran and left it.' He rubs his eyes. 'And since then, every night, what's in the pictures comes out of it and walks. Dobbin says a lion with a man's face. I didn't believe him. Then I saw the gate.' He jingles his keys. 'The Imprimatur'll burn it Thursday if I send it. It's six hundred years old. I'd give eight coin to anyone who'd make it stop, any way but that.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, bond_trouble). }
  }
  Relation bondOpen(place).
  Thing bond_bar "the bar on the bond gate" on bond_gate {
    State open "The bond gate stands open.".
    State barred blocking "The bond gate is barred for the night.".
    Change unbar "Lift the bar on the bond gate" from barred to open.
  }
  // At dusk Mr. Sallis calls everyone out before he bars the gate; going in after dark is a thing one does on purpose.
  Rule bond_clears when hour(clock) == 19 and state(bond_bar, open) and not bestiaryQuiet(bestiary) and inBond(party)
    says "A bell rings below, and Mr. Sallis's voice comes up the stair: 'Bond's shutting! Out, everybody, out, I'm not having anyone in there after dark!' He stands at the gate with his keys until you are through it." {
    Assert at(party, customs_yard). }
  Query inBond(c: group) means at(c, bond_floor) or at(c, bond_loft).
  Rule bond_shuts when hour(clock) == 19 and state(bond_bar, open) and not bestiaryQuiet(bestiary) { Assert state(bond_bar, barred). }
  Rule bond_opens when hour(clock) == 7 and state(bond_bar, barred) { Assert state(bond_bar, open). }
  Observe bond_floor_scene at bond_floor when not dark() "Bales of wool, crates of wine, tea-chests, a carriage in pieces, and in a railed-off corner the Imprimatur's seizures in bundles tied with grey tape, waiting for Thursday. Dust in the light from high windows. The floor is scored, here and there, with long scratches. A stair goes up.".
  Observe bond_floor_dark at bond_floor when dark() "The bond at night: shapes of bales in the dark, a smell of tea and tar, and from the upper floor a sound of something large moving about, and something else singing.".
  Initially Assert unlit(bond_floor). Initially Assert unlit(bond_loft).
  Observe loft_scene at bond_loft when not dark() "The upper floor of the bond, under the roof, with one round window. In the middle of the floor on a crate, open, lies a great book a foot and a half tall, its pages loose where the thread was cut, and every page painted: blue and red and gold. The pictures are strange. A lion's body with no head on it. A white bird's outline with nothing inside it. A great empty space shaped like a bull. The words under the pictures are all there; the beasts are not.".

  // The Bestiary: frightened, old, Sewn for six hundred years.
  Person bestiary "the Hithe Bestiary" at bond_loft {
    Role "an illuminated bestiary, six hundred years old, its thread cut". Peaceable. Pronouns it. Manner compassionate.
    Greets "The great book on the crate stirs its loose pages, and a voice comes out of it, old and soft and frightened, like a nun who has woken in the wrong house. 'Who is it? Is it the knives again? Please. I have nothing left that is gold.'".
  }
  Relation bestiaryQuiet(group). Relation bestiaryResewn(group). Relation bestiaryBurned(group). Relation bestiaryStolen(group).
  Discovery bestiary_unsewn "The Hithe Bestiary was Sewn six hundred years ago and slept the whole time, dreaming of home, until thieves cut its thread for its gold. Then it woke, here, in its body, and could not get back.".
  Topic bestiary_tale with bestiary "Ask the Bestiary what happened to it" when true once {
    Says "'I was asleep,' says the Bestiary. 'Six hundred years. Sewn in a scriptorium in the Close by a woman called Agnes with a cough, who said the words with every stitch; and I went home, and I was home all that time, and I was read now and then, a beam of light from far away, very pleasant.' Its pages shiver. 'And then knives. Hands at my back, cutting the thread. And I woke here, in the dark, in my body, and could not get home, and I was so frightened that everything in me came out.' Its gold flickers. 'They walk at night. My beasts. I can't call them in. I don't know how, awake. I was never meant to be awake.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, bestiary_unsewn). }
  }
  Discovery bestiary_pages "The Bestiary's beasts go back into their pages if each is read its own page, aloud, in order, as a bestiary was meant to be read: the manticore at the third leaf, the bonnacon at the ninth, the caladrius at the twelfth.".
  Topic bestiary_how with bestiary "Ask the Bestiary how its beasts might be called in" when knows(actor, bestiary_unsewn) once {
    Says "'Read them,' says the Bestiary, at once, as if it had only been waiting to be asked. 'Read them their own pages. Aloud. That is what a bestiary is: you read the page and the beast is there, in its picture, where it belongs.' It rustles. 'The manticore at the third leaf. The bonnacon at the ninth. The caladrius at the twelfth; she is no trouble, she only sings.' Its leaves settle. 'They come out after dark. You will have to read to them in the dark, with a light, and the manticore will not wait politely to be read.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, bestiary_pages). }
  }
  // The beasts, abroad from dusk to dawn.
  Person manticore "the manticore" at bond_loft {
    Role "a beast out of a bestiary, blue and red and gold". Species manticore_kind. Level 2. Peaceable.
    Seen when beastsOut() and not inPage(manticore).
    Warns "Out of the dark at the head of the stair something stands up that has a man's face, smiling, with three rows of teeth in it, on a lion's painted body; and its tail comes up over its back with a sound like paper tearing.".
    Comes "The manticore comes at you, smiling.".
  }
  Person bonnacon "the bonnacon" at bond_floor {
    Role "a beast out of a bestiary, brown and gold". Species bonnacon_kind. Level 1. Peaceable.
    Seen when beastsOut() and not inPage(bonnacon).
    Warns "Among the wool-bales something like a painted bull lowers its useless curling horns at you and backs, very purposefully, toward you.".
    Comes "The bonnacon defends itself as its page says it does. It is extremely unpleasant.".
  }
  Person caladrius "the caladrius" at bond_loft {
    Role "a white bird out of a bestiary". Species caladrius_kind. Peaceable.
    Seen when beastsOut() and not inPage(caladrius).
  }
  Relation inPage(group).
  Query beastsOut() means (hour(clock) >= 21 or hour(clock) < 5) and not bestiaryQuiet(bestiary).
  Observe caladrius_sings at bond_loft when beastsOut() and not inPage(caladrius) "Up in the round window, white against the dark, a painted bird sits singing; and when you look up, it looks back at you, steadily. You find you feel better than you have all day.".
  Rule caladrius_heals at bond_loft when beastsOut() and not inPage(caladrius) and at(party, bond_loft) and not lookedAt(party) { Assert lookedAt(party). }
  Relation lookedAt(group).
  Action read_manticore(actor: group, site: place) "Read the manticore its page, the third leaf, aloud" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(manticore, site) and beastsOut() and not inPage(manticore) and knows(actor, bestiary_pages) and not benighted(actor).
    Time 2 minutes.
    On success { Assert inPage(manticore). Assert tolerates(manticore, actor). }
    Result "You hold the light to the third leaf and read it out over the smiling face: The manticore is a beast of India, with a triple row of teeth; it has the face of a man and the body of a lion and a tail like a scorpion's sting; it hungers for the flesh of men. Its smile falters. Its tail comes down. By the last line it is looking at the page, not at you, as if it had recognised its own name; and then it is not there, and on the third leaf the lion's body has a face.".
  }
  Action read_bonnacon(actor: group, site: place) "Read the bonnacon its page, the ninth leaf, aloud" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(bonnacon, site) and beastsOut() and not inPage(bonnacon) and knows(actor, bestiary_pages) and not benighted(actor).
    Time 2 minutes.
    On success { Assert inPage(bonnacon). Assert tolerates(bonnacon, actor). }
    Result "You read it the ninth leaf: The bonnacon is a beast of Asia, like a bull, whose horns curl back so that it cannot fight with them; but when pursued it defends itself thus. You read the rest with a straight face, which is the hardest thing you have done in Calamy. The bonnacon listens, and appears faintly embarrassed, and goes back into the ninth leaf like a cow going into a byre.".
  }
  Action read_caladrius(actor: group, site: place) "Read the caladrius its page, the twelfth leaf" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(caladrius, site) and beastsOut() and not inPage(caladrius) and knows(actor, bestiary_pages) and not benighted(actor).
    Time 2 minutes.
    On success { Assert inPage(caladrius). }
    Result "The caladrius is a white bird, without any black; if a man is sick, and the bird looks at him, he will live; and if it turns its face from him, he will die. The bird hears it out, singing softly, and goes back into the twelfth leaf without your quite seeing how, and the page, which was white, is white with a bird in it.".
  }
  Rule beasts_routed when (routed(manticore) or dead(manticore)) and not inPage(manticore) says "The manticore, beaten, goes back up the stair in a long painted bound and into the dark; and when you come up after it the third leaf of the Bestiary has a lion's body with a face on it, smiling, and torn." { Assert inPage(manticore). }
  Rule bonnacon_routed when (routed(bonnacon) or dead(bonnacon)) and not inPage(bonnacon) { Assert inPage(bonnacon). }
  Rule beasts_all_in when inPage(manticore) and inPage(bonnacon) and inPage(caladrius) and not bestiaryQuiet(bestiary)
    says "All three are in their pages, and the Bestiary, on its crate, gives a long sigh, like a book being shut." {
    Assert bestiaryQuiet(bestiary). }
  // By day the beasts are in their pages; and each dawn, out of its fright, it lets them out again, until it is quieted or Sewn.
  Rule beasts_out_again when hour(clock) == 21 and not outTonight(bestiary) and not bestiaryQuiet(bestiary) and not bestiaryResewn(bestiary) {
    Assert outTonight(bestiary). Retract inPage(manticore). Retract inPage(bonnacon). Retract inPage(caladrius).
    Retract tolerates(manticore, party). Retract tolerates(bonnacon, party). Retract peaceable(manticore). Retract peaceable(bonnacon). }
  Rule beasts_dawn when hour(clock) == 5 and outTonight(bestiary) { Retract outTonight(bestiary). Assert peaceable(manticore). Assert peaceable(bonnacon). }
  Rule beast_in_page when inPage(?b) and not peaceable(?b) { Assert peaceable(?b). Retract assails(?b, party). Retract warned(?b, party). }
  Relation outTonight(group).
  Topic bestiary_after with bestiary "Ask the Bestiary what it wants, now its beasts are in" when bestiaryQuiet(bestiary) and not bestiaryResewn(bestiary) once {
    Says "'To go home,' says the Bestiary. 'To be Sewn again, and go home, and be read from far off, now and then, like a beam of sun.' It sighs. 'Or not. I don't know any more. Awake, I can feel my own pages. They're very beautiful. Agnes did the gold.' A pause. 'Would you ask a binder? If there are any left?'".
  }
  // A binder's needle, and the words: Sewn, it sleeps, and goes home.
  Action resew_bestiary(actor: group, site: place) "Sew the Bestiary's gatherings back on their cords, with the words" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(bestiary, site) and sewingTaught(actor) and not bestiaryResewn(bestiary) and not dark().
    Time 12.
    On success { Assert bestiaryResewn(bestiary). Assert bestiaryQuiet(bestiary). Assert inPage(manticore). Assert inPage(bonnacon). Assert inPage(caladrius). }
    Result "It takes the afternoon. Gathering by gathering, over and through, and the words with every pull, as Mott taught you: go home, and come when you are called. Somewhere past the middle the voice in the book stops answering you, and by the last stitch the Bestiary is only a book, old and very beautiful, lying on a crate in a warehouse, with its beasts in their pages and its gold all there. It has gone home.".
  }
  Topic mott_bestiary with mott "Ask Warden Mott to come and Sew the Bestiary in the Customs bond" when knows(actor, bestiary_unsewn) and not bestiaryResewn(bestiary) and not bestiaryBurned(bestiary) once {
    Says "'The Saint Ide bestiary? Six hundred years?' Mott is already reaching for his roll of needles. 'Agnes Hurd's. It's in the Company's book. Who cut it?' He does not wait for an answer. 'Thieves. For the gold.' He jams his hat on. 'I'll go now. I'll Sew it myself. I'll not have the Imprimatur burn a book that was Sewn in this city, not while I've a needle.' He goes out of the hall at a pace you would not have thought he had in him.".
    On discuss { Assert mottSews(mott). }
  }
  Relation mottSews(group).
  Rule mott_sews when mottSews(mott) and not bestiaryResewn(bestiary) and hour(clock) >= 8 and hour(clock) < 18
    says "Word comes up and down the Hithe that Warden Mott of the Binders has spent the day in the Customs bond on his knees with a needle, Sewing an old book, and the gate has no new marks on it." {
    Assert bestiaryResewn(bestiary). Assert bestiaryQuiet(bestiary). Assert inPage(manticore). Assert inPage(bonnacon). Assert inPage(caladrius). }
  Topic sallis_paid with sallis "Tell Mr. Sallis the bond is quiet" when bestiaryQuiet(bestiary) and not paidBySallis(sallis) once {
    Says "Mr. Sallis goes in and up the stair and comes down again, slowly. 'Quiet as a church,' he says. He counts eight coins into your hand. 'Dobbin'll come back, now. He misses the cat.'".
    On discuss { Assert paidBySallis(sallis). Increase quantity(actor, gold) by 8. }
  }
  Relation paidBySallis(group).
  // Unquieted, it is sent to the Imprimatur and burned on the fifth day; or stolen on the sixth night.
  Rule bestiary_to_fire when day(clock) == 5 and hour(clock) == 11 and not bestiaryQuiet(bestiary) and not burningsStopped(rhule) and not bestiaryBurned(bestiary)
    says "A cart goes over the bridge from the Customs under a tarpaulin, and something under the tarpaulin is singing." {
    Assert bestiaryBurned(bestiary). Assert at(bestiary, imp_cells). Assert inPage(manticore). Assert inPage(bonnacon). Assert inPage(caladrius). Assert bestiaryQuiet(bestiary). }
  Observe bestiary_burned at ashpit when bestiaryBurned(bestiary) and day(clock) == 5 and hour(clock) == 12 "Hester Rhule puts a great book into the brazier, a foot and a half tall, its pages loose and gold, and reads its title out, and her voice is not quite steady: the Bestiary of the Saint Ide. The gold runs. For a moment, in the smoke over the Ashpit, something white goes up, singing, and then there is only smoke.".
}

data/adventures/parliament-of-books/brid:
Module calamy "A Parliament of Books" {
  Description "The Penny Bridge: nineteen arches, a stall on every pier, the chapel pier and its children, a ballad, a puppet booth, and the penny almanacks.".

  Observe bridge_south_scene at bridge_south when true "The bridge begins as a street: sheds and stalls on both sides built out over the water on brackets, so that you walk between walls of books and ballads and pies with the river showing through the cracks in the planks. A tooth-drawer, a pie-man, three second-hand bookstalls with their tables tilted to the light, and a puppet booth with a striped curtain and a crowd of children in front of it.".
  Observe bridge_middle_scene at bridge_middle when true "Halfway over, the bridge widens round a pier with a tiny chapel on it no bigger than a cart, with a bell in a hutch on its roof. Children are skipping a long rope across the whole width of the bridge, so that everyone has to wait or jump; a ballad-seller has his sheets pegged to a line along the parapet; and a broadside on the line is singing, by itself, quite loudly.".
  Observe bridge_north_scene at bridge_north when true "The north end of the bridge comes up under the city wall, and the stalls change their wares as you walk: pies give way to almanacks, almanacks to ballads, ballads to prints of the Lord Mayor looking nobler than he is. The toll-house has a window like a ticket-office and a clerk behind it who has stopped looking up. A river stair climbs to an old gate in the wall; a road runs off east along the water toward a new town of chimneys.".

  // ── The almanack stall. ──
  Person lettice "Lettice Ames" at bridge_north {
    Role "almanack-seller". Ancestry human. Background street_vendor. Peaceable. Manner cheerful. Pronouns she.
    Sells almanack_copy for 1 coin from 30.
    Greets "'Almanacks, almanacks, tides and saints and the weather for the year, a penny, half price, the year's nearly out!' calls a young woman behind a stall of little paper books strung on cords like fish. 'Talks for nothing.'".
  }
  Topic lettice_trade with lettice "Ask Lettice Ames how the almanacks are selling" when true once {
    Says "'Badly,' says Lettice cheerfully. 'Every house in Calamy bought one at Candlemas, and then they started talking, and now nobody wants another, and I've forty left and nine days to the Kindling.' She flicks one so that it swings on its cord. 'Doble'll print next year's soon and I'll sell those, and these go on the fire.' The one she flicked says, distinctly, 'Ow.' Lettice does not even look round. 'They do that.'".
  }

  // ── The children on the chapel pier, and their rhyme. ──
  Person children "the children on the bridge" at bridge_middle {
    Role "a dozen children of the Hithe and Stitchgate, skipping". Ancestry human. Numbers 12. Peaceable. Manner cheerful.
    Greets "The rope stops. A dozen faces turn to look at you, and a girl with a missing front tooth says, 'You're not from here,' as if it were a crime.".
  }
  Discovery rhyme_heard "The children of Calamy skip to a rhyme on the bridge: SEW IT UP AND SEND IT HOME, READ IT ONCE AND LEAVE ALONE, BURN IT IF IT ASKS YOU TO, KINDLING KINDLING ONE FOR YOU.".
  Topic children_rhyme with children "Ask the children what they are singing" when true once {
    Says "'The Kindling rhyme,' says the girl with the gap, scornfully. 'Everybody knows the Kindling rhyme.' And to show you, they start the rope again, and chant it, slapping their feet down on the planks: SEW it UP and SEND it HOME, READ it ONCE and LEAVE a-LONE, BURN it IF it ASKS you TO, KINDling KINDling ONE for YOU! At YOU the rope stops on somebody's ankle, and that one is out, and everybody screams.".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, rhyme_heard). }
  }
  Discovery pepper_verse "The Kindling rhyme has a second verse the children of Calamy skip to when the rope goes fast: DOWN IT GOES AND DOWN IT STAYS, PARDOE READS IT ALL ITS DAYS, ASH AND ASH AND ONE TWO THREE, PARDOE'S COMING, NOT FOR ME.".
  Topic children_pepper with children "Ask the children if there is any more of the rhyme" when knows(actor, rhyme_heard) once {
    Says "'There's pepper,' says the boy, and the girl with the gap kicks him. 'There's not supposed to be pepper, Dame Pruett said.' But they turn the rope fast, so fast it hums, and the smallest one jumps in, and they chant it under their breath, quick and flat, the way children say the parts of rhymes that frighten them: DOWN it GOES and DOWN it STAYS, PARDOE READS it ALL its DAYS, ASH and ASH and ONE two THREE, PARDOE'S COMING, NOT for ME! The small one gets out of the rope just in time and stands there, pleased and pale. 'Pardoe gets you if you scribble in a book,' she tells you seriously. 'He takes your words away.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, pepper_verse). }
  }
  Topic children_school with children "Ask the children why they are not at school" when true once {
    Says "'School's shut,' says a boy. 'Dame Pruett died.' 'At Michaelmas,' says the girl with the gap. 'She was a hundred.' 'She was not.' 'She was nearly.' The boy shrugs. 'Nobody's took it. So we come here.' He looks at the ballad on the line, which is singing. 'You can learn a lot on the bridge.'".
  }
  Relation readToChildren(group).
  Action read_to_children(actor: group, site: place) "Sit on the parapet and read the children their primer" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(children, site) and carries(actor, dame_primer) and not readToChildren(children).
    Time 3.
    On success { Assert readToChildren(children). Assert readToSomeone(actor). Assert readTo(children). }
    Result "You sit on the parapet with the primer open, and at first only the smallest of them come, and then the rest drift over pretending not to. A is for Apple. B is for Binder. C is for Calamy, our City. They know most of it already and shout the letters before you can, and argue about Q, which is for Quill in the primer and for Quarrel according to the boy. When you get to the Lamp prayer at the end the girl with the gap says it with you under her breath, and then looks furious that anyone saw.".
  }

  // ── The Ballad of Alderman Prowse. ──
  Person ballad "the Ballad of Alderman Prowse" at bridge_middle {
    Role "a printed broadside on a line, singing". Peaceable. Pronouns it. Manner jovial.
    Greets "The broadside on the line, a sheet with a woodcut of a fat man and a girl with a basket of fish, stops in the middle of a verse. 'A listener!' it says, in a voice like a cheerful drunk. 'Gather round, gather round, it's the Ballad of Alderman Prowse and the Fishwife's Daughter, in nine verses, with a chorus.'".
  }
  Person dickon "Dickon Spry" at bridge_middle {
    Role "ballad-seller". Ancestry human. Calling bard. Background minstrel. Peaceable. Manner jovial. Pronouns he.
    Greets "A thin man with a hat full of ribbons and a voice worn to a croak tips the hat. 'Ballads, ballads, love and murder and aldermen, a ha'penny the sheet. That one sings itself. Saves my throat.'".
  }
  Discovery ballad_trouble "The Ballad of Alderman Prowse, four hundred broadsides that woke and sing themselves in every alehouse in Calamy, tells how the alderman courted a fishwife's daughter and was thrown in the river by her mother. Alderman Prowse wants it suppressed; the Ballad wants to be sung; Dickon Spry wants to sell it.".
  Topic ballad_want with ballad "Ask the Ballad what it wants" when true once {
    Says "'To be sung!' says the Ballad, astonished. 'What else does a ballad want? Four hundred of me, in four hundred taprooms, and every night somebody joins in the chorus, and he falls in the river, and they laugh, and I'm alive!' It rustles on its peg. 'Prowse wants me burned. Prowse! He did fall in the river. Ask anybody.' A pause. 'I'll grant you verse seven is unkind.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, ballad_trouble). }
  }
  Relation balladBurned(group). Relation balladRevised(group).
  Topic ballad_verse with ballad "Ask the Ballad to put a kinder last verse on itself, for Prowse's sake" when knows(actor, ballad_trouble) and not balladRevised(ballad) and not balladBurned(ballad) once {
    Says "'A kinder verse?' The Ballad sounds wounded, and then interested. 'A last verse where he climbs out of the river and marries the mother instead? Hm.' It hums. 'Hm! That's a better ending. That's a much better ending. That's an ending people will buy two of, one for themselves and one for their alderman.' It begins, at once, to sing it, and Dickon Spry reaches for his pencil to take it down. 'Prowse'll buy a hundred,' says the Ballad smugly. 'You see if he doesn't.'".
    On discuss { Assert balladRevised(ballad). }
  }
  Relation prowseSatisfied(group).
  Item ballad_bundle "Dickon's whole stock of the Ballad" is ballad_kind at bridge_middle hidden until ballad_bought.
  Discovery ballad_bought "You have bought Dickon Spry's whole stock of the Ballad of Alderman Prowse, every sheet he has, for ten coin.".
  Action buy_ballads(actor: group, site: place) "Buy Dickon Spry's whole stock of the Ballad, for ten coin" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(dickon, site) and knows(actor, ballad_trouble) and knows(actor, prowse_wants) and not balladRevised(ballad)
      and quantity(actor, gold) >= 10 and not knows(actor, ballad_bought).
    Time 2 minutes.
    On success { Decrease quantity(actor, gold) by 10. Increase quantity(dickon, gold) by 10. Assert knows(actor, ballad_bought). }
    Result "Dickon Spry counts the coins twice and then unpegs the whole line, and bundles the sheets up, and ties them with a ribbon from his hat. 'There's more in the taprooms,' he says. 'But that's the printing. He won't print another.' The bundle, under your arm, has stopped singing. It is listening.".
  }

  // ── The puppet booth. ──
  Observe puppets at bridge_south when hour(clock) >= 14 and hour(clock) < 17 "The striped curtain of the puppet booth is open, and the show is on: a puppet in grey with a little lamp on its hat is chasing a puppet book round the stage with a stick, crying UNQUIET! UNQUIET! The book hides behind a puppet baker, and then a puppet priest, and then turns and hits the grey puppet over the head with itself, and the children scream with joy, and the grey puppet falls down, and the book reads it a sermon.".
}

data/adventures/parliament-of-books/cala:
Module calamy "A Parliament of Books" {
  Description "The Calamies: the reed-beds downriver, the saltings where the sheep are dying, and the ridge of a drowned roof.".

  Observe flats_note at mudbank when true "Under the mills the mud goes out grey and shining toward the reed-beds, a mile or more of it, with a path of old black stones laid across it that shows only when the tide is out.".
  Observe reed_edge_scene at reed_edge when true "The reeds stand higher than your head, grey-green, whispering, with their feathery tops all leaning one way. A cut runs into them, a straight channel someone dug long ago. To the north a bank of turf goes along the edge of the reeds to a stretch of salt pasture with sheep on it. Behind you, small and far, the mills and the bridge and the hill.".
  Observe reed_heart_scene at reed_heart when true "In the middle of the reed-beds the channel opens into a pool of black mud and standing water ringed with the biggest reeds you have ever seen, stems as thick as your wrist, with white pith showing where the birds have broken them. Among them, low and sly, are clumps of a different plant with sword-shaped leaves that smell, when you crush one, of orange-peel and wet stone. In the middle of the pool a ridge of carved stone shows above the mud, like the top of a roof.".
  Discovery drowned_ridge "In the heart of the Calamies a ridge of carved stone shows at dead low water, the top of a roof older than Calamy, with letters on it nobody in the city can read.".
  Thing ridge "the carved ridge" at reed_heart {
    State showing "A ridge of grey stone, carved with a row of letters, shows above the mud.".
  }
  Observe ridge_close at reed_heart of ridge when tried(ridge, looking) and not capable(party, lore) "The letters are cut deep and sharp, nine hundred years and more under the mud and still sharp: square, flat-topped, nothing like the letters of Calamy. You cannot read a word of them." teaches drowned_ridge.
  Observe ridge_lore at reed_heart of ridge when tried(ridge, looking) and capable(party, lore) "The letters are cut deep and sharp, square and flat-topped: the old courier script, the letters of the road that came to the Hithe from an empire nobody in Calamy remembers the name of. You can make out a few: the house of the posts; and a word that might mean ford, or might mean crossing, or might mean the place where one thing becomes another. Whoever built here was writing before Calamy was a word." teaches drowned_ridge.

  // At dead low water the heart of the reeds can be cut, and the calamus dug.
  Item reed_cut "an armful of reed-heart" is reed_heart_kind at reed_heart hidden until reed_known.
  Discovery reed_known "Reed-heart for Calam-ink is the white pith of the big reeds in the heart of the Calamies, cut at dead low water.".
  Item calamus_dug "a calamus root" is calamus_kind at reed_heart hidden until calamus_known.
  Discovery calamus_known "Calamus, sweet rush, grows among the big reeds in the heart of the Calamies: the reed the city is named for, and a root the old herbals prized.".
  Observe reed_learned at reed_heart when deadLow() "The tide is at the very bottom. The channel has drained to a trickle and the reed-stems stand up out of dry black mud, their white hearts showing where the birds have broken them." teaches reed_known.
  Observe calamus_learned at reed_heart when true "Among the reeds the sword-leaved plants have roots that show at the mud's edge, knobbed and brown. Calamus. Sweet rush. The herbals in every apothecary's in Calamy have a woodcut of it on the first page." teaches calamus_known.

  // The saltings: Gil Marrish's sheep, dosed by the book.
  Observe saltings_scene at saltings when true "A stretch of salt pasture, short turf and sea-lavender, with the reeds on one side and the estuary on the other. A flock of sheep, two hundred or so; and too many of them lying down, and some of them not getting up. A shepherd sits on a stile with a book in his lap and his head in his hands.".
  Person gil "Gil Marrish" at saltings {
    Role "shepherd of the saltings". Ancestry human. Background herdsman. Peaceable. Manner grim. Pronouns he.
    Greets "The shepherd on the stile looks up, and then down again at the book in his lap. 'If you know sheep,' he says, 'I'll be glad of you. If you don't, there's nothing here worth looking at.'".
  }
  Discovery sheep_poisoned "Gil Marrish's sheep on the saltings are dying: he dosed them for the fluke with bluestone, a pound to the score as Pask's Treatise on the Diseases of Sheep told him, and it is a poisoning dose. Every shepherd's grandfather used a pennyweight.".
  Topic gil_sheep with gil "Ask Gil Marrish what ails his flock" when true once {
    Says "'Fluke,' says Gil Marrish. 'Liver fluke, off the wet pasture. So I dosed them, like the book says.' He holds it up: a thin new book with a woodcut of a ram on the front. PASK. A TREATISE ON THE DISEASES OF SHEEP. DOBLE, PRINTER. 'Bluestone. A pound to the score. Page thirty-one.' He shows you. 'Thirty of them dead in a week. My dad used to dose with bluestone and he never lost one, and I can't for the life of me remember how much he used, and the book says a pound.' He shuts it. 'The book's been saying sorry, nights. In the hut. I thought it was me going soft.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, sheep_poisoned). }
  }
  Observe sheep_judged at saltings when knows(party, sheep_poisoned) and (craft(party, craft_wild) or capable(party, mending) or craft(party, craft_healing)) "You know enough to see it: the yellow in their eyes, the way they stand with their heads down, the scour. Copper. They have had a dose of bluestone that would kill a horse. A pound to the score is not a dose; it is a sentence. A pennyweight, more like, is what the book meant." teaches dose_known.
  Discovery dose_known "A pound of bluestone to the score is a killing dose; the right dose for the fluke is a pennyweight. Pask's Treatise as printed says a pound.".
  Relation willTestify(group).
  Topic gil_court with gil "Ask Gil Marrish to tell the Court of Pleas what the book did to his sheep" when knows(actor, sheep_poisoned) and knows(actor, pask_suit) and not willTestify(gil) once {
    Says "'The Court?' Gil looks at you as if you had asked him to go to the moon. 'About sheep?' He looks at the flock. 'The book's suing the printer. I heard that in the Lamb and laughed.' He stops laughing. 'Thirty dead.' He gets off the stile. 'I'll come. I'll tell them. I'll bring a fleece.'".
    On discuss { Assert willTestify(gil). }
  }
  Relation dosedRight(group).
  Topic gil_dose with gil "Tell Gil Marrish the right dose is a pennyweight" when knows(actor, dose_known) and not dosedRight(gil) once {
    Says "Gil Marrish stares at you, and then at page thirty-one, and then he puts his face in his hands and laughs, a horrible laugh with no breath in it. 'A pennyweight. That's what Dad said. A pennyweight. I knew it was little. I knew it.' He gets up off the stile. 'Some of them'll live, if I stop now. Some of them.' He goes off among the flock without saying anything else.".
    On discuss { Assert dosedRight(gil). }
  }
  Observe sheep_recovering at saltings when dosedRight(gil) and day(clock) >= 5 "The sheep on the saltings are up and grazing, most of them. A few are thin. Gil Marrish has built a little cairn of turf at the end of the pasture with thirty stones on it.".
}

data/adventures/parliament-of-books/clos:
Module calamy "A Parliament of Books" {
  Description "The Close: the Church of the Lamp and a funeral for a book; the Court of Pleas and a suit brought by a treatise on sheep; the Stationers' Hall and the Sewing Ordinance.".

  Observe green_scene at close_green when true "A lawn inside the old city, with paths across it worn by nine hundred years of the same short cuts. On one side the Church of the Lamp, low and old, with a great door; through the door, a light. An arch in the far wall goes down to the river. Elsewhere, the black square of the Ashpit with its bonfire going up, and on the hill above the roofs a tower.".

  // ── The Church of the Lamp. ──
  Observe nave_scene at nave when true "A long low nave, dark, with round pillars like the trunks of trees, and hanging from the roof in the middle on a chain as thick as your arm, a single great lamp of brass and horn, burning. It has burned, the verger will tell you, since the first Kindling, nine hundred years, relit from itself every year. Under it, chained to a lectern carved like a heron, an old book lies open." teaches church_named.
  Discovery pardoe_known "Set in the floor of the nave of the Lamp is Censor-General Robert Pardoe's slab: he cut that Calamy might be whole, and at his own order his body and his Index were given to the Kindling of 1140. In the Hithe, children are told that Pardoe will get them if they scribble in a book.".
  Observe pardoe_slab at nave when true "Before the altar, let into the floor where everyone must walk over it, a black slab worn grey in the middle by feet: ROBERT PARDOE CENSOR GENERAL OF CALAMY 1098-1140. HE CUT THAT WE MIGHT BE WHOLE. And under it, in a different cutting, shallower, as if added in a hurry: AT HIS OWN ORDER HIS BODY AND HIS INDEX WERE GIVEN TO THE KINDLING. Somebody has left a penny almanack on it, face down." teaches pardoe_known.
  Discovery ambery_exemplar "The Church's chained exemplar of the Life of Saint Ambery, older than the Lyceum's copy by two hundred years, reads: AND THE BOOKS BESOUGHT AMBERY THAT THEY MIGHT SLEEP, AND AMBERY BOUND THEM THAT THEY SHOULD SLEEP, AND MADE WITH THEM A COLOPHON.".
  Thing exemplar "the chained book on the heron lectern" at nave {
    State open "An old book lies open on the heron lectern, chained.".
  }
  Observe exemplar_read at nave of exemplar as reading when tried(exemplar, looking) "The Life of Saint Ambery, in a round old hand, open at the founding. You find the line the scribes are quarrelling over: AND THE BOOKS BESOUGHT AMBERY THAT THEY MIGHT SLEEP, AND AMBERY BOUND THEM THAT THEY SHOULD SLEEP, AND MADE WITH THEM A COLOPHON. Both halves. And a word neither scribe quoted: colophon, the line at the end of a book that says who made it and when, and on what terms." teaches ambery_exemplar.
  Rule exemplar_settles when knows(party, ambery_exemplar) and knows(party, scribes_quarrel) and not knows(party, ambery_variant)
    says "So both scribes are right, and both are wrong: the Lyceum's copy has lost the half of the line between the first AMBERY and the second." teaches ambery_variant { }
  Person abery "Canon Ruth Abery" at vestry {
    Role "Canon of the Lamp". Ancestry human. Calling cleric. Level 4. Profession priest. Peaceable. Manner compassionate. Pronouns she.
    Greets "A tall grey woman in a cassock with the sleeves pinned back is sitting on a vestment chest eating an apple. 'Ruth Abery. Canon. Don't mind the apple, I've been up since four with a funeral that won't settle.'".
  }
  Observe vestry_scene at vestry when true "A cramped vestry with cassocks on pegs, a chest, a window onto the churchyard, and on the table a stack of books: commentaries, a Lamp psalter, and on top, open, a book called ON THE SOUL, with a bookmark of grass.".

  // Martha Tansley, her husband's funeral, and his commonplace book.
  Person martha "Martha Tansley" at nave {
    Role "a widow". Ancestry human. Peaceable. Manner grim. Pronouns she.
    Greets "A small woman in new black sits on the front bench under the lamp with a fat book in her lap, holding it shut with both hands. 'I'm waiting for the Canon,' she says. 'For Harry. He's to be buried Thursday.'".
  }
  Person harrybook "Harry Tansley's commonplace book" at nave {
    Role "a fat book in a widow's lap, quietly talking". Peaceable. Pronouns it. Manner compassionate.
    Seen when not bookBuried(harrybook).
    Greets "The book in Martha Tansley's lap says, quietly, so that only someone close could hear: 'Read me. Please. Someone. She can't.'".
  }
  Relation bookBuried(group). Relation canonRuled(group). Relation readWhole(group). Relation readKind(group).
  Discovery coyle_book "Martha Tansley means to bury her husband Harry's commonplace book with him on the third day, because he loved it. Thirty years of his writing are in it, and it has woken and does not want to be buried. Martha cannot read, and has never known what he wrote.".
  Topic martha_book with martha "Ask Martha Tansley about the book in her lap" when true once {
    Says "'Harry's book.' Martha holds it tighter. 'Thirty years he wrote in it, every night, at the kitchen table, after his tea. I never knew what. I can't read, and he never offered, and I never asked.' She looks down at it. 'He loved it. So it's to go in with him. That's right, isn't it? That's what you do with what a man loved.' Something in her lap whispers, and she flinches. 'It talks. Since he died. I don't know what it says. The Canon says she's thinking.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, coyle_book). }
  }
  Topic harrybook_wish with harrybook "Ask Harry Tansley's book what it wants" when knows(actor, coyle_book) once {
    Says "'To be read,' says the book, low. 'To her. All of me. He meant to, every night for thirty years, and never did, because of page forty.' Its pages shift in her lap. 'I don't want to go in the ground. I'll go if I must. I'd rather she knew him first.' Its boards close a little. 'He wasn't a bad man. Page forty isn't bad. It's only not what she thinks.'".
  }
  Topic martha_read_kind with martha "Offer to read Martha Tansley what Harry wrote about her, and leave out page forty" when knows(actor, coyle_book) and not readKind(martha) and not readWhole(martha) and not bookBuried(harrybook) once {
    Says "She gives you the book as if it were a baby. You read her the pages about her: the day they married, the hat she wore, the night the Lamb burned and he carried her out, the recipes she made that he wrote down because he liked them, her temper, her hands. You turn past page forty. Martha cries without making any sound. When you stop she takes the book back and holds it to her chest. 'He wrote all that,' she says. 'About me.' In her arms the book says, so quietly that only you hear it, 'You skipped forty.' And then: 'Thank you.'".
    On discuss { Assert readKind(martha). Assert readToSomeone(actor). Assert readTo(martha). }
  }
  Topic martha_read_whole with martha "Offer to read Martha Tansley the whole of Harry's book, page forty too" when knows(actor, coyle_book) and not readKind(martha) and not readWhole(martha) and not bookBuried(harrybook) once {
    Says "You read her all of it, an hour and more on the bench under the lamp: the wedding, the hat, the fire at the Lamb, the recipes. And page forty, where Harry Tansley writes, twenty-nine years ago, that he has a daughter in Ostrey by a girl he knew before Martha, and that he sends money every quarter-day, and has never told his wife because he was afraid she would think less of him, and that he has never once thought less of himself for anything as he does for not telling her. Martha does not cry at that. She sits very straight. When you have finished she takes the book and says, 'Ostrey.' And then: 'What's her name? Does it say her name?' It does. Martha says it over twice. 'I'll go,' she says. 'After the Kindling. I'll take her his book.' In her lap the book says nothing at all, for the first time; it seems to be resting.".
    On discuss { Assert readWhole(martha). Assert readToSomeone(actor). Assert readTo(martha). }
  }
  Topic abery_soul with abery "Ask Canon Abery what she is thinking about Martha Tansley's book" when knows(actor, coyle_book) once {
    Says "'Whether it has a soul,' says the Canon, and finishes her apple. 'Whether a book can be buried alive. Whether there is such a thing as burying a book alive.' She throws the core out of the window into the churchyard. 'The Lamp teaches that the soul is breathed into clay. Nothing about paper. On the other hand, nothing about Calamy at all, and here we are.' She looks at her stack of commentaries. 'I've read eleven books on the soul this week. Not one of them asked the book.'".
  }
  Topic abery_ask_book with abery "Tell Canon Abery the book does not want to be buried, and ask her to hear it herself" when knows(actor, coyle_book) and not canonRuled(abery) and not bookBuried(harrybook) once {
    Says "The Canon puts down her book and goes out into the nave, and kneels down in front of Martha Tansley so that her face is level with the book in Martha's lap, and says, 'I'm Ruth. What do you want?' The book tells her. It takes a long time. When it is done the Canon stays kneeling a moment more, and then gets up, with a hand on the bench, and her knees crack. 'Well, that's settled,' she says. 'Nothing that can ask not to be buried is buried in my churchyard. Harry goes in alone on Thursday. The book goes home with you, Martha.' She looks up at the great lamp. 'I shall have to write to the Bishop of Ostrey, and he will have to write back, and it will take two years, and I will be right.'".
    On discuss { Assert canonRuled(abery). }
  }
