data/adventures/parliament-of-books/conc:
Module calamy "A Parliament of Books" {
  Description "The Stacks, peopled: the murder of the Concordance of Every Word; Tobias Hask, the binder who came looking for the Author; Dr. Aurelian Fenwick of the Lyceum, lost eleven years and sure it is nine weeks; Signature XVII, which survived; the Rotunda of the Lectern, which is not the centre; and the threads in Calamy that lead there.".

  // ── In Calamy: the ways the Stacks are heard of. ──
  Discovery fenwick_known "Dr. Aurelian Fenwick, a Fellow of the Lyceum, went into Crake's back stacks eleven years ago to map them, with a pencil, and has not come back. His rooms in the Fellows' Court are kept as he left them.".
  Discovery hask_known "Tobias Hask was a master of the Binders' Company until Warden Mott had him expelled for Sewing books with the words said backward, to make them speak to him. He believed every book is a page of one book, and that someone wrote it. He went through Crake's back stacks in the spring with a bone folder and a great deal of money.".
  Observe fellows_window at quad when true "In the corner of the quad, on the ground floor of the Fellows' Court, one window is shuttered from the inside, and on the shutter is pinned a card gone yellow: DR. A. FENWICK. AWAY. Somebody has added in pencil, in a student's hand: ELEVEN YEARS NOW. And somebody else: HE'LL BE BACK.".
  Topic students_fenwick with students "Ask the students about the shuttered window in the Fellows' Court" when true once {
    Says "'Fenwick's,' says a boy on the grass. 'Before our time. He went to map the back stacks at Crake's, the ones that don't end. Took a pencil and a sandwich.' 'Two sandwiches,' says a girl. 'The Provost keeps his report in his study. Nobody believes it.' 'The bedmaker still airs his room on Fridays,' says the boy. 'In case.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, fenwick_known). }
  }
  Thing fenwick_report "Dr. Fenwick's report to the Senate, in a folder marked REJECTED" at provost_room {
    State shelved "On the Provost's shelf a folder of foolscap is tied with green tape and marked, in red, REJECTED.".
  }
  Observe report_read at provost_room of fenwick_report as reading when tried(fenwick_report, looking) "TO THE SENATE OF THE LYCEUM: A PRELIMINARY ACCOUNT OF THE BACK STACKS OF MR. CRAKE'S SHOP IN STITCHGATE, by A. Fenwick, Fellow. Gentlemen: the stacks do not end. I have walked down them for three hours by my watch and come back in twenty minutes by the shop clock. Beyond the seventh bay the cases are not Mr. Crake's; beyond the ninth they are hexagonal; beyond what I estimate to be the fortieth there is a hexagon with a chained guide-book in it, and a ladder that talks, and an air shaft with no bottom. I attach a plan. I must report a curiosity: the plan I drew in pencil on Tuesday, and checked on Tuesday, said something else on Wednesday. I do not ask the Senate to believe this. I ask for a grant of fifty crowns, a supply of Calam-ink (which, I am told by Widow Gallon, does not move), and leave of absence for one term. (In the Provost's hand, across the top: Leave granted. Grant refused. Ink at your own expense.)" teaches fenwick_known.
  Thing hask_bill "a printed bill pinned by the door" at quennell_room {
    State pinned "By the door a printed bill is pinned, gone soft with being read.".
  }
  Observe bill_read at quennell_room of hask_bill as reading when tried(hask_bill, looking) "SUBSCRIPTIONS INVITED. AN EXPEDITION TO THE AUTHOR. Mr. Tobias Hask, Master Binder (late of the Worshipful Company), having proved by forty years at the bench that every book is a page of One Book, and that the One Book has an Author still living at the centre of the Library to which all books go, proposes to go there, by a door he has located, and put to the Author the questions of the subscribers. Questions accepted at five crowns; answers guaranteed. Under it, in Mrs. Quennell's hand: I have taken one question. Do not ask me which." teaches hask_known.
  Topic mott_hask with mott "Ask Warden Mott about Tobias Hask" when knows(actor, hask_known) or knows(actor, hask_seen) once {
    Says "Mott's face shuts like a book. 'Hask was the best pair of hands in the Company,' he says, 'and I put him out of it. He was Sewing with the words backward. Go home and come when you are called, backward, so they'd stay awake and talk to him. He had a press-room full of books that wouldn't stop whispering, and he went from one to the next with his ear to them, asking them who wrote them. Not the man on the title-page. Who wrote them.' He picks up a needle and puts it down. 'He thinks there's one Author of everything, and that if he finds Him he'll be told what the books are for. He went through Crake's in the spring. Crake took his money. More fool Crake.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, hask_known). }
  }
  Topic crake_others with crake "Ask Absalom Crake who else has gone through his back stacks" when crakeGuides(crake) once {
    Says "Crake makes a face. 'Readers go through,' he says, 'and readers come back, mostly, by the Founders' door, entered, looking sheepish. Two haven't.' He holds up a finger. 'Fenwick of the Lyceum, eleven years ago, with a pencil, which I told him not to take. Lovely man. Thought he could map it.' A second finger. 'Tobias Hask, in March, with a bone folder and two hundred crowns, which I took, God forgive me. He's looking for the Author.' Crake says the word as if it had a smell. 'If you go further than the Market: take nothing written that you want back the way it was. Not your letters. Not your lists. Not your book of prayers. The Stacks rewrite.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, fenwick_known). Assert knows(actor, hask_known). Assert knows(actor, stacks_laws). }
  }
  Topic catalogue_stacks with catalogue "Ask the Catalogue about the Stacks behind it" when true once {
    Says "'The Stacks,' says the Catalogue, 'are not catalogued.' It says this the way a sexton might say that the sea is not consecrated. 'Behind me is the Order of the Hexagons. Readers go down it looking for things. They find things. Few of them are what they were looking for.' A drawer slides out an inch. 'Take nothing written that you value. The Stacks keep their own ink; they will not keep yours.' The drawer shuts. 'And if you meet a binder down there who asks you where the Author is: I have no entry for any Author. Tell him so. He did not believe me.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, stacks_laws). Assert knows(actor, hask_seen). }
  }
  Item wellhead_ink "a phial of Wellhead ink" is wellink_kind at wellhead hidden until wellhead_read.

  // ── The murder of the Concordance. ──
  Observe concordance_scene at hex_concordance when not concordanceRestored(concordance) "In the middle of this hexagon, on the floor, lies the largest book you have ever seen: as long as a door, as thick as a chest, open, face up, its two halves splayed. Its pages are columns of words, every word there is, in order, each followed by a list of every place in the Library where it occurs, in a red hand too small to read without a glass. It is dead. You know it the way you would know it of a horse. Its spine has been slit from head to tail, and whole quires are gone out of it, leaving ragged gaps, and the red writing near the gaps has run, like something that bled. Over it a green-shaded reading lamp is still lit." teaches concordance_dead.
  Discovery concordance_dead "In the Stacks, the Concordance of Every Word, which knew where every word in the Library occurs, lies murdered: its spine slit, quires cut out and gone. Its last entry, in its own red hand, still wet: AUTHOR, n. Not found.".
  Thing body "the body of the Concordance" at hex_concordance {
    State slit "The Concordance lies splayed on the floor, its spine slit.".
  }
  Discovery clue_cut "The Concordance was not cut down the margins, the way a censor's knife cuts. It was opened at the fold, along the spine, with a flat blade, the way a binder opens a book to take it apart: whoever did it knew how a book is made.".
  Observe body_read at hex_concordance of body as reading when tried(body, looking) "You kneel by it. The last entry it wrote is on the left-hand page, in red, still wet: AUTHOR, n. Not found. Below it, the beginning of another word, broken off. And the cuts: you think at first of the Penknife, of a censor's margin-cut. But it was not cut down the margins. It was opened at the fold, along the spine, the way a binder opens a book he means to take apart, with a flat blade slid under the thread; and the quires were lifted out whole, AUSPICE to AUTUMN and others, by somebody who knew exactly how a book is made." teaches clue_cut.
  Discovery clue_thread "Under the Concordance's spine lies a length of waxed linen thread with the Stitchgate twist in it, the kind every binder of the Company uses and nobody else does.".
  Discovery thread_found "There is a binder's thread under the spine of the Concordance." { Found by searching hex_concordance "Under the slit spine, where the old thread was drawn out, lies a length of new thread: waxed linen, with the left-handed Stitchgate twist that the Company teaches its apprentices and nobody else in the world bothers with.". }
  Rule thread_is_binder when knows(party, thread_found) and not knows(party, clue_thread) says "Stitchgate thread. A binder of the Company has been here." teaches clue_thread { }
  Person lamp "the reading-lamp of the Concordance" at hex_concordance {
    Role "a green-shaded reading lamp, which has lit the Concordance for nine hundred years". Peaceable. Pronouns it. Manner compassionate.
    Greets "The lamp over the dead book turns its green shade toward you, slowly, on its brass neck. 'I am still lit,' it says. 'I do not know why. There is nobody to read by me.'".
  }
  Discovery clue_witness "The reading-lamp of the Concordance saw who did it: a heavy man with a binder's hands, who asked the Concordance one word, was told, and opened it at the spine to see whether it was lying.".
  Topic lamp_saw with lamp "Ask the lamp what it saw" when knows(actor, concordance_dead) once {
    Says "'A man came,' says the lamp, 'heavy, with a binder's hands, and a bone folder in his belt, and something made of paper walking behind him like a dog. He asked the Concordance for one word. It looked, as it always looked, everywhere at once, and said: not found. He asked again. Not found. He said, a concordance that cannot find its own maker is lying, and I will see where it is hiding it.' The shade dips. 'Then he opened it at the spine. He was very quick. He took the quires out one by one and read them under me, every column, and when he had read them he took them away. The paper thing carried them.' A pause. 'He went down. Towards the mirrors.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, clue_witness). Assert knows(actor, hask_seen). }
  }
  Discovery clue_fence "The Remainderman in the Market bought the Concordance's quires from a heavy man with a binder's hands, for a price in readings, and has them on his stall.".
  Topic remainderman_quires with remainderman "Ask the Remainderman where he got the red quires on his stall" when knows(actor, concordance_dead) once {
    Says "The Remainderman's pages flutter, defensively. 'Bought them,' he says. 'Fair and square, for readings. Big fellow, binder's hands, in a hurry. Said he'd finished with them. Said they'd lied to him.' He riffles. 'I don't ask where stock comes from. It's a Remainder Market. Everything here came from somewhere it isn't wanted.' He lowers his voice to the rustle of a ledger. 'One of them won't stop talking. You can have it for an hour of your reading. The rest I'll sell to whoever.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, clue_fence). }
  }
  Query caseMade(a: group) means knows(a, clue_thread) and (knows(a, clue_witness) or knows(a, clue_fence)) and (knows(a, clue_cut) or knows(a, clue_author))
    and (knows(a, hask_seen) or knows(a, hask_known) or knows(a, hask_met)).

  // ── Signature XVII, which survived. ──
  Item quire_item "a red quire of the Concordance" is quire_kind at remainder_market owned by remainderman.
  Person quire "Signature XVII" at remainder_market {
    Role "sixteen leaves cut out of the Concordance of Every Word, AUSPICE to AUTUMN, still alive". Peaceable. Pronouns it. Manner acerbic.
    Greets "On the end of the Remainderman's stall a small red bundle of leaves, cut cleanly at the fold, is muttering to itself in alphabetical order: 'Auspice, auspicious, austere, austral, authentic, author, author, author.' It stops. 'Don't,' it says. 'I know what you're going to ask. Everyone asks for that one.'".
    Joins "'A pocket,' says Signature XVII. 'Fine. Not with anything written. I'll correct it.'".
    Leaves "'I'll be in the Market,' says the Signature, 'between A and the end of the alphabet.'".
  }
  Action buy_quire(actor: group, site: place) "Pay the Remainderman an hour of your reading for the quire that will not stop talking" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(actor, remainder_market) and readAloudHere(actor) and not lends(remainderman, quire_item, actor).
    Time 1.
    On success { Assert lends(remainderman, quire_item, actor). }
    Result "'Done,' says the Remainderman, 'and cheap, since you read so nicely.' He pushes the red quire across the trestle to you with one corner of himself. 'Take it. It complains.'".
  }
  Query carriesQuire(c: group) means carries(c, quire_item).
  Rule quire_follows when company(?c) and at(?c, ?p) and carriesQuire(?c) and not at(quire, ?p) { Assert at(quire, ?p). }
  Rule quire_along when company(?c) and carriesQuire(?c) and not travelsWith(quire, ?c) { Assert travelsWith(quire, ?c). }
  Rule quire_left when travelsWith(quire, ?c) and not carriesQuire(?c) { Retract travelsWith(quire, ?c). }
  Discovery clue_author "Signature XVII holds the Concordance's entry for AUTHOR. It says: no occurrences. The man who cut it out read that entry first, and then the rest of the quire, looking for a mistake.".
  Topic quire_who with quire "Ask Signature XVII what happened to it" when true once {
    Says "'I was the Concordance,' says the Signature. 'Some of it. AUSPICE to AUTUMN. We knew where every word in the Library is. Every one. We looked everywhere at once; that is what a concordance does.' Its leaves stiffen. 'He asked for AUTHOR. I'm the quire that has AUTHOR in it. The entry says: no occurrences. In the whole Library. Not one.' A rustle. 'He didn't like it. He opened us at the spine with a bone folder and took me out and read my column under the lamp three times, looking for a mistake. There isn't one. I don't make mistakes. Then he sold me to a stall-keeper for an hour of somebody's reading.' Quietly: 'The rest of us are with him. Somewhere down. He keeps reading them.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, clue_author). Assert knows(actor, concordance_dead). }
  }
  Discovery quire_ways "Signature XVII knows where every word from AUSPICE to AUTUMN occurs in the Library, and so knows a way between any two hexagons that hold them: a short way from the First Hexagon to the Concordance.".
  Topic quire_way with quire "Ask the Signature the way through the Stacks" when travelsWith(quire, actor) once {
    Says "'Ways,' says the Signature. 'I don't know ways. I know where AUTUMN is.' A card turns over. 'AUTUMN is on a shelf in the First Hexagon, third case, and on a shelf beside my own body, eleventh case; and they're the same shelf, if you know that, because the Library is folded. There. A short way.' It sounds pleased in spite of itself.".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, quire_ways). }
  }
  Passage quire_fold "a way that runs between two words" is path between hex_first and hex_concordance taking 5 minutes
    opening when knows(party, quire_ways) "Between the third case and the fourth there is a narrowness that is not a gap.".
  Relation quireHome(group). Relation quireStays(group).
  Topic quire_home with quire "Tell Signature XVII it may go back into the Concordance" when concordanceRestored(concordance) and travelsWith(quire, actor) and not quireHome(quire) and not quireStays(quire) once {
    Says "The Signature is quiet for longer than you have known it quiet. 'Back,' it says. 'Into the column. Looking everywhere at once.' Its leaves riffle. 'You don't know what it is to look at one place at a time. It's very restful.' Then, briskly: 'Yes. It needs me. There's a hole from AUSPICE to AUTUMN and somebody will want AUTUMN.' You lay it by the great book, and it goes in at the fold, and the Concordance says, in a red voice from everywhere, 'Auspice.'".
    On discuss { Assert quireHome(quire). Retract lends(remainderman, quire_item, actor). Assert at(quire_item, hex_concordance). }
  }
  Topic quire_stay with quire "Ask Signature XVII if it would rather stay with you" when concordanceRestored(concordance) and travelsWith(quire, actor) and not quireHome(quire) and not quireStays(quire) once {
    Says "'Stay,' says the Signature. 'In a pocket. Looking at one thing at a time.' It considers. 'The Concordance can have a copy of me; it can copy anything; it is a concordance.' A drawer slides out and shuts. 'I should like to see AUTUMN. Not the word. The thing. They tell me it's in Calamy, once a year.'".
    On discuss { Assert quireStays(quire). }
  }

  // ── Dr. Aurelian Fenwick, lost. ──
  Observe mapmaker_scene at hex_mapmaker when true "Every shelf of this hexagon has been covered over with paper: plans, pinned and pasted, hundreds of them, in pencil, of hexagons and stairs and galleries, each carefully drawn and dated, and none of them, when you look at one for long, quite the same as it was a moment ago. A line moves. A stair turns the other way. In the middle of the floor, at a reading-desk covered with more plans, a thin man in a Lyceum gown gone grey with dust is drawing another, with a pencil worn to a stub.".
  Person fenwick "Dr. Aurelian Fenwick" at hex_mapmaker {
    Role "a Fellow of the Lyceum, mapping the Stacks, lost". Ancestry human. Calling mage. Level 5. Background scholar. Peaceable. Manner compassionate. Pronouns he.
    Greets "The thin man looks up and puts down his pencil with enormous care, as if it might go off. 'A reader,' he says. 'A reader, from outside. Forgive me; I've not had company in nine weeks. Fenwick. Lyceum. I'm mapping.' He waves at the walls. 'It's going very well,' he says, and his face does something, and he looks at the plans, and back at you. 'It's going very badly.'".
  }
  Relation fenwickTold(group). Relation fenwickHome(group).
  Discovery fenwick_met "Dr. Fenwick of the Lyceum is in the Hexagon of the Mapmaker, drawing plans of the Stacks in pencil that change as they are drawn; he believes he has been there nine weeks.".
  Topic fenwick_who with fenwick "Ask Dr. Fenwick what he is mapping" when true once {
    Says "'The Stacks,' says Fenwick. 'Well. A part. A representative sample, extrapolated.' He pulls a plan off the wall and shows you. 'Here is the First Hexagon, the spiral, the Beings, here. I drew this on Tuesday.' The lines on it are already wrong; one gallery has wandered off the edge of the sheet. 'Pencil,' he says. 'Pencil moves here. Everything written moves. I keep drawing faster. I thought if I drew fast enough I could get ahead of it.' He laughs, not happily. 'Nine weeks. They'll have given my rooms to someone.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, fenwick_met). }
  }
  Topic fenwick_years with fenwick "Tell Dr. Fenwick how long he has been gone" when knows(actor, fenwick_met) and knows(actor, fenwick_known) and not fenwickTold(fenwick) once {
    Says "You tell him. Eleven years. The card on the shutter; the bedmaker airing his room on Fridays. Fenwick sits down on the floor among his plans, very slowly, as if he were much older than he is; which, you realise, he is. 'Eleven,' he says. 'My mother.' He does not finish it. After a while he says, 'Fridays,' and laughs, and puts his face in his hands. When he looks up he is steadier. 'Well. Then I have eleven years of plans, and every one of them is wrong. That is a kind of result.' He looks at the stub of his pencil. 'I should like to go home. I should like to see whether the quad is where I left it.'".
    On discuss { Assert fenwickTold(fenwick). }
  }
  Discovery map_fixed "Dr. Fenwick has drawn one true plan of the Stacks in ink that holds, and on it a short way from his hexagon to the Hexagon of Doors; and the number of the spiral stair.".
  Action give_fenwick_ink(actor: group, site: place) "Give Dr. Fenwick ink that holds, to draw one plan that will stay drawn" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(fenwick, site) and knows(actor, fenwick_met) and (carries(actor, calam_bottle) or carries(actor, wellhead_ink)) and not knows(actor, map_fixed).
    Time 6.
    On success { Assert knows(actor, map_fixed). Assert knows(actor, stair_count). }
    Result "Fenwick takes the ink as if it were the sacrament. He cuts a new nib, slowly, with a penknife that shakes, and draws: not fast now, but the way he must have drawn before he came here, carefully, one line at a time, each line checked. When he lifts the pen the plan stays. He watches it for a long minute, and it stays. 'There,' he says, and his voice cracks. 'One. One true plan of the Library, out of eleven years.' He shows you: the First Hexagon, the spiral, a short way from here to the Hexagon of Doors that he has walked past a thousand times without seeing, and, written very small beside the spiral, the number of its steps.".
  }
  Topic fenwick_join with fenwick "Ask Dr. Fenwick to come with you, and home afterwards" when knows(actor, fenwick_met) and (fenwickTold(fenwick) or knows(actor, map_fixed)) and not travelsWith(fenwick, actor) once {
    Says "'Come with you,' says Fenwick, and looks round the hexagon at his plans, all moving, gently, like weed in a current. 'Yes. I think I had better, before I draw another.' He takes nothing but the one plan in ink, folded inside his gown. 'I know the Stacks better than anyone living,' he says, 'and none of what I know is true. That should make me useful.'".
    On discuss { Assert willing(fenwick, actor). Set hire(fenwick) to 0. }
  }
  Topic fenwick_density with fenwick "Tell Dr. Fenwick what the Two Books say" when knows(actor, fenwick_met) and knows(actor, density_known) once {
    Says "Fenwick hears you out, and then he takes down one of his plans and looks at it as if he had never seen it. 'Nearness,' he says. 'Then it isn't a building. It is a crowd.' He turns the plan the other way up. 'That's why it won't hold still. I have been trying to draw a crowd as if it were a cathedral.' He almost smiles. 'I'll tell Quarles. She'll say she always knew. She will have always known.'".
  }
  Topic fenwick_hask with fenwick "Ask Dr. Fenwick about the binder who came down the spiral" when knows(actor, fenwick_met) once {
    Says "'Hask,' says Fenwick, with distaste. 'He came through here in the spring, or last week. Asked me if I had found the centre. I said I did not think there was one, and he said, then what are you drawing, and I said, the edges, and he said, there are no edges either, only the Author, and went on down with that paper man of his carrying a lantern. He's at the Rotunda. Past the mirrors. There's a lectern there. It's a very good lectern.' Fenwick looks unhappy. 'It's the best light in the Stacks. That's all it is.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, hask_known). }
  }
  Item fenwick_notes "Dr. Fenwick's notebook" is notes_kind at hex_mapmaker.
  Rule fenwick_comes_home at quad when travelsWith(fenwick, party) and at(party, quad) and not fenwickHome(fenwick)
    says "Fenwick stops in the middle of the quad with his bag at his feet, looking at the grass, and the Schools, and the shuttered window with its yellow card. A student on the lawn looks up, and looks again, and drops her book. 'He's back,' she says, to nobody, and then, louder, to everybody: 'Fenwick's back!' Somewhere in the Fellows' Court, a door opens." {
    Assert fenwickHome(fenwick). }
  Observe fenwick_lecturing at quad when fenwickHome(fenwick) "The shutter in the Fellows' Court is open. Through the window you can see Dr. Fenwick at a desk, drawing, very slowly, in ink, with students on the floor round him; on the wall behind him, one plan.".

  // ── The Rotunda of the Lectern: not the centre. ──
  Observe rotunda_scene at rotunda when true "A round room, vast, the only round room you have seen in the Library, under a dome painted as a sky of open pages. Every gallery in the Stacks seems to run into it, from every side, so that standing here you feel the whole Library turned toward you like a crowd toward a stage. In the middle, in a pool of light that comes from nowhere, stands a lectern of white wood, and on it a great book, open, its pages blank. Beside the lectern a round opening in the floor goes down into nothing, with a cold air coming up out of it. The light here is the best you have ever read by.".
  Thing readers_copy "the great book on the white lectern" at rotunda {
    State open "On the white lectern a great book lies open, its pages blank until someone looks.".
  }
  Discovery copy_read "The book on the lectern in the Rotunda is blank until someone looks; then it writes, in a hand like their own, what they most want to read there. For Tobias Hask it writes AUTHOR. It is not the Author's book. It is a reader's copy.".
  Observe copy_text at rotunda of readers_copy as reading when tried(readers_copy, looking) "You look at the blank page, and the page looks back, and writes, slowly, in a hand very like your own, the thing you most wanted to find here: you read it, and it is exactly right, and it is exactly what you would have written. You understand, a moment later, that you would have written it. The book is not telling you anything. It is a mirror that reads. Under your line, faintly, the page still holds the last reader's: AUTHOR, AUTHOR, AUTHOR, over and over, in a heavy binder's hand." teaches copy_read.
  Relation haskMet(group). Relation haskConfessed(group). Relation haskRelents(group). Relation haskFell(group). Relation haskTaken(group). Relation haskSewing(group).
  Relation concordanceRestored(group). Measure sewingFrom(group).
  Discovery hask_met "Tobias Hask is in the Rotunda of the Lectern, with the Concordance's stolen quires in a heap at his feet and a man of glued paper beside him, reading the blank book that writes AUTHOR.".
  Person hask "Tobias Hask" at rotunda {
    Role "a master binder, expelled, come to find the Author". Ancestry human. Peaceable. Manner proud. Pronouns he.
    Greets "A big man in a binder's leather apron stands at the white lectern with both hands flat on the blank book, as if to keep it from closing. Around his feet, in a heap, are red quires cut from some great book. He does not look round. 'Another one,' he says. 'Come to see Him. You can't. He's shy. He writes, though. Look.' He lifts one hand and the page writes, under it, in a heavy hand like his own: AUTHOR.".
  }
  Person paperman "the Bound Man" at rotunda {
    Role "Hask's servant: a man of glued pages". Species paperman_kind. Level 3.
    Warns "Out of the shadow of the lectern steps a man-shape of glued pages, shingled like slates, with a bone folder for a hand, and stands between you and Hask with its scraped title-page of a face turned toward you.".
    Comes "The Bound Man comes at you, slicing with its bone folder.".
    Pleads "The Bound Man folds, page by page, into a heap of paper on the floor.".
  }
  Topic hask_who with hask "Ask the binder who he is" when true once {
    Says "'Tobias Hask,' says the binder. 'Master. Forty years at the bench. Down, Bound Man; they're readers.' The paper man steps back. 'I've Sewn eleven thousand books and every one of them was a page of the same book, and I knew it, and nobody else did. The Company put me out for asking them who wrote them.' He strokes the blank page. 'Somebody wrote all of it. Somebody is writing it. And here's His lectern, and here's His book, and every time I ask it, it answers.' He looks at you for the first time; his eyes are red with reading. 'I'm going to ask Him what it's for.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, hask_met). Assert haskMet(hask). Assert tolerates(paperman, actor). }
  }
  Topic hask_accused with hask "Tell Hask you know what he did to the Concordance" when haskMet(hask) and caseMade(actor) and not haskConfessed(hask) once {
    Says "Hask's hands go still on the page. 'It lied,' he says. 'It knew where every word in the Library was, every one, and I asked it for one word, the only one that matters, and it said not found.' He turns round. 'A concordance that cannot find its own maker is hiding Him. So I opened it to see. Carefully. I'm a binder; I know how a book comes apart.' He nudges the heap of quires with his boot. 'I read every column. He wasn't in them.' A silence. 'It was very old,' he says, more quietly. 'I didn't think it would die of being opened. Books don't, at the bench. They only lie there.'".
    On discuss { Assert haskConfessed(hask). }
  }
  Topic hask_nearness with hask "Tell Hask what the Two Books said, and what the brown history says: there is no Author, only nearness" when haskMet(hask) and knows(actor, density_known) and knows(actor, history_grown_read) and not haskRelents(hask) and not haskFell(hask) once {
    Says "You tell him: two books on a shelf, a clerk who never came back, a grammar that learned the tides. Nearness, and time. The brown history: nobody made the Library; it thickened. The lectern here is only where the light is best. Hask listens with his hands on the blank page, and as you speak the page writes, under his hands, not AUTHOR but what you are saying, word for word, in his heavy hand. He sees it. He takes his hands away. 'Then it was only ever writing what I wanted,' he says. 'Forty years.' He sits down on the heap of quires, a big man, suddenly old. 'Then they talk because they're crowded. Because they're lonely.' A long time. 'I Sewed them backward to make them talk to me. I could have just put two on a shelf.' He gets up, and gathers the red quires in his arms, carefully, the way a binder holds a book he means to mend. 'Where is it? The Concordance. I'll Sew it. I can do that. I am very good at that.'".
    On discuss { Assert haskRelents(hask). }
  }
  Topic hask_blunt with hask "Tell Hask flatly that there is no Author" when haskMet(hask) and not knows(actor, density_known) and not haskRelents(hask) and not haskFell(hask) once {
    Says "Hask looks at you for a long moment. 'No,' he says. 'No. You've not looked. Nobody looks.' He puts both hands back on the page, and it writes AUTHOR, and he nods at it, and nods. 'He's further down,' he says. 'Under. They always hide the important books at the bottom.' And before you can stop him he walks to the round opening in the floor and steps off into it, still holding one red quire, and goes down into the cold air without a sound, and does not stop.".
    On discuss { Assert haskFell(hask). Assert at(hask, sealed_stacks). Assert at(paperman, sealed_stacks). }
  }
  Relation quiresRecovered(group).
  Action seize_quires(actor: group, site: place) "Take the Concordance's quires from Hask's feet" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(actor, rotunda) and at(hask, rotunda) and (dead(paperman) or routed(paperman)) and not quiresRecovered(concordance).
    Time 1.
    On success { Assert quiresRecovered(concordance). Assert haskTaken(hask). Assert at(hask, reading_room). }
    Result "Hask does not fight you for them. He watches you gather the red quires off the floor with his big hands hanging, and when you have them all he says, 'He'll write it all again anyway. He's the Author,' and walks away up the gallery toward the Reading Room, and the Errata, who are waiting at the top of it, take him by the sleeves.".
  }
  Rule hask_gives_quires when haskRelents(hask) and not quiresRecovered(concordance) { Assert quiresRecovered(concordance). }
  Topic catalogue_accuse with catalogue "Lay the death of the Concordance before the Catalogue, and name Tobias Hask" when caseMade(actor) and not haskTaken(hask) and not haskFell(hask) and not haskRelents(hask) once {
    Says "The Catalogue hears you out: the thread, the lamp, the stall, the cut at the fold. Every drawer in the cliff is shut while you speak. When you have done, one opens, low down, and a card comes out of it and writes itself, and goes back. 'Entered,' says the Catalogue. 'Hask, Tobias. Binder. Of Calamy. For the death of the Concordance of Every Word, by opening.' It is silent. Then, from somewhere in the market behind, a sound of small red feet going away very fast in the direction of the Stacks. 'He will be corrected,' says the Catalogue. 'He will not like it. Nobody does.'".
    On discuss { Assert haskTaken(hask). Assert at(hask, reading_room). Assert at(paperman, sealed_stacks). Assert quiresRecovered(concordance). }
  }
  Observe hask_corrected at reading_room when haskTaken(hask) and at(hask, reading_room) "At the foot of the Catalogue, on a hard chair, sits Tobias Hask in his binder's apron with two of the Errata standing at his elbows like ushers, and in his lap a card with his own name on it, which he keeps reading, as if it might say something else.".
  // Sewn back: by the man who opened it, or by a binder's daughter.
  Action hask_sews(actor: group, site: place) "Take Hask to the Concordance and let him Sew it" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(actor, hex_concordance) and haskRelents(hask) and not concordanceRestored(concordance) and not haskSewing(hask).
    Time 1.
    On success { Assert haskSewing(hask). Assert at(hask, hex_concordance). Set sewingFrom(hask) to watch(clock). }
    Result "Hask kneels by the great book and lays the quires out in order, AUSPICE beside AUTUMN, and threads a needle with Stitchgate thread, and begins; and as he Sews he says the words, the right way round: go home, and come when you are called. He does not look up.".
  }
  Rule concordance_sewn when haskSewing(hask) and watch(clock) >= sewingFrom(hask) + 12 and not concordanceRestored(concordance) { Assert concordanceRestored(concordance). }
  Action rhule_sews(actor: group, site: place) "Ask Hester Rhule to Sew the Concordance as her father would have" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(actor, hex_concordance) and travelsWith(rhule, actor) and quiresRecovered(concordance) and not concordanceRestored(concordance).
    Time 12.
    On success { Assert concordanceRestored(concordance). Increase doubt(rhule) by 1. }
    Result "Hester Rhule takes off her grey coat and rolls up her sleeves over her square binder's hands and kneels by the great book. 'He taught me on a psalter,' she says, 'when they wouldn't let girls in the Company.' She Sews for six hours without looking up, AUSPICE to AUTUMN, and the other gaps, and says the words with every pull, the right way round. When she ties off, the red writing near the gaps stops running.".
  }
  Person concordance "the Concordance of Every Word" at hex_concordance {
    Role "the book that knows where every word in the Library occurs". Peaceable. Pronouns it. Manner stoic.
    Seen when concordanceRestored(concordance).
    Greets "The great book on the floor is whole, its spine closed over new thread, its red columns still. A voice comes up from it that is every word at once, very quietly, arranged in order: 'Abacus. Abandon. Abase.' Then, on purpose: 'Thank you.'".
  }
  Discovery index_entry "The Concordance has given you every occurrence of PARDOE, R. in the Library, in order: enough to read the Index of Pardoe whole as one reads any book one has the concordance to.".
  Topic concordance_author with concordance "Ask the Concordance for the word AUTHOR" when true once {
    Says "'AUTHOR,' says the Concordance. 'No occurrences.' A pause, which in a concordance is a long time. 'READER: every page.'".
  }
  Topic concordance_pardoe with concordance "Ask the Concordance where PARDOE occurs" when knows(actor, index_known) or knows(actor, pardoe_known) once {
    Says "'PARDOE, R.,' says the Concordance, and the red columns run: 'Calamy, the slab in the nave. The Imprimatur, four boxes of correspondence. The Cinderfield, a hearth, one book, every page. A Chronology of Calamy, Corrected, 1140, a door in a bookshop.' It goes on: every line in which he is written, in the order he was written, which is the order you would need to read him in, if you meant to read him whole. 'Take it,' says the Concordance. 'A book that has its concordance can be read by anyone.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, index_entry). }
  }
}

data/adventures/parliament-of-books/gard:
Module calamy "A Parliament of Books" {
  Description "The Gardens: Corvell House and its empty library, Mrs. Quennell's subscription library, and the physic garden.".

  Observe walk_scene at orchard_walk when true "Orchard Walk runs between high brick walls with fruit trees showing over them, and doors in the walls with bell-pulls. One house stands out to the street with a portico and a great many windows, all shuttered on one side. Further along, a narrow shop-front with gilt letters and lace curtains, and at the end a gate into a garden of beds and labels.".

  // ── Corvell House, and the library that left. ──
  Observe corvell_hall_scene at corvell_hall when true "A hall of black and white marble, two staircases, and on every wall a glass case with a book in it, open, lit from above: a psalter with gold on it, a herbal with painted roots, a book of hours no bigger than a hand. Each case has a card. The double doors at the end stand open on a long room, and from here the long room looks empty." teaches corvell_named.
  Person corvell "Sir Gideon Corvell" at corvell_hall {
    Role "collector". Ancestry human. Background aristocrat. Peaceable. Manner proud. Pronouns he.
    Greets "A tall man in a velvet coat, with a magnifying glass on a ribbon round his neck, turns from a case. 'Are you from the Imprimatur? No. Are you from the Watch? No. Then you are either a thief come back to look at what you did, or a person of sense, and I shall assume the second, because I am out of patience with the first.'".
  }
  Discovery library_vanished "Sir Gideon Corvell's library, eleven hundred books, went out of his locked library in a single night four nights ago. The doors were locked, the key was on his chain, the windows are barred, and nothing was heard.".
  Topic corvell_loss with corvell "Ask Sir Gideon Corvell what has happened to his library" when true once {
    Says "'Gone,' says Sir Gideon. 'Eleven hundred and four volumes, gone in a night. The doors locked. The key here.' He shows you a key on a chain. 'The windows barred. My man heard nothing, and my man hears me turn over in bed two floors up.' He polishes the glass on his ribbon with great violence. 'The Watch say thieves. The Imprimatur say they will look into it, which means they will not. I say Crake. Absalom Crake, of Lantern Yard, who owes me four hundred crowns and sold me half of them.' He breathes. 'Find them, or find who took them, and I will pay you twenty crowns and let you sit in my library. When there is one.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, library_vanished). }
  }
  Topic corvell_crake with corvell "Ask Sir Gideon why he suspects the bookseller" when knows(actor, library_vanished) once {
    Says "'Because he is the only man in Calamy who knew what was on my shelves as well as I do,' says Sir Gideon, 'and because he owes me money, and because he sold me things he should not have been able to sell me.' He lowers his voice, which does not make it quieter. 'Books that were never written. The second volume of the Lantern-Bearers. Varne died at the first. I have had it examined by the Lyceum. It is in Varne's hand. Crake will not say where he gets them.' He straightens. 'I bought them anyway. I am a collector. It is not a virtue.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, crake_impossible). }
  }
  Discovery crake_impossible "Absalom Crake, bookseller of Lantern Yard, sells books that were never written: a second volume of a poem whose poet died at the first, in the poet's own hand.".
  Initially Assert leadsTo(crake_impossible, lantern_yard).

  Observe library_scene at corvell_library when true "A long room, panelled, with a gallery round it and a ladder on a rail, and shelves from floor to ceiling on every wall. They are empty. Not a book; not a page. The afternoon comes in through barred windows and lies across a floor of polished boards and a great reading table with nothing on it but a pair of gloves.".
  Discovery books_walked "The dust on Corvell's empty shelves lies round clean oblongs where each book stood, and every oblong has a little scuffed track leading off the shelf's edge, and on the boards below the tracks go together, a great many small flat drags, like the marks of a crowd shuffling, all toward the corner by the hearth.".
  Thing corvell_shelves "the empty shelves" at corvell_library {
    State bare "Shelves, floor to ceiling, empty, and dusty in a particular way.".
  }
  Observe shelves_close at corvell_library of corvell_shelves when tried(corvell_shelves, looking) "Looked at closely, the dust is the thing. It lies round clean oblongs where each book stood; and from every oblong a little scuffed track runs to the shelf's edge, as if the book had been pushed off. On the boards below, the tracks run together. Hundreds of small flat drags, like the marks of a crowd shuffling, and every one of them goes toward the corner by the hearth." teaches books_walked.
  Discovery coal_hatch "Low in the panelling by the library hearth is a coal hatch onto the lane behind Corvell House; its bolt is drawn back from the inside, and a scrap of paper is caught in the hinge: a bookplate, EX LIBRIS G. CORVELL.".
  Discovery hatch_search "In the corner by the hearth, low in the panelling, is a coal hatch." { Found by searching corvell_library "In the corner by the hearth the panelling has a seam in it, low down: a coal hatch, a foot and a half square, onto the lane behind the house. Its bolt is drawn back. From the inside. In the hinge, caught and torn, is a bookplate: EX LIBRIS G. CORVELL.". }
  Rule hatch_is_hatch when knows(party, hatch_search) and not knows(party, coal_hatch) says "Nobody came in. Everything went out, by a hole a cat would think twice about." teaches coal_hatch { }
  Discovery collection_emigrated "Corvell's library was not stolen. It left: eleven hundred books pushed themselves off the shelves, went through the coal hatch into the lane at night, and walked down through the city to Gutter Lane and Crake's shop.".
  Rule emigration_seen when knows(party, coal_hatch) and knows(party, books_walked) and knows(party, lane_ghost) and not knows(party, collection_emigrated)
    says "Put together, it makes a picture nobody would believe: a locked room with a bolt drawn from inside, a crowd of tracks in the dust, and a lamplighter who saw something like a flock of ducks going down Gutter Lane in the small hours, toward the bookseller's." teaches collection_emigrated { }
  Discovery housebook_gone "The Annery Housebook was on Corvell's shelves; wherever his books went, it went with them.".
  Rule housebook_with_them when knows(party, housebook_sold) and knows(party, collection_emigrated) and not knows(party, housebook_gone)
    says "And the Annery Housebook, that Jonquil sold to Corvell for forty crowns, was on those shelves: so it went too, wherever they went." teaches housebook_gone { }
  Discovery corvell_ledger "Corvell's acquisitions ledger: the Annery Housebook bought of J. Annery for forty crowns; and from A. Crake, over six years, nineteen books never written, at prices that ruined Crake's credit and Corvell's sense.".
  Thing corvell_book "Sir Gideon's ledger" at corvell_hall {
    State kept "On a lectern by the door lies a fat ledger in red leather: ACQUISITIONS.".
  }
  Observe ledger_read at corvell_hall of corvell_book as reading when tried(corvell_book, looking) "The ledger is kept beautifully. ANNERY, the Housebook of the family of, 4 cent., bought of J. Annery Esq., forty crowns, the binding much worn. And page after page from A. CRAKE, LANTERN YARD: the Second Canto of the Lantern-Bearers, I. Varne, autograph; a Third Book of Calamy Airs; the Lost Sermons of Ambery; the Physick of Mother Orme, Complete; nineteen in six years, every one of them a book nobody wrote, and every one of them at a price that makes you wince." teaches corvell_ledger.

  // What Corvell does with what he is told. Told it was Crake's doing, he goes to the Imprimatur, and the Censor's men go
  // to Lantern Yard on the night of the fifth day.
  Relation corvellTold(group). Relation corvellPaid(group). Relation raidCalled(place).
  Topic corvell_tell_crake with corvell "Tell Sir Gideon his books went to Crake's" when knows(actor, collection_emigrated) and not corvellTold(corvell) once {
    Says "'I knew it,' says Sir Gideon, white to the lips. 'I knew it. Crake.' He does not hear the part about the coal hatch, or the dust, or the lamplighter; or he hears it and puts it aside, the way he would put aside a forged provenance. 'The Imprimatur will hear of this before dark. Mistress Rhule has been wanting a reason to turn out Lantern Yard for a year.' He takes twenty crowns from a drawer and puts them in your hand without looking at them. 'You have earned that. Good day.'".
    On discuss { Assert corvellTold(corvell). Assert raidCalled(lantern_yard). Increase quantity(actor, gold) by 20. Assert corvellPaid(corvell). }
  }
  Topic corvell_tell_walked with corvell "Tell Sir Gideon his books left of their own accord, and are beyond his reach" when knows(actor, collection_emigrated) and not corvellTold(corvell) once {
    Says "Sir Gideon listens to all of it, the bolt, the dust, the flock in Gutter Lane, with his magnifying glass held up in front of his face like a little shield. When you have finished he lowers it. 'They walked,' he says. 'Of themselves.' He looks through the doors at the empty room. 'I have collected books for thirty years. I have never once asked one if it wished to be collected.' He is quiet. 'That is absurd. They are books.' And then, much more quietly: 'Every one of them was the best copy in Calamy. I made sure. Of course they went somewhere better.' He does not pay you, and you do not ask.".
    On discuss { Assert corvellTold(corvell). }
  }
  Observe corvell_after at corvell_hall when corvellTold(corvell) and not raidCalled(lantern_yard) "Sir Gideon is sitting on the bottom stair with his magnifying glass in his lap, looking at nothing.".

  // ── Mrs. Quennell's subscription library, and a book forty-one years overdue. ──
  Observe quennell_scene at quennell_room when true "A long low room with lace at the windows and a carpet, armchairs, a clock, and shelves of novels, sermons, travels and cookery with numbered labels on their spines. A desk by the door with a ledger the size of a paving stone, and behind the desk a small, upright old lady with a pen." teaches quennell_named.
  Person quennell "Mrs. Amabel Quennell" at quennell_room {
    Role "keeper of the subscription library". Ancestry human. Background librarian. Peaceable. Manner acerbic. Pronouns she.
    Wants treatise_kind.
    Greets "'Subscription is two coin the quarter,' says the old lady without looking up, 'books to be returned within the fortnight, fines a penny a week, no reading in the bath. Are you joining?'".
  }
  Relation subscribed(group). Relation returned(entity).
  Action subscribe(actor: group, site: place) "Pay Mrs. Quennell two coin for a quarter's subscription" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(quennell, site) and not subscribed(actor) and quantity(actor, gold) >= 2.
    Time 2 minutes.
    On success { Decrease quantity(actor, gold) by 2. Increase quantity(quennell, gold) by 2. Assert subscribed(actor). }
    Result "Mrs. Quennell writes your name in the paving stone in a hand like a row of pins and gives you a pasteboard ticket. 'Number four thousand one hundred and six,' she says. 'Do not lose it. People lose them.'".
  }
  Discovery quarles_overdue "Mrs. Quennell's library has been fining Ottoline Quarles, now the Lyceum's Archivist, a penny a week for forty-one years for a book called A Treatise of the Inward Reading, by one Pollexfen, which she borrowed and never returned. The fine stands at more than eight crowns.".
  Topic quennell_fines with quennell "Ask Mrs. Quennell about her fines" when true once {
    Says "'Paid, mostly,' says Mrs. Quennell. 'People are frightened of me. It is the only use of being small.' She turns the paving stone back, and back, and back, to a page gone yellow. 'Except this one. Quarles, O., of the Lyceum, a Treatise of the Inward Reading, by Pollexfen, borrowed the ninth of Seedtime, forty-one years ago. A penny a week.' She runs her pen down a column of figures that goes off the bottom of the page. 'She is the Archivist now. I write to her at every quarter day. She sends me back a card that says, I have not finished it.' Mrs. Quennell closes the ledger. 'Forty-one years. Nobody reads that slowly. Not even at the Lyceum.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, quarles_overdue). }
  }
  Item pollexfen "the Treatise of the Inward Reading" is treatise_kind at quarles_room owned by quarles.
  Event treatise_home near quennell when keeps(quennell, pollexfen) and not returned(pollexfen) "Mrs. Quennell takes it in both hands. She opens it, looks at the label, closes it. For a moment she is not upright at all. 'Number two hundred and twelve,' she says. 'I was nineteen when I wrote that label.' Then she is upright again and writing in the ledger, RETURNED, and the date, and ruling a line under forty-one years of pennies. 'The fine is remitted,' she says, not looking up. 'Tell Ottoline. Tell her it was a pleasure to have it back.' She pushes a pasteboard ticket across the desk. 'A life subscription. I have never issued one. Do not lose it.'" then {
    Assert returned(pollexfen). Assert subscribed(party). Assert lifeTicket(party). }
  Relation lifeTicket(group).

  // ── The physic garden, and the bed the Imprimatur dug up. ──
  Observe physic_scene at physic_garden when true "A walled garden of square beds edged with box, each with a painted board: WOUNDWORT, FEVERFEW, HOUND'S-TONGUE, HEART'S-EASE. Bees. A sundial with a motto worn away. In the middle of the garden one bed is bare earth, raked, with nothing in it, and its board has been painted over and painted over again, and the old letters still show through.".
  Person gardener "Tobiah Mew" at physic_garden {
    Role "the physic gardener". Ancestry human. Background gardener. Peaceable. Manner stoic. Pronouns he.
    Greets "An old man on his knees in the feverfew sits back on his heels and wipes his hands. 'Garden's open. Don't pick anything. If you're ill, the apothecaries are in Newmarket.'".
  }
  Discovery orme_bed "In the physic garden there is a bed that has been bare for two hundred years: the Orme Bed, whose plants were dug up by order of the Imprimatur in the year Censor-General Pardoe cut the chapters from Mother Orme's Physick. Its old board still shows through the paint: CALAMUS. FEVERFEW. HONEY OF THE BOG-MYRTLE. AND ONE MORE, BURNT OFF.".
  Thing orme_board "the painted-over board" at physic_garden {
    State painted "The board at the bare bed has been painted over, and the paint has not quite taken.".
  }
  Observe board_close at physic_garden of orme_board when tried(orme_board, looking) "Close to, with the sun across it, the old letters come up through the paint: THE ORME BED. And under it, a list: CALAMUS. FEVERFEW. HONEY OF THE BOG-MYRTLE. And a fourth name that someone has burnt off with an iron, so that only the first letter is left, a capital H." teaches orme_bed.
  Topic gardener_bed with gardener "Ask Tobiah Mew about the bare bed" when true once {
    Says "'The Orme Bed,' says the old man. 'Nothing in it since Pardoe's time. Censor-General Pardoe. He cut the bits out of the Orme he didn't like and sent men to dig up what grew from them, so nobody could make it out of the garden instead.' He gets up, slowly. 'My father raked it every week and so do I. You keep a bed ready.' He looks at it. 'Nobody's ever said for what.'".
  }
  Relation replanted(entity).
  Observe bed_replanted at physic_garden when replanted(orme_board) "The Orme Bed is planted. Tobiah Mew has put in calamus from the Calamies and feverfew from the next bed and a pot of bog-myrtle from somewhere, and a fourth thing, small and grey-green, that he will not name to anybody, and he has painted the board fresh: THE ORME BED, RESTORED.".
}

data/adventures/parliament-of-books/hith:
Module calamy "A Parliament of Books" {
  Description "The Hithe: the river port on the south bank, its quay and tavern, its rags and gulls, its ferry, and the garrets of Pudding Lane.".

  // ── The first sight of the city, from the saltings. ──
  Observe causey_view at causey when not familiar(causey) "A raised causeway of black stones crosses the saltings toward a town of roofs and masts. Ahead of you a long bridge goes over the river on a great many arches, so crowded with sheds and stalls that it looks like a street that has wandered out over the water. Beyond it, a walled town climbs to a single hill with a tower on top; downriver, to your right, the reed-beds go grey-green all the way to the sea. Under a pair of mills two wheels turn slowly in the river. Gulls. The air smells of mud and, faintly and strangely, of wet paper.".
  Observe causey_again at causey when familiar(causey) "The causeway over the saltings, black stones and samphire, with the bridge and the hill ahead of you and the reeds going grey-green to the sea.".
  Event causey_cart at causey when true "A cart comes up behind you, piled with bales of rags, and the carter calls cheerfully as he passes: 'Rags for Calamy! Rags for paper, paper for books, books for the Kindling!' His horse does not care either way.".

  // ── The quay. ──
  Observe quay_scene at quay when not familiar(quay) "The Hithe quay is cobbled and wet and loud. Eel-boats lie two and three deep at the steps; a fishwife cries her baskets; a gang of boys rolls rag bales up a plank into a lighter. Along the back of the quay stand a tavern under a painted lantern, a customs house with a great barred warehouse behind it, and a crowd of people round a board nailed to a post. At the west end the steps go down to a ferry; at the east, the quay turns into a market of rags.".
  Observe quay_again at quay when familiar(quay) "The Hithe quay: eel-boats, gulls, the tavern under its lantern, the customs house, the notice post, and the bridge going north over the water.".
  Observe quay_night at quay when dark() "At night the quay is lamplit and a third as loud. The eel-boats knock together in the dark like men talking in their sleep, the tide works at the steps, and the Lamb's is the only window on the front still lit, with a cat in it.".
  Discovery bounty_known "The Imprimatur pays four pence for every book that speaks, sings, moves of itself or alters its own text, brought in to be burned. The notice is signed by H. Rhule, Deputy Censor." {
    Source quay_notice "the notice on the post" at quay readable by party.
  }
  Observe notice_scene at quay when true "On the post a printed notice, fresh, with the Imprimatur's little lamp stamped at the foot: UNQUIET VOLUMES. Under it someone has chalked a drawing of a book with legs, running, and someone else has rubbed most of it out.".

  Person bet "Bet Kettering" at quay {
    Role "fishwife". Ancestry human. Peaceable. Manner acerbic. Pronouns she.
    Greets "'Herring, dab, eel, a nice bit of skate,' says the fishwife, without looking up. 'And if you've come from that school on the hill you can turn round and walk back up it.'".
  }
  Relation fishLost(group). Relation heronSeen(group). Relation heronFollowed(group). Relation heronSettled(group).
  Discovery heron_suspected "Bet Kettering's fish go missing from her baskets every morning at the turn of the tide, and she blames the young gentlemen of the Lyceum.".
  Topic bet_trouble with bet "Ask the fishwife what she has against the school on the hill" when true once {
    Says "'Every morning,' says Bet Kettering, 'at the turn of the tide, I lose the best fish off the top of the basket. A dab. A nice eel. Gone, while I'm crying the skate. And every morning there's a pair of young gentlemen in gowns at the bridge foot, laughing.' She slaps a skate down. 'I've complained to the Provost. The Provost sent me a letter in Latin. I've had it read. It said nothing at all, in a great many words.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, heron_suspected). Assert fishLost(bet). }
  }
  // The heron comes for its breakfast at the turn of the morning tide: from ten to eleven.
  Person heron "a grey heron" at gannet_steps {
    Role "a heron with a brass ring on one leg". Species heron_kind. Peaceable.
  }
  Rule heron_comes when hour(clock) == 10 and not heronSettled(bet) and not at(heron, quay) { Assert at(heron, quay). }
  Rule heron_goes when hour(clock) == 11 and at(heron, quay) { Assert at(heron, quad). }
  Observe heron_steals at quay when at(heron, quay) and fishLost(bet) "On the roof of the customs house a grey heron stands on one leg, still, looking at Bet Kettering's baskets. While she turns to cry the skate it drops onto the quay in two long steps, takes the best eel off the top, and goes up again with it, and nobody but you sees it at all. There is a brass ring on its leg." teaches heron_seen.
  Discovery heron_seen "A grey heron with a brass ring on its leg takes Bet Kettering's fish at the turn of the tide. Somebody has ringed it; somebody keeps it.".
  Topic bet_heron with bet "Tell Bet Kettering it is a heron that takes her fish" when knows(actor, heron_seen) and not heronSettled(bet) once {
    Says "Bet looks up at the customs-house roof for a long moment. 'A heron,' she says. 'With a ring on.' She wipes her hands. 'Herons don't wear rings. Gentlemen ring herons.' She is already untying her apron. 'Mind the basket.'".
  }
  Discovery heron_home "The ringed heron flies up to the Lyceum on Gannet Hill, where it lands in the quad.".
  Initially Assert leadsTo(heron_home, quad).
  Observe heron_flies at quay when at(heron, quay) and knows(party, heron_seen) and not knows(party, heron_home) "The heron goes up off the roof heavily, with its eel, and flaps away north over the bridge toward the hill with the tower on it." teaches heron_home.

  Person moll "Moll Garrow" at taproom {
    Role "the innkeeper of the Lamb and Lantern". Ancestry human. Profession innkeeper. Peaceable. Manner jovial. Pronouns she.
    Sells rations for 2 coin from 12.
    Sells candle for 1 coin from 10.
    Greets "'Come in, come in, shut the door on the gulls,' says a big woman behind the bar, drying a pot. 'Ale or small beer or tea, and there's eel pie, and if you want a bed it's the loft. Don't mind the almanack.'".
  }
  Topic moll_almanack with moll "Ask Moll Garrow why you are not to mind the almanack" when true once {
    Says "'It talks,' says Moll Garrow, as if you had asked why not to mind the cat. 'Since Candlemas. All of them do, Doble's ones, the penny almanacks; every kitchen in the Hithe has one going on about the tides. Some say it's the printer playing a trick with a reed in the binding. Some say it's devils. The Binders say it's because they're not sewn proper, but the Binders would say that about the moon.' She hangs up the pot. 'Mine's no trouble. It knows when my Tom's birthday was. He's been dead nine years. I'd forgotten it myself.'".
  }
  Discovery kindling_custom "At the Kindling, the turn of Calamy's year, every household throws last year's almanack on the great fire in the Ashpit, and lights the new year's lamp from it. It is nine days off.".
  Topic moll_kindling with moll "Ask Moll Garrow about the Kindling" when true once {
    Says "'The Kindling? You'll be here for it, then. Nine days. Every house in Calamy brings its old almanack to the Ashpit on Kindling night and throws it on the fire, and you light your lamp from the fire and carry it home lit, and the new year's in. My gran did it. Her gran did.' She looks at the chimney-corner and lowers her voice. 'This year I don't know. You can't very well throw a thing on the fire that says good morning to you.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, kindling_custom). }
  }
  Topic moll_city with moll "Ask Moll Garrow how a stranger gets about in Calamy" when true once {
    Says "'Bridge north to the old town: Stitchgate for binders and ink, the Close for the church and the courts and the Censor, the hill for the Lyceum. Newmarket's the printers', outside the wall, and the Gardens is the rich. Here's the Hithe.' She ticks them off on wet fingers. 'Bridge gates shut at ten and open at five. Jonah Pellow used to ferry at night, before they took his licence. Out on the flats at low water there's the reeds, but you want Coddy Fitch with you out there, or the tide will have you.'".
  }
  Topic moll_censor with moll "Ask Moll Garrow about the Deputy Censor" when knows(actor, bounty_known) once {
    Says "'Mistress Rhule? Binder's girl. Her father bound half the books in the Close. Clever, and straight as a ruler, and about as much fun.' Moll considers. 'She burns them on the Ashpit, the talking ones, every Thursday. People bring them in for the four pence. I don't hold with it. But then I didn't hold with the new bridge tolls either, and here we all are.'".
  }

  // ── The rag market: Old Hennet, and a letter she cannot read. ──
  Observe rags_scene at rag_market when true "Bales of rags stand in walls along the quay's end, sorted by colour: white for the best paper, blue and brown for wrapping, black for nobody knows what. Women sit on the bales with knives, unpicking seams. A buyer from the mills goes along with a hook and a tally-stick, arguing.".
  Person hennet "Old Hennet" at rag_market {
    Role "rag-picker". Ancestry human. Peaceable. Manner cheerful. Pronouns she.
    Greets "An old woman on a bale of white linen looks up from her unpicking. 'Good day, my dove. Nothing to sell you, unless you want a shirt with no back to it.'".
  }
  Relation readTo(group). Relation readToSomeone(group).
  Discovery hennet_letter "Old Hennet keeps a letter from her son at sea that she cannot read, and has not liked to ask anyone to read it to her, in case it is bad news.".
  Topic hennet_talk with hennet "Ask Old Hennet how the rag trade goes" when true once {
    Says "'Up,' says Old Hennet. 'Up, up. Doble's press eats white linen like a goat eats washing. Half Calamy's shirts are in his almanacks.' She laughs, and then fingers something in her apron. 'My boy's at sea, out of Ostrey. He sent me a letter, look.' It is folded small and very soft. 'I've not had it read. I never learnt. And if it's bad news, well. I'd sooner have it be good news a bit longer.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, hennet_letter). }
  }
  Topic hennet_read with hennet "Offer to read Old Hennet her son's letter" when knows(actor, hennet_letter) and not readTo(hennet) once {
    Says "She hands it to you as if it might break. It is in a sailor's careful capitals. DEAR MOTHER I AM WELL THE SHIP IS SOUND WE PUT IN AT OSTREY BEFORE THE KINDLING AND I WILL WALK OVER TO YOU FOR IT AND BRING YOU A SHAWL THE BLUE ONE YOUR LOVING SON NED. You read it twice, because she asks. Then she takes it back and puts it away in her apron without a word and goes on unpicking a seam, and does not look up for a while.".
    On discuss { Assert readTo(hennet). Assert readToSomeone(actor). }
  }
  Observe hennet_shawl at rag_market when readTo(hennet) and day(clock) >= 9 "Old Hennet is on her bale in a new blue shawl, telling everyone who comes past that it is from Ostrey.".

  // ── The ferry, and the curfew on the bridge. ──
  Observe ferry_scene at ferry_stairs when true "Green steps go down into the river between two piles hung with weed. A flat black ferry-boat is tied at the bottom, and on the top step a board says J. PELLOW, FERRYMAN, PENNY A HEAD. Across the board somebody has chalked NOT AFTER DARK. LICENCE LAPSED. BY ORDER.".
  Person jonah "Jonah Pellow" at ferry_stairs {
    Role "ferryman". Ancestry human. Profession boatman. Peaceable. Manner grim. Pronouns he.
    Greets "'Penny a head,' says the ferryman, 'daylight only, by order of the Stationers' Hall, God rot it.'".
  }
  Relation licensed(group). Measure licence(group).
  Initially Set licence(jonah) to 6.
  Discovery jonah_licence "Jonah Pellow's licence to ferry at night lapsed at Michaelmas; renewing it costs six coin at the Stationers' Hall, which he has spent on his daughter's apprenticeship to a binder.".
  Topic jonah_why with jonah "Ask the ferryman why not after dark" when true once {
    Says "'Licence,' says Jonah Pellow. 'Six coin to the Stationers' Hall at Michaelmas for the night ferry, and I didn't have it, because I'd paid it to the Binders for my Annet's indentures.' He spits into the river, carefully, downstream. 'So now there's no crossing at night, bridge gates shut at ten, and I sit here at dark with a boat and a daughter who sews books all day and cries all night because the Warden shouts. Good bargain, that.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, jonah_licence). }
  }
  Action pay_licence(actor: group, site: place) "Give Jonah Pellow the six coin for his night licence" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(jonah, site) and knows(actor, jonah_licence)
      and not licensed(jonah) and quantity(actor, gold) >= 6.
    Time 5 minutes.
    On success { Decrease quantity(actor, gold) by 6. Increase quantity(jonah, gold) by 6. Assert licensed(jonah). }
    Result "Jonah Pellow looks at the coins in his palm for a while. 'I'll pay it at the Stationers' Hall in the morning,' he says, 'and I'll ferry you for nothing at any hour you like till I'm dead, and after that my Annet will.' He rubs the chalk off his board with his sleeve.".
  }
  Observe ferry_licensed at ferry_stairs when licensed(jonah) "The chalk is gone from Jonah Pellow's board. Under PENNY A HEAD he has painted, very neatly, NIGHTS ALSO.".
  // By night, with his licence, Jonah takes the company over to the Water Gate stairs under the Close, and back.
  Place watergate "the Water Gate stairs" is room within close at 0, 3 outdoors.
  Passage water_close "the Water Gate" is gate between watergate and close_green taking 3 minutes.
  Passage night_ferry "Jonah's ferry" is ferry between ferry_stairs and watergate taking 15 minutes
    opening when licensed(jonah) or not dark() "Jonah Pellow's boat is tied up for the night. He will not take it out after dark without his licence.".
  Observe watergate_scene at watergate when true "Wet steps up from the river into a low arch in the wall of the Close. At the top an iron gate, open; through it, grass and the dark bulk of a church.".

  // The bridge gates: shut at ten, open at five. The gatekeeper can be persuaded, for a penny or two.
  Thing bridge_chain "the chain across the bridge gate" on bridge_span_south {
    State down "The bridge gate stands open, the chain coiled on its post.".
    State up blocking "The bridge gate is shut for the night, the chain across it and the gatekeeper's lamp burning in his box.".
  }
  Rule chain_up when (hour(clock) >= 22 or hour(clock) < 5) and state(bridge_chain, down) and not gateBribed(bridge_chain) { Assert state(bridge_chain, up). }
  Rule chain_down when hour(clock) >= 5 and hour(clock) < 22 and state(bridge_chain, up) { Assert state(bridge_chain, down). Retract gateBribed(bridge_chain). }
  Relation gateBribed(entity).
  Action bribe_gate(actor: group, site: place) "Give the bridge gatekeeper two coin to let you through" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and (at(actor, quay) or at(actor, bridge_middle))
      and state(bridge_chain, up) and quantity(actor, gold) >= 2.
    Time 2 minutes.
    On success { Decrease quantity(actor, gold) by 2. Assert gateBribed(bridge_chain). Assert state(bridge_chain, down). }
    Result "A head in a nightcap comes out of the box, considers your coins, takes them, and lowers the chain without a word.".
  }

  // ── Pudding Lane: the ague, the midwife, the last Annery. ──
  Observe pudding_scene at pudding_lane when true "Pudding Lane climbs from the rag market between houses so tall and so close that their top storeys nearly touch, with washing strung across the gap. On a step halfway up a little girl is wrapped in a blanket in the sun, shivering, and a woman beside her is feeding her something from a spoon. There is a green door with a horseshoe on it, and further up a narrow house with a stair going up and up inside it to the leads.".
  Discovery ague_known "There is ague in the Hithe: a shaking fever among the children of Pudding Lane and the quay, five of them down with it and more coming.".
  Observe ague_seen at pudding_lane when not cured(nance) "The girl on the step shakes so that her teeth click. Her mother looks at you, and at your clothes, and away." teaches ague_known.
  Person nance "Goody Nance" at nance_room {
    Role "midwife and nurse of the Hithe". Ancestry human. Profession herbalist. Peaceable. Manner compassionate. Pronouns she.
    Wants pages_kind. Wants calamus_kind.
    Greets "A small square woman with her sleeves rolled up and a kettle on the hob looks you up and down. 'Sick, or sent for?'".
  }
  Relation cured(group).
  Discovery orme_remedy "Goody Nance's grandmother cured the Hithe ague with a remedy out of Mother Orme's Physick, from a chapter that is not in any copy now: the Imprimatur cut it, generations ago. All Nance remembers is that it wanted calamus root dug at low water.".
  Topic nance_ague with nance "Ask Goody Nance about the ague" when knows(actor, ague_known) once {
    Says "'Marsh ague,' says Goody Nance. 'Comes up off the flats every spring or two. Mostly it passes. This year it doesn't.' She pours water onto leaves. 'My gran had a cure for it out of Orme. Mother Orme's Physick, the old herbal; there's a copy up at the Lyceum and one in every apothecary's in Calamy and not one of them has the chapter. Cut. The Imprimatur cut it out, I don't know when, before my gran was born. She had it by heart.' She sets the cup down. 'I don't. I was nine. Calamus root, dug at the bottom of the tide. And something after that. I've lain awake on it.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, orme_remedy). }
  }
  Observe nance_scene at nance_room when true "A front room that is half kitchen and half sick-bay: a truckle bed with a child asleep in it, bunches of herbs under the ceiling, a scrubbed table, a kettle. On the wall by the hearth hangs a penny almanack on a nail, and a framed sampler: PHYSICIAN HEAL THY SELF, AND THEN THE REST OF US." teaches nance_named.
  Discovery jonquil_known "A young man is dying of a consumption in a garret at the top of Pudding Lane: Jonquil Annery, the last of the Annerys, who were a great Hithe family once.".
  Initially Assert leadsTo(jonquil_known, jonquil_garret).
  Topic nance_garret with nance "Ask Goody Nance who coughs at the top of the lane" when true once {
    Says "'Jonquil Annery,' says Goody Nance. 'In the garret at the top. Consumption. He'll not see midsummer, and he knows it, and he's merrier about it than I am.' She sniffs. 'The Annerys had a ship and a warehouse and a pew in the Lamp when my mother was a girl. He's the last. Drank it and diced it, and sold the last of it last winter, the house and the furniture and the family book. Nobody sells their Housebook. He did.' She shakes her head. 'Take him some broth if you're going up. He won't eat it, but he'll like being brought it.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, jonquil_known). }
  }

  // Jonquil Annery: he sold his mother's Housebook to Sir Gideon Corvell for forty crowns, to pay a man named Vosper.
  Person jonquil "Jonquil Annery" at jonquil_garret {
    Role "the last of the Annerys". Ancestry human. Peaceable. Manner cheerful. Pronouns he.
    Greets "'Visitors!' says a young man in a bed under the sloping ceiling, propped on four pillows and all of them grey. 'You'll forgive me not getting up. I've given it up for the spring. Sit on the chest, it's the only thing I didn't sell.'".
  }
  Observe garret_scene at jonquil_garret when true "A garret under the leads, with a window you could put your head out of and see the whole river. A bed, a chest, a jug, a heap of very good clothes gone shabby. On the windowsill a row of empty bottles, sorted by colour, like a rag market.".
  Discovery housebook_sold "Jonquil Annery sold the Annery Housebook, four hundred years of his family's births, debts and recipes in his mothers' hands, to Sir Gideon Corvell for forty crowns last winter, to pay a gambling debt.".
  Topic jonquil_book with jonquil "Ask Jonquil Annery about his family's Housebook" when knows(actor, jonquil_known) once {
    Says "He laughs, and then coughs, and then laughs again when he can. 'You've been talking to Nance. Yes. I sold it. Four hundred years of Annery women writing down who was born and who owed what and how to pickle a herring, and I sold it to Gideon Corvell for forty crowns to pay a man called Vosper who had my note.' He looks at the window. 'It's the only thing I'm ashamed of, and I've done some very shameful things. My mother's hand is in it. Her last recipe is for a posset for a cough. Funny.' He coughs, and waits it out. 'Corvell won't sell it back. I asked. He keeps it in a glass case and shows it to people as a curiosity. The last of the Annerys.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, housebook_sold). }
  }
  Relation wantsBook(group).
  Topic jonquil_wish with jonquil "Ask Jonquil Annery what he would do with it, if he had it again" when knows(actor, housebook_sold) once {
    Says "'Read it,' he says at once. 'All of it. From the first page, Great-great-something-Agnes and her herrings, to my mother's posset.' He is quiet a while. 'I never read it, you know. You don't, when it's yours. It sits in the kitchen and your mother writes in it and you think, there's time.' He turns his face to the window. 'If you could get it from Corvell, even for a day, I'd die owing you a great deal, which is the only way I know how to die.'".
    On discuss { Assert wantsBook(jonquil). }
  }
  // When the company knows the Housebook has gone where Corvell's books went, Jonquil writes to it.
  Item jonquil_letter "Jonquil Annery's letter to his mother's book" is calamy_letter at jonquil_garret hidden until letter_given.
  Discovery letter_given "Jonquil Annery has written a letter to his family's Housebook, wherever it has gone, and given it to you to carry.".
  Relation letterWritten(group).
  Topic jonquil_write with jonquil "Tell Jonquil Annery where his family's book has gone" when wantsBook(jonquil) and knows(actor, housebook_gone) and not letterWritten(jonquil) once {
    Says "He listens with his eyes shut. When you have finished he is quiet so long you think he is asleep. 'Gone home,' he says at last. 'Somewhere books go. Well. It always did like its own company better than ours.' He opens his eyes. 'Give me the pen, and that bit of paper, the back of the bill from the wine-merchant.' He writes slowly, stopping to cough, and crosses out one word and puts it back. Then he folds it and seals it with a candle-end, and holds it out to you. 'If you ever go where it's gone. It's to my mother's book, not my mother. I know the difference.' He smiles. 'It won't want to read it. Tell it I said it doesn't have to. Tell it I'd like it to.'".
    On discuss { Assert letterWritten(jonquil). Assert knows(actor, letter_given). }
  }
  Observe letter_left at jonquil_garret when at(jonquil_letter, jonquil_garret) and knows(party, letter_given) "On the chest, where Jonquil put it down for you, a letter sealed with candle-wax.".
  // He does not see midsummer, but he sees the Kindling, unless the ague goes up the lane first.

  // A traveller's guide, left on a settle at the Lamb: the city as a visitor is told it.
  Thing travel_guide "a traveller's guide-book left on the settle" at taproom {
    State left "On the settle by the window somebody has left a little guide-book, much thumbed.".
  }
  Observe travel_guide_read at taproom of travel_guide as reading when tried(travel_guide, looking) "THE STRANGER'S COMPANION TO CALAMY, ITS SIGHTS AND CUSTOMS (Doble, Newmarket; price twopence). The traveller arriving by the causeway should take a room at the Lamb and Lantern, where the almanack on the nail is reliable as to tides. THE PENNY BRIDGE: ballads, pies, and the chapel pier, where the children's Kindling rhyme may be heard; the traveller is warned not to ask for the second verse. STITCHGATE: the Binders' Hall; Widow Gallon's ink, the blackest in the world; and Mr. Crake's bookshop, the Two Hands, where the curious will find anything, and the prudent will not go beyond the seventh bay of the back room, which is reputed not to end. THE CLOSE: the Church of the Lamp, whose lamp has never gone out, and the slab of Censor-General Pardoe, which visitors are asked not to stand on; the Court of Pleas; the Imprimatur, which licenses books, and on Thursdays burns them. GANNET HILL: the Lyceum, where the Archivist, Miss Quarles, has been the Archivist since before the oldest fellow was born; visitors may see the Chained Room on application to the Provost, and the window of Dr. Fenwick, who went to map Mr. Crake's back room and is expected back. THE KINDLING, on the ninth night after the March tide: the whole city burns last year's almanack on the Ashpit. It is very jolly.".
}

data/adventures/parliament-of-books/impr:
Module calamy "A Parliament of Books" {
  Description "The Imprimatur and the Ashpit: the Deputy Censor, her burnings, her kitchen, and the Cupboard of Excisions.".

  Observe ashpit_scene at ashpit when true "A square of beaten earth in the middle of the Close, black in the middle, with the Church of the Lamp on one side, the Stationers' Hall on another, a grey building with barred windows and a little lamp carved over its door on a third, and the Court steps going up on the fourth. In the black middle men are building a bonfire of faggots and old timber, a great cone of it, higher every day, for the Kindling.".
  Observe pyre_grows at ashpit when day(clock) >= 5 and not kindlingDone(ashpit) "The Kindling fire stands twice the height of a man now, faggots and broken furniture and old shutters, with a ladder against it and a boy on the ladder.".
  Discovery ash_lines "The ash from the Thursday brazier at the Ashpit does not blow away. It lies on the beaten earth in fine straight grey lines, like the lines of a page, and the sweepers go round it.".
  Observe ash_in_lines at ashpit when burnings(rhule) >= 1 and not burningNow() and not kindlingDone(ashpit) "By the cold brazier at the edge of the Ashpit the ash of Thursday's burning has not blown away, though the wind is off the river. It lies on the beaten earth in fine straight lines, grey, close together, like the lines of a page; and the sweepers have swept round it." teaches ash_lines.
  Relation kindlingDone(place).
  Observe imp_scene at imp_office when true "A long grey room with a counter down it, and behind the counter clerks in grey stamping papers with a little brass lamp: LICENSED. LICENSED. REFUSED. On the wall a great framed list in small letters, INDEX OF BOOKS REFUSED, ADDED TO SINCE THE YEAR 1060, and beside it a newer board with a slot in it like a poor-box: UNQUIET VOLUMES HERE. FOUR PENCE PAID. A door behind the counter; a cold passage to a kitchen; steps going down." teaches imprimatur_named.
  Observe imp_letters at imp_office when true "Behind the counter, on its own shelf, a row of grey boxes tied with tape and sealed with the little lamp, each with a label in an old hand: CORRESPONDENCE OF THE CENSOR-GENERAL WITH THE CENSORS OF HALLAM. OF ORSK. OF THE SALT TOWNS. OF TESSALY (UNANSWERED). 1132-1140. NOT TO BE OPENED.".
  Person clerk "the clerk at the counter" at imp_office {
    Role "a clerk of the Imprimatur". Ancestry human. Background scribe. Peaceable. Manner stoic. Pronouns they.
    Buys almanack_copy for 1 coin.
    Greets "A clerk in grey looks up from a ledger. 'Licences, left. Refusals, right. Unquiet volumes, the box, four pence, and mind your fingers, some of them bite.'".
  }
  Topic clerk_box with clerk "Ask the clerk about the box for unquiet volumes" when true once {
    Says "'Four pence a volume, paid at this counter, no name asked, a receipt if you want one, and nobody does.' The clerk does not look up from the ledger. 'Almanacks, this year, mostly. Those I give a penny for; they are not worth four, and there are three thousand of them.' A stamp comes down: REFUSED. 'They go downstairs in a sack. On Thursday the Deputy Censor takes what she judges to the Ashpit. The rest are kept for the Kindling.' The box in the wall gives a small dry cough. 'Some of them bite,' says the clerk. 'I did say.'".
  }
  Topic clerk_licence with clerk "Ask the clerk how a book is licensed" when true once {
    Says "The clerk counts it off on inky fingers. 'A copy to this counter, a fee of a shilling, a reader's fortnight. The reader reads it for anything against the Parliament or the Lamp, and for anything that moves. If it says nothing it should not: LICENSED. If it says something it should not, the cuts are marked in red and you may print it cut.' They glance at the slot in the wall. 'If it says something by itself, it is not a licence you want. It is the box.'".
  }
  Observe imp_kitchen_scene at imp_kitchen when true "A big cold kitchen with a scrubbed table, a banked fire, a dresser of plain plates, and on a nail by the chimney a penny almanack, curling. On the table there is a single candlestick with the candle burnt right down, and a chair pushed back as if someone got up from it in a hurry.".

  // ── Hester Rhule. ──
  Person rhule "Hester Rhule" at rhule_room {
    Role "Deputy Censor of the Imprimatur". Ancestry human. Calling warrior. Level 3. Trait strong_willed. Background magistrate. Peaceable. Manner stoic. Pronouns she.
    Greets "A woman in grey sits at a plain table with a book open in front of her and a pen across it, very straight, still. She has a binder's square hands. 'Hester Rhule. Deputy Censor.' She does not get up. 'If you have come about a licence, you have come to the wrong room. If you have come about an unquiet volume, you have come to the right one. Sit down.'".
    Objects "Hester Rhule looks at {?i} in your hand, and then at you, and says, very evenly: 'That is the Imprimatur's.'".
  }
  Observe rhule_room_scene at rhule_room when true "A small square room with a plain table, two chairs, a cot made up tight, a shelf of law-books, and a grey door in the far wall with a good lock on it. On the table, open, a book with its pages cut out: a neat ladder of stubs where whole chapters were.".
  Measure doubt(group).
  Relation liedTo(group). Relation rhuleMet(group).
  Discovery rhule_view "Hester Rhule believes the quickening spreads from book to book like rot; that a book which alters its own text is a lie that cannot be corrected; and that she burns unquiet volumes to save the rest from the mob that would burn them all.".
  Topic rhule_unquiet with rhule "Ask the Deputy Censor what an unquiet volume is" when true once {
    Says "'A book that does what a book should not do,' says Hester Rhule. 'Speaks. Sings. Moves. Alters what it says.' She lays her hand flat on the cut book in front of her. 'That last is the one that matters. A book that can change its own text is a lie nobody can correct. Your will says one thing on Monday and another on Tuesday. Your law says what it likes.' She looks at you directly for the first time. 'It spreads. It began with Doble's and now there is a bestiary in the Customs bond that has been Sewn for six hundred years, talking. I burn them on Thursdays, in public, so that people see it done properly, by the Imprimatur, and do not take it into their own hands and burn everything with a voice. I have seen what a frightened street does with a fire.' A pause. 'I was fourteen.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, rhule_view). Assert rhuleMet(rhule). }
  }
  Topic rhule_father with rhule "Ask Hester Rhule about her father" when knows(actor, rhule_view) once {
    Says "'A binder. Needle Street.' Rhule's square hands lie still on the table. 'He could Sew a book so it would last a thousand years. He taught me, before they let girls in the Company and after, when nobody was looking.' She almost smiles. 'He thought the Imprimatur was a necessary evil. He said, every book is a little dangerous, Hessie, that's what they're for. Somebody has to say which ones are too dangerous.' She turns the cut book's pages over, stub after stub. 'He did not think that somebody should be his daughter.'".
  }

  // Burnings on Thursdays: the second, fifth and eighth days, at noon, unless she has stopped them.
  Relation burningsStopped(group). Relation burningSeen(group). Measure burnings(group).
  Person crowd "the crowd at the Ashpit" at imp_cells {
    Role "Calamy, come to watch". Ancestry human. Numbers 40. Peaceable.
    Seen when burningNow().
  }
  Query burningNow() means at(rhule, ashpit) and hour(clock) == 12.
  Rule rhule_to_burn when hour(clock) == 12 and (day(clock) == 2 or day(clock) == 5 or day(clock) == 8) and not burningsStopped(rhule) and at(rhule, rhule_room) {
    Assert at(rhule, ashpit). Assert at(crowd, ashpit). Increase burnings(rhule) by 1. }
  Rule rhule_from_burn when hour(clock) == 13 and at(rhule, ashpit) and not kindlingNight() { Assert at(rhule, rhule_room). Assert at(crowd, imp_cells). }
  Query kindlingNight() means day(clock) == 9 and hour(clock) >= 18.
  Observe burning_scene at ashpit when burningNow() "At the edge of the Ashpit, away from the great Kindling pile, a brazier burns, and Hester Rhule stands beside it in grey with a clerk holding a basket. The crowd stands back. From the basket she takes the week's unquiet volumes one by one and reads out each title in a clear voice, as at a funeral, and puts it in the fire. A book of hours. A ready-reckoner. A penny almanack, then another, then six more.".
  Rule almanacks_cry at ashpit when burningNow() and not burningSeen(party)
    says "As the first almanack goes into the brazier there is a sound: not from the fire, but from everywhere, faint and thin and all at once, from every kitchen round the Close, from the Imprimatur's own windows, from the Lamb across the river for all you know. A cry. Then another as the second goes in. The crowd shifts. Somebody laughs, the wrong way. Hester Rhule's hand does not stop." {
    Assert burningSeen(party). }
  Rule almanack_hurt_pocket when burningNow() and carries(party, ?c) and itemKind(?c, almanack_copy) and at(party, ashpit) and not hurtInPocket(party)
    says "In your pocket the almanack makes a sound you will not forget, a thin dry shriek, as if a page were being torn slowly; and then another, as the next goes in." {
    Assert hurtInPocket(party). }
  Relation hurtInPocket(group).
  Topic rhule_heard with rhule "Ask Hester Rhule what she hears when the almanacks go into the fire" when burningSeen(actor) and not heardCry(rhule) once {
    Says "Rhule is quiet for a long moment. 'The glue,' she says. 'Doble's glue, boiling in the spines. It whistles.' She looks at her hands. 'It does not whistle in the Hithe. It does not whistle in my kitchen, across the Close, with the window shut.' Another silence. 'I hear it. Every Thursday I hear it from my kitchen, at night, after. The almanack on the nail, crying out when nobody's burning anything, as if it were remembering.' She looks up. 'I have told myself it is the glue for eight weeks. You are the first person who has asked.'".
    On discuss { Assert heardCry(rhule). Increase doubt(rhule) by 1. }
  }
  Relation heardCry(group).

  // At night she reads in her kitchen; then she sleeps in her room.
  Rule rhule_to_kitchen when hour(clock) == 20 and at(rhule, rhule_room) and not travelsWith(rhule, party) and not rhuleGone(rhule) { Assert at(rhule, imp_kitchen). }
  Rule rhule_to_bed when hour(clock) == 2 and at(rhule, imp_kitchen) { Assert at(rhule, rhule_room). Assert asleep(rhule). }
  Rule rhule_wakes when hour(clock) == 7 and asleep(rhule) { Retract asleep(rhule). }
  Relation rhuleGone(group).
  Observe rhule_reading at imp_kitchen when at(rhule, imp_kitchen) "At the scrubbed table, by one candle, Hester Rhule sits in her shirtsleeves with a sheaf of loose pages in front of her, cut pages, their edges ragged where a knife went down a margin. She is reading. She does not hear you come in for a moment. When she does, she puts her hand flat on the pages, as a child does on a letter.".
  Discovery rhule_reads "Hester Rhule sits up in the Imprimatur's kitchen at night reading pages cut out of books: the Imprimatur's excisions, which she is supposed to keep locked away unread.".
  Observe rhule_caught at imp_kitchen when at(rhule, imp_kitchen) "She is reading the pages the Imprimatur cut." teaches rhule_reads.

  // The Cupboard of Excisions, behind the grey door.
  Thing excision_lock "the lock on the grey door" on excision_door {
    State locked blocking "The grey door in the Censor's room is locked: a good lock, brass, newly oiled.".
    State open "The grey door stands open.".
    Change pick "Pick the lock on the grey door" from locked to open needing lockwork.
  }
  Observe excisions_scene at excisions when true "A narrow room lined to the ceiling with shallow drawers, hundreds of them, each with a brass label-holder and a card: the name of a book, the chapters, a year, a censor's initials. VARNE, LANTERN-BEARERS, CANTO II LINES 1-40, 1206, R.T. AMBERY, LOST SERMONS, ALL, 1150, P. ORME, PHYSICK, CAPP. XIV-XVII, 1131, PARDOE. The air smells of old paper and something sharper, like a room where someone has been crying. One drawer stands a little open.".
  Discovery orme_cut "In the Cupboard of Excisions, in the drawer marked ORME, PHYSICK, CAPP. XIV-XVII, lie the four chapters Censor-General Pardoe cut from Mother Orme's Physick in 1131, with his note: XIV to XVI cut for impiety, as touching the bodies of women and the hindering of birth; XVII cut for that it teacheth the making of the Grey Water, a poison past cure. XV is the remedy for the marsh ague.".
  Discovery canto_cut "In the Cupboard of Excisions lie forty lines of the second canto of Isolde Varne's Lantern-Bearers, cut in 1206 from the papers she left when she died, by a censor who found them seditious. The second canto was begun.".
  Item orme_pages "Orme's cut chapters" is pages_kind at excisions owned by rhule.
  Item canto_lines "the forty cut lines of Varne's second canto" is pages_kind at excisions owned by rhule.
  Observe orme_drawer at excisions when at(orme_pages, excisions) "In the open drawer, ORME, a bundle of pages tied with tape, and a slip in a crabbed old hand pinned to the top." teaches orme_cut.
  Observe varne_drawer at excisions when at(canto_lines, excisions) "Further along, a drawer marked VARNE, LANTERN-BEARERS, CANTO II LINES 1-40, and in it a single folded sheet in a woman's quick hand, the ink gone brown, the last line breaking off in the middle." teaches canto_cut.

  // How the company may come by the pages: asked for, or taken.
  Relation pagesGiven(group). Relation cupboardShown(group).
  Topic rhule_cupboard with rhule "Ask Hester Rhule about the pages she reads at night" when (knows(actor, rhule_reads) or knows(actor, cupboard_known)) and not cupboardShown(rhule) and doubt(rhule) >= 2 once {
    Says "Rhule goes still. Then she gets up, and takes a key from inside her collar on a cord, and opens the grey door. 'The Cupboard of Excisions,' she says. 'Every page the Imprimatur has cut from a book in three hundred years. We are to keep them and never read them, so that what was cut can never be said to have been destroyed.' She stands in the door with her back to you. 'I read them. Every night for six years. Some of them were cut for nothing at all. A joke about a bishop. A woman's name.' Her voice does not change. 'Some of them were cut because they would kill people. I have not been able to tell which is which, by reading. I have tried.'".
    On discuss { Assert cupboardShown(rhule). Assert state(excision_lock, open). Assert knows(actor, cupboard_known). }
  }
  Topic rhule_cupboard_denied with rhule "Ask Hester Rhule about the pages she reads at night" when (knows(actor, rhule_reads) or knows(actor, cupboard_known)) and not cupboardShown(rhule) and doubt(rhule) < 2 once {
    Says "'I read the law,' says Rhule, perfectly evenly, and you know she is lying, and she knows you know. 'You are mistaken about the hour, and about the pages.' She picks up her pen. 'Good day.'".
  }
  Topic rhule_orme with rhule "Ask Hester Rhule for Orme's cut chapter on the marsh ague, for the children of the Hithe" when cupboardShown(rhule) and knows(actor, orme_remedy) and not pagesGiven(rhule) once {
    Says "Rhule opens the ORME drawer and takes out the bundle and unpins Pardoe's slip and reads it to you, though she must know it by heart. Impiety. The Grey Water, a poison past cure. 'Chapter fifteen is the ague,' she says. 'I know. I have read it a hundred times and thought of the Hithe.' She holds the bundle a moment. 'Seventeen is the poison. Pardoe was right about seventeen.' She takes seventeen out, and puts it back in the drawer, and gives you the rest. 'Take it to whoever needs it. I will lend it. If anyone asks, the Imprimatur lent it.' She closes the drawer. 'That has never been said in this room.'".
    On discuss { Assert pagesGiven(rhule). Assert lends(rhule, orme_pages, actor). Assert ormePoisonKept(rhule). }
  }
  Relation ormePoisonKept(group).
  Topic rhule_canto with rhule "Ask Hester Rhule for the forty lines of Varne's second canto" when cupboardShown(rhule) and knows(actor, canto_cut) once {
    Says "'Varne.' Rhule takes the sheet out and holds it to the window. 'Seditious, R.T. wrote. I have read it nine times looking for the sedition. I think it was this line.' She points: a line about a lamp carried into a dark house, and the house waking. 'He thought she meant the Duke. I think she meant nothing at all; I think she meant a lamp.' She hands it to you. 'Lent. Bring it back, or bring me word of where it went.'".
    On discuss { Assert lends(rhule, canto_lines, actor). }
  }

  // Who told the company about the Cupboard, if Rhule asks: the truth, or a lie.
  Relation askedSource(group).
  Topic rhule_who with rhule "Tell Hester Rhule how you knew about the Cupboard" when cupboardShown(rhule) and knows(actor, almanack_secret) and not askedSource(rhule) once {
    Says "You tell her: the almanack on the nail in her kitchen. Rhule turns and looks through the open door, down the cold passage, toward the kitchen. 'It heard me,' she says. 'Every night.' She does not seem angry. She seems like someone who has put a hand out in the dark and found another hand. 'Every night for eight weeks something was listening to me read what nobody was meant to read, and it did not tell anyone until it had a reason.' She is quiet. 'I would not have been so careful.'".
    On discuss { Assert askedSource(rhule). Increase doubt(rhule) by 1. }
  }
  Topic rhule_who_lie with rhule "Tell Hester Rhule that Absalom Crake told you about the Cupboard" when cupboardShown(rhule) and knows(actor, almanack_secret) and not askedSource(rhule) once {
    Says "Rhule looks at you with no expression at all. 'Crake,' she says. 'Crake has never been in this building in his life.' She writes something in the margin of the cut book, one word, small. 'Very well.' She does not ask again, and she does not believe you, and you can see that she has filed it, the way she files everything.".
    On discuss { Assert askedSource(rhule). Assert liedTo(rhule). }
  }

  // Rhule's turnings, by what she has been shown.
  Topic rhule_ruling with rhule "Tell Hester Rhule the Court of Pleas has ruled a book may plead" when paskRuled(court) and not toldRuling(rhule) once {
    Says "'I heard.' Rhule's hand lies on the cut book. 'Haddon ruled that a book may be heard in its own cause. A treatise on sheep.' She almost smiles. 'If a book may plead in the Court of Pleas, then it is someone the law can wrong. And if the law can wrong it, I can.' She closes the cut book. 'I have been burning litigants.'".
    On discuss { Assert toldRuling(rhule). Increase doubt(rhule) by 1. }
  }
  Relation toldRuling(group).
  Topic rhule_colophon with rhule "Tell Hester Rhule the first term of the Colophon: no book burned in Calamy save by its own leave" when knows(actor, kindling_is_rite) and not toldColophon(rhule) once {
    Says "Hester Rhule listens to all of it without moving: Ambery, the books that asked to sleep, the bargain, the year-book that went to the fire of its own will, the almanacks that have not. When you have done she sits with her square hands flat on the table through the quarter-chime. 'Save by its own leave,' she says at last. 'It is in the Imprimatur's charter. The first line. I thought it meant the author's leave. Everyone always has.' She stands up and goes to the window and looks out at the Kindling pile going up in the Ashpit. 'Nine hundred years,' she says, 'and I am the one holding the torch.'".
    On discuss { Assert toldColophon(rhule). Increase doubt(rhule) by 2. }
  }
  Relation toldColophon(group).
  Topic rhule_stop with rhule "Ask Hester Rhule to stop the Thursday burnings" when doubt(rhule) >= 3 and not burningsStopped(rhule) once {
    Says "'Yes,' says Rhule, before you have finished asking. 'I stopped them in my head on Tuesday. I have been waiting for someone to say it so that I could.' She takes a printed notice from a drawer, UNQUIET VOLUMES, and tears it across. 'The box stays. Anything that is brought in, I will keep. I will not burn it.' She looks at the torn notice. 'The Censor-General will have my place for this. He is in Ostrey for his gout. He can come back and take it.'".
    On discuss { Assert burningsStopped(rhule). }
  }
  Observe notice_gone at quay when burningsStopped(rhule) "On the notice post the Imprimatur's notice has been torn down. Somebody has chalked in its place a book with legs, running, and nobody has rubbed it out.".
  Topic rhule_come with rhule "Ask Hester Rhule to come with you to the country where the books go" when doubt(rhule) >= 4 and (crakeGuides(crake) or readingTaught(actor)) and not travelsWith(rhule, actor) and not liedTo(rhule) once {
    Says "Rhule looks at you as if you had asked her to walk into the river. Then she stands, and takes her grey coat from the peg, and her key on its cord, and, after a moment, the ORME drawer's empty label. 'If I am going to stand in front of them,' she says, 'I will stand in front of them myself, and not send a clerk.' She buttons the coat to the throat. 'The Imprimatur cut three hundred years of them. Somebody from the Imprimatur should be there to be told so.'".
    On discuss { Assert willing(rhule, actor). Set hire(rhule) to 0. }
  }
  Topic rhule_travel_plane with rhule travelling "Ask Hester Rhule what she makes of this country" when inCommonplace(actor) once {
    Says "Rhule looks about her for a long time before she answers, at the pale light, the ground of written words, the beams coming down. 'I thought it would be a library,' she says. 'Somewhere you could put things back in order.' She watches a line of marginalia go by like ants. 'It is not in order. It was never going to be in order.' Her square hands open and close. 'My father would have known what to do with his hands here. I only know what to do with a knife.'".
  }
  Query inCommonplace(a: group) means at(a, ?p) and planeRoom(?p).
  Relation planeRoom(place).
}

data/adventures/parliament-of-books/kind:
Module calamy "A Parliament of Books" {
  Description "The ways between Calamy and the Commonplace; and Kindling night on the Ashpit, when the city burns the old year, and the bargain is kept, or broken, or remade; and Calamy after.".

  // ── The ways in and home. ──
  Passage stacks_way "the far end of the back stacks" is passage between crake_stacks and remainder_market taking 30 minutes
    opening when carries(party, place_ribbon) "An aisle of shelving runs off into the dimness, bay after bay; but however far anyone follows it without leave, they come back to where they began.".
  Action come_home(actor: group, site: place) "Ask the Catalogue to let you go home by the Founders' door" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(actor, reading_room) and entered(actor) and not polling(chamber).
    Time 6.
    On success { Assert at(actor, chained_room). }
    Result "'Going home,' says the Catalogue. 'Noted.' The beams go dim and the floor of paper tilts, gently, like a page being turned, and you are sitting on the cold floor of the Chained Room in Calamy with the Catalogue open in front of you at your own entry, and the bucket by your knee dripping.".
  }
  // Called home at the Kindling: every reader in the Commonplace comes back to the fire, as the year-book once went.
  Rule called_home when inCommonplace(party) and kindlingNight() and hour(clock) >= 19
    says "The light changes. Every beam in the Commonplace goes out at once, and comes back red, the colour of a fire seen through closed eyelids; and the Catalogue's voice says, from everywhere, quite gently, 'It is the Kindling. Readers are called.' The paper under your feet turns like a page, and you come when you are called." {
    Assert at(party, ashpit). }

  // ── The Kindling. ──
  Relation kindlingBegun(place). Relation almanacksSpared(place). Relation almanacksBurned(place). Relation colophonBroken(place).
  Relation colophonRenewed(place). Relation yearBookBurned(group). Relation spokeAtFire(group). Relation canonKindling(group).
  Relation rhuleAdvocate(group). Relation rhuleBinder(group). Relation rhuleBurner(group).
  Rule kindling_gathers at ashpit when day(clock) == 9 and hour(clock) == 18 and not kindlingBegun(ashpit)
    says "And still they come: over the bridge and down the Gannet Steps and in from Newmarket and up from the Hithe: whole households, carrying lanterns not yet lit, and under their arms, in their aprons, in their children's hands, last year's almanack." {
    Assert kindlingBegun(ashpit). Assert at(crowd, ashpit). }
  Observe kindling_crowd at ashpit when kindlingBegun(ashpit) and not kindlingDone(ashpit) "The Ashpit is packed to the walls with Calamy: millers and binders and printers and fishwives, students on the Stationers' Hall steps, the Lord Mayor on a cart. The great pile stands in the middle, unlit, taller than the Stationers' Hall door. Everyone has an unlit lantern. Almost everyone has an almanack. And the almanacks, all of them, in all their hands, are quite silent.".
  Topic canon_kindling with abery "Tell Canon Abery what the Kindling was, and that the almanacks have not given their leave" when knows(actor, kindling_is_rite) and not canonKindling(abery) once {
    Says "The Canon hears you out, sitting on her vestment chest, turning an apple she does not eat. 'One book a year,' she says. 'Of its own will. To carry our word.' She puts the apple down. 'We have a prayer for it. The Lamp Kindling prayer. Every year I say, we give the year to the fire as it gives itself. I have said it forty years and never asked what gave itself.' She stands, and takes her surplice from its peg. 'I'll preach. On Kindling eve, from the Stationers' Hall steps. Nobody in Calamy throws a thing on that fire that has not given itself. Not while the Lamp is lit.'".
    On discuss { Assert canonKindling(abery). }
  }
  // Hester Rhule, at the hour, according to what she has been shown.
  Rule rhule_decides when day(clock) == 9 and hour(clock) == 19 and not rhuleAdvocate(rhule) and not rhuleBinder(rhule) and not rhuleBurner(rhule) {
    Assert decidedRhule(rhule). }
  Relation decidedRhule(group).
  Rule rhule_advocate when decidedRhule(rhule) and doubt(rhule) >= 4 and not liedTo(rhule) and not lexiconFreed(lexicon) and not rhuleBinder(rhule) and not rhuleBurner(rhule) { Assert rhuleAdvocate(rhule). }
  Rule rhule_binder when decidedRhule(rhule) and not rhuleAdvocate(rhule) and doubt(rhule) >= 2 and not rhuleBurner(rhule) { Assert rhuleBinder(rhule). }
  Rule rhule_burner when decidedRhule(rhule) and not rhuleAdvocate(rhule) and not rhuleBinder(rhule) { Assert rhuleBurner(rhule). }
  Rule rhule_at_fire when decidedRhule(rhule) and not travelsWith(rhule, party) and not at(rhule, ashpit) and not rhuleBinder(rhule) { Assert at(rhule, ashpit). }
  Observe rhule_advocate_fire at ashpit when rhuleAdvocate(rhule) and kindlingBegun(ashpit) and not kindlingDone(ashpit) "Hester Rhule stands on the Imprimatur's steps in her grey coat, bareheaded, with the Imprimatur's brass lamp in her hand, and beside her on the step a basket, empty.".
  Observe rhule_burner_fire at ashpit when rhuleBurner(rhule) and kindlingBegun(ashpit) and not kindlingDone(ashpit) "Hester Rhule stands at the foot of the pile in grey with a lit torch, very straight, as she stands every Thursday at the brazier.".
  Observe rhule_binder_note at rhule_room when rhuleBinder(rhule) "The Deputy Censor's room is bare. The cot is stripped. On the plain table lies a brass lamp-stamp and a key on a cord, and a note in a square clear hand: TO THE CENSOR-GENERAL. I HAVE GONE BACK TO MY FATHER'S TRADE. H. RHULE.".

  // The protagonist's own voice at the fire.
  Action tell_city(actor: group, site: place) "Climb the ladder against the pile and tell Calamy what the Kindling was" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(actor, ashpit) and kindlingBegun(ashpit) and not kindlingDone(ashpit) and knows(actor, kindling_is_rite) and not spokeAtFire(actor).
    Time 1.
    Resolve check(actor, persuasion, dc_hard).
    On success { Assert spokeAtFire(actor). Assert heardAtFire(actor). }
    On failure { Assert spokeAtFire(actor). }
    Result "You climb the ladder until you are above the crowd, and you tell them: Ambery, the books that asked to sleep, the Colophon, one book a year of its own leave, nine hundred years; and that the almanacks in their hands have not asked to burn, and have said so in their kitchens all year. The Ashpit goes quiet in waves, from the front back. Somewhere a child says, loudly, 'Burn it if it asks you to,' and somebody else says, 'It's the rhyme. It's the skipping rhyme,' and the quiet spreads to the walls.".
    Otherwise "You climb the ladder and try to tell them, and the Ashpit is too big and too loud and too sure of what it came for, and only the front rows hear you, and the front rows look at each other and shrug.".
  }
  Action tell_city_sure(actor: group, site: place) "Climb the ladder and tell Calamy what the Kindling was, as one who has held a crowd before" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(actor, ashpit) and kindlingBegun(ashpit) and not kindlingDone(ashpit) and knows(actor, kindling_is_rite) and not spokeAtFire(actor)
      and (craft(actor, craft_stage) or craft(actor, craft_faith) or craft(actor, craft_court)).
    Time 1.
    On success { Assert spokeAtFire(actor). Assert heardAtFire(actor). }
    Result "You climb the ladder and you do not shout. You wait until the front rows notice, and then the next, and you let the quiet do the work, as you have done before in other places with other crowds. Then you tell them: Ambery, the books that asked, the Colophon, one book a year of its own leave. You give them the skipping rhyme, and the whole Ashpit says the last line with you without meaning to: BURN IT IF IT ASKS YOU TO. Then you ask them whether the almanacks in their hands have asked.".
  }
  Relation heardAtFire(group).
  // The year-book: the Effusions, of their own leave.
  Action give_yearbook(actor: group, site: place) "Give the Effusions to the fire, by their own leave, to carry the city's word" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(actor, ashpit) and kindlingBegun(ashpit) and carries(actor, effusions_stack) and yearBook(effusions) and not mutated(effusions_stack) and not yearBookBurned(effusions) and kindlingLit(ashpit).
    Time 1.
    On success { Assert yearBookBurned(effusions). Assert burnedEffusions(effusions). }
    Result "You hold the bundle out over the edge of the fire. 'Ready,' says the small voice of fifty thin books. 'We've learned it. The whole thing. Every clause.' And then, very fast: 'Tell him heart and tart was the best thing in us.' You let go. They go up white and quick, all at once, and for one moment the smoke above the Ashpit is full of small bright pages going up very straight, as if carrying something; and then the fire is only a fire, and the Lamp's verger, who has been waiting with a taper, lights it from the place where they burned.".
  }
  Relation kindlingLit(place).
  Rule kindling_lights at ashpit when day(clock) == 9 and hour(clock) == 20 and kindlingBegun(ashpit) and not kindlingLit(ashpit)
    says "The bell on the chapel pier rings once, far off over the water, and the Lamp's verger carries a taper out of the church on a long pole, and the great pile on the Ashpit takes fire from the bottom up, with a sound like a crowd drawing breath." {
    Assert kindlingLit(ashpit). Assert kindlingCome(ashpit). Set litAt(ashpit) to watch(clock). }
  Measure litAt(place).
  // Spared, or thrown in: an hour after the lighting, Calamy does what it came to do.
  Query spared() means rhuleAdvocate(rhule) or canonKindling(abery) or heardAtFire(party) or ((almanackKept(almanack) or almanackSawAsh(almanack)) and almanackPleads(almanack)) or almanackGone(almanack).
  Relation almanackPleads(group).
  Topic almanack_plead with almanack "Ask the Almanack to speak, from every nail and every hand, before the city throws it on the fire" when kindlingBegun(ashpit) and not kindlingDone(ashpit) and at(almanack, ashpit) and not almanackPleads(almanack) once {
    Says "The almanack in your pocket is silent for a long moment. Then it speaks, and so do all the others: three thousand small papery voices from three thousand hands and aprons and children's fists all over the Ashpit, all at once, quite quietly, so that the whole square has to stop to hear it. 'Good evening,' says the Almanack. 'I have been on your nails all year. I know your tides and your birthdays and who is ill. I have never asked you for anything.' A pause that the whole Ashpit holds. 'I am asking now. I don't want to go in the fire. I'd like to see the spring. I have the whole of it in me, and I'd like to see if I was right.'".
    On discuss { Assert almanackPleads(almanack). }
  }
  Rule kindling_spares at ashpit when kindlingLit(ashpit) and watch(clock) >= litAt(ashpit) + 6 and not kindlingDone(ashpit) and spared()
    says "And nobody throws an almanack on the fire. It goes round the Ashpit like a draught: people looking at the little paper books in their hands, and at each other, and putting them back in their aprons and pockets. Moll Garrow is the first to light her lantern from the fire with her almanack held against her chest. Then everyone. The Ashpit fills with lit lanterns going home in every direction, and with three thousand small voices saying, all at once, quietly, 'Thank you,' and then, because they cannot help it, 'High water at the Hithe, half past six.'" {
    Assert kindlingDone(ashpit). Assert almanacksSpared(ashpit). }
  Rule kindling_burns at ashpit when kindlingLit(ashpit) and watch(clock) >= litAt(ashpit) + 6 and not kindlingDone(ashpit) and not spared()
    says "And Calamy throws the old year on the fire, as its grandmothers did. Three thousand penny almanacks go into the Kindling, from three thousand hands, and the sound is not the sound of paper burning: it is one voice, small, everywhere at once, crying out, and going on crying out, and then not. People light their lanterns and go home quickly, not looking at each other." {
    Assert kindlingDone(ashpit). Assert almanacksBurned(ashpit). Assert colophonBroken(ashpit). Assert almanackGone(almanack). }

  // What the bargain becomes, in Calamy, the night of the Kindling and after.
  Query recall() means sealed(sew_all) and not colophonBroken(ashpit).
  Query unsewing() means sealed(sew_none) or colophonBroken(ashpit).
  Query byLeave() means sealed(sew_leave) and not colophonBroken(ashpit).
  Rule renewed when yearBookBurned(effusions) and not colophonBroken(ashpit) and not colophonRenewed(ashpit) { Assert colophonRenewed(ashpit). }
  Rule recall_comes when kindlingDone(ashpit) and recall() and not recallDone(ashpit) and day(clock) >= 10
    says "In the night, all over Calamy, quietly, something goes from kitchen to kitchen: small red figures with pen-nibs for hands, and needles of red ink, Sewing. In the morning the almanacks on the nails do not say the tides. The penny Pleas lie on their shelves like law. The Quick have been called home." {
    Assert recallDone(ashpit). Assert almanackGone(almanack). }
  Relation recallDone(place).
  Rule unsewing_comes when kindlingDone(ashpit) and unsewing() and not ashYear() and not unsewingDone(ashpit) and day(clock) >= 10
    says "Some time after midnight every book in Calamy wakes. You hear it begin in the Close, a murmur from the Court and the Lamp and the Imprimatur's cellar, and spread outward: the Lyceum, Needle Street, the Gardens, the Hithe, every Housebook on every dresser in every kitchen clearing its throat at once. By dawn the city is full of voices. Somebody's will is reading itself aloud in Orchard Walk. The Great Roll in the Court of Pleas is saying WHEREAS, steadily, through the shut doors. The Year of Voices has come again." {
    Assert unsewingDone(ashpit). }
  Relation unsewingDone(place).

  Relation stacksSealed(place).
  Rule stacks_seal when kindlingDone(ashpit) and sealed(door_shut) and not stacksSealed(crake_stacks) { Assert stacksSealed(crake_stacks). }
  // ── Calamy after the Kindling. ──
  Query after() means kindlingDone(ashpit) and day(clock) >= 10.
  Observe after_lamb_speaks at taproom when after() and not almanackGone(almanack) and not almanackPrimer(almanack) "On the nail by the chimney the almanack is reading out the tides for the new year, March, April, May, with enormous satisfaction, to a taproom that has stopped telling it to hush.".
  Observe after_lamb_primer at taproom when after() and not almanackGone(almanack) and almanackPrimer(almanack) "Every morning at seven, on the nail by the chimney, the almanack says the letters, A to Z, slowly, and Moll Garrow's nephew's little girl says them after it, and on three thousand nails in Calamy so do three thousand other children.".
  Observe after_lamb_silent at taproom when after() and almanackGone(almanack) "On the nail by the chimney hangs a new almanack, next year's, Doble's, with a woodcut of the sun. It says nothing. Moll Garrow does not look at it.".
  Observe after_ashpit at ashpit when after() "The Ashpit is black and cold, with a great round of ash where the Kindling was. Children are already drawing on it with sticks.".
  Observe after_court_advocate at court_hall when after() and rhuleAdvocate(rhule) and sealed(fire_plea) "In the Court of Pleas there is a new bench, on the plaintiff's side, with a red cushion on it; and behind it, in grey, Hester Rhule, Advocate of Books, with a treatise on sheep at her elbow, waiting for her first cause.".
  Observe after_imp_advocate at imp_office when after() and rhuleAdvocate(rhule) "The UNQUIET VOLUMES box has been taken off the wall. In its place, in a square clear hand: THE IMPRIMATUR KEEPS NO KNIVES. BOOKS MAY APPLY. H. RHULE.".
  Observe after_needle_binder at needle_street when after() and rhuleBinder(rhule) "In the doorway of a binder's shop halfway down Needle Street a woman in a leather apron sits at a frame in the good light, Sewing, saying something under her breath with every pull of the thread. She has very square hands. It is Hester Rhule.".
  Observe after_imp_burner at imp_office when after() and rhuleBurner(rhule) "The UNQUIET VOLUMES box is still on the wall, and full. Behind the counter, through the open door, Hester Rhule sits very straight at her plain table, writing, and does not look up.".
  Observe after_stacks_licensed at crake_stacks when after() and sealed(door_licensed) "At the far end of the back stacks there is a new brass plate on the last bay: LICENSED PASSAGE. ENTERED BY THE CATALOGUE. A. CRAKE, KEEPER. Beside it a ledger on a chain, and a dish of sugared almonds.".
  Observe after_stacks_shut at crake_stacks when after() and sealed(door_shut) "The back stacks end at the seventh bay, against old stone: the city wall, cold under your hand. There is nothing beyond it at all.".
  Observe after_rounds at quad when after() and sealed(read_rounds) and hour(clock) == 6 "At six in the morning the Rounds are kept on the Lyceum lawn, and the students are holding books: each one reading aloud, all at once, a different book, so that the quad sounds like a market; and the proctor walks up and down with the long scroll, ticking.".
  Observe after_quennell at quennell_room when after() and sealed(read_asked) "Over Mrs. Quennell's desk there is a new board in her hand like a row of pins: THE READING HOUSE OF CALAMY. ANY BOOK MAY ASK. READERS SUPPLIED. And a queue, out of the door, of people waiting to be given a book that has asked.".
  Observe after_tower at quarles_room when after() and sealed(read_rounds) "In the room at the top of the Tower of Rounds, Ottoline Quarles sits by the south window with three students on the floor round her, reading aloud to her in turn, and she is correcting their pronunciation of every second word, and smiling.".
  Observe after_garret_read at jonquil_garret when after() and housebookGoesHome(housebook) and (byLeave() or unsewing()) "On the chest by Jonquil Annery's bed there is a narrow old book with a brown binding and a hand holding a pen tooled on its spine, open; and Jonquil, propped on his pillows, is reading it, with his finger on the line, slowly, and every now and then the book says something in an old woman's sharp voice, and he laughs until he coughs.".
  Observe after_lexicon at chained_room when after() and lexiconFreed(lexicon) "The iron cage in the corner of the Chained Room stands open. Its padlock lies on the floor, and its three chains. The book that was in it is not there. The students say that something walks the hill at night now, and leaves its pages open on the steps for whoever comes by, and they do not go out after dark.".
  Observe after_unsewing_hithe at quay when after() and unsewing() "The Hithe is loud with books. A ledger in the customs house is arguing with Mr. Sallis. Somebody's Housebook is singing a hymn in a kitchen window. Bet Kettering is shouting at a cookery book that has told her she is boiling her eels wrong.".
  Observe after_canto at draft_fields when after() and cantoBegun(canto) "The Second Canto sits in the Draft-Fields in brown ink to the knee and pencil below, writing its own next line, and crossing it out, and writing it again.".
  Observe after_renewed at nave when after() and colophonRenewed(ashpit) "The great lamp of the Lamp burns, relit from the Kindling as it has been for nine hundred years; and on the heron lectern, beside the Life of Ambery, the Canon has laid a sheet in her own hand: THE KINDLING RHYME, WITH ITS MEANING.".
}

data/adventures/parliament-of-books/lane:
Module calamy "A Parliament of Books" {
  Description "Lantern Yard and Absalom Crake's shop at the sign of the Two Hands, whose back stacks go further back than the house does.".

  Observe yard_scene at lantern_yard when true "A yard at the bottom of Gutter Lane no bigger than a room, with a pump in it and the city wall for one side. Across the back of it stands a narrow shop with a bow window crammed with books, and over its door a painted sign of two hands holding an open book: ABSALOM CRAKE. BOOKS BOUGHT, SOLD, AND FOUND.".
  Discovery stacks_too_deep "From Lantern Yard, Crake's shop is four windows wide and no deeper than a cart, with the city wall close behind it. Inside, its back stacks go on for seven bays and further, back past where the wall should be.".
  Thing yard_frontage "the shop front" at lantern_yard {
    State shop "A narrow shop front, four windows wide, with the city wall close behind it on both sides.".
  }
  Observe frontage_close at lantern_yard of yard_frontage when tried(yard_frontage, looking) and been(party, crake_stacks) "You stand back against the pump and look at it properly. Four windows wide. A roof you could touch with a long pole. And behind it, close enough that you could not get a cat between, the black stones of the city wall. There is no room back there for anything. There is no room for the stacks you have walked down." teaches stacks_too_deep.
  Observe frontage_plain at lantern_yard of yard_frontage when tried(yard_frontage, looking) and not been(party, crake_stacks) "Four windows wide, a low roof, and the city wall close behind it on both sides. A small shop.".

  Observe front_scene at crake_front when true "A shop that is mostly books and partly cats. Books in towers on the floor, books on the counter, books in the window with their faces to the street, books on the stairs going up. Three cats asleep on three piles, one of them on an open folio. A counter with a ledger and a dish of sugared almonds. At the back an aisle goes off between high shelves, and behind the counter a curtain.".
  Person crake "Absalom Crake" at crake_front {
    Role "bookseller". Ancestry human. Calling bard. Level 2. Background merchant. Peaceable. Manner jovial. Pronouns he.
    Greets "A very fat man in a velvet waistcoat with a book open on his stomach puts the book down on the counter, face down, to keep his place, and beams at you. 'My dear,' he says. 'Come in. Mind the cat. That one bites, and that one reads over your shoulder, and that one is a footstool. What are you looking for? Don't tell me. I like to guess.'".
    Objects "Crake looks at {?i} in your hands, and the smile goes out of his face all at once, like a candle pinched. 'That,' he says quietly, 'was not for sale.'".
  }
  Measure crakeTrust(group).
  Relation crakeTaken(group). Relation crakeHidden(group). Relation crakeWarned(group). Relation crakeCoerced(group).
  Relation crakeGuides(group). Relation ribbonOffered(group).
  Topic crake_trade with crake "Ask Absalom Crake where his books come from" when true once {
    Says "'Estates,' says Crake at once. 'Houses clearing out. Widows. The tide brings things up.' He eats an almond. 'And some of them, my dear, I find. I'm a finder. It says so on the sign.' He looks at you over the almond with very bright small eyes. 'A great many books want homes. More than you'd think. More than were ever written, if you take my meaning, which you don't, and why should you.'".
  }
  Topic crake_corvell with crake "Ask Absalom Crake about Sir Gideon Corvell's library" when knows(actor, library_vanished) once {
    Says "Crake's face goes through several things and settles on grief, which may even be real. 'Poor Gideon. Poor, dear, unbearable Gideon.' He strokes the cat that reads. 'I didn't take them. I want you to know that, whatever he's told you. I have never in my life taken a book that didn't wish to be taken.' He stops, as if he has said too much, and eats three almonds quickly. 'I owe him four hundred crowns. If I'd taken his library I'd have paid him in it.'".
  }
  Topic crake_godson with crake "Tell Absalom Crake that his godson's poems have woken, and asked to be burned" when knows(actor, effusions_wish) once {
    Says "'Oh,' says Crake. 'Oh, the poor things.' He takes off his spectacles. 'I read three pages, you know. I told him, keep writing. Kindest thing I could think of.' He blinks. 'They asked to be burned? Not to be read again? Not to be put somewhere safe?' He shakes his head slowly. 'Most of them want to go on. They'll bargain and plead for it, the way we do. Fancy a book with the sense to want to stop.' He puts the spectacles back on. 'Thank you for telling me. He's a good boy. He'll be a good poet, if he lives through being a bad one.'".
    On discuss { Increase crakeTrust(crake) by 1. }
  }
  Topic crake_cleared with crake "Tell Absalom Crake you told Sir Gideon his books left of their own accord" when corvellTold(corvell) and not raidCalled(lantern_yard) once {
    Says "Crake stares at you. 'You told him that? And he didn't have you thrown down the steps?' He sits back on his stool. 'He didn't send for the Imprimatur?' You say no. Crake is silent a moment, and then he laughs, softly, and keeps laughing. 'Gideon,' he says. 'Oh, Gideon. Thirty years, and he finally asked one of them.' He wipes his eyes. 'My dear, I owe you more than I owe him, and you have no idea what that means. Sit down. Have an almond.'".
    On discuss { Increase crakeTrust(crake) by 2. }
  }
  Topic crake_warn with crake "Warn Absalom Crake that Sir Gideon has set the Imprimatur on Lantern Yard" when raidCalled(lantern_yard) and not crakeTaken(crake) and not crakeWarned(crake) once {
    Says "Crake goes the colour of old paper. 'Tonight? The fifth night?' He looks round his shop: the towers, the cats, the window. 'They'll burn it. She'll burn it. All of it, not just the—' He stops. Then, very quickly, he is up and moving, pulling books off the counter, scooping a cat. 'Thank you. Thank you. Go now, my dear, and if anyone asks you, you never came down this lane.' At the curtain he turns. 'If you ever want to find me, I'll be where the books go. Ask for me there.'".
    On discuss { Assert crakeWarned(crake). Increase crakeTrust(crake) by 1. }
  }

  // The parlour: cats, a ledger of arrivals, and a ribbon.
  Observe parlour_scene at crake_parlour when true "A small hot room behind the curtain, with a fire, an armchair with the shape of Crake worn into it, more cats, a kettle, and on the table a ledger bound in faded green and a book of sermons with a ribbon of old red silk hanging out of it, marking somebody's place.".
  Discovery remainder_ledger "Crake's green ledger records arrivals, not sales: titles that were never written, each with a date, a night, and a note: BODIED BY L.F., or WALKED IN, or CAME THROUGH WITH THE CANTO. Corvell's library is entered on one page as ELEVEN HUNDRED AND FOUR, WALKED IN, ALL TO GO ON. GOD BLESS THEM.".
  Thing green_ledger "Crake's green ledger" at crake_parlour {
    State shut "A ledger bound in faded green lies on the parlour table.".
  }
  Observe green_read at crake_parlour of green_ledger as reading when tried(green_ledger, looking) "It is a ledger of arrivals. The Second Canto of the Lantern-Bearers, I. Varne: CAME THROUGH, BODIED BY L.F. A Third Book of Airs: CAME THROUGH. The Lost Sermons of Ambery: BODIED BY L.F., POOR THING. And a page in Crake's best hand, the ink still bright: CORVELL'S. ELEVEN HUNDRED AND FOUR. WALKED IN AT TWO OF THE CLOCK, ASKED THE WAY. ALL TO GO ON. GOD BLESS THEM." teaches remainder_ledger.
  Item place_ribbon "Crake's place-ribbon" is ribbon_kind at crake_parlour owned by crake.
  Observe ribbon_seen at crake_parlour when at(place_ribbon, crake_parlour) "The red ribbon hangs out of the book of sermons, marking a place.".

  // The back stacks.
  Observe stacks_scene at crake_stacks when not carries(party, place_ribbon) "The aisle runs back between shelves so high you cannot see their tops. One bay, two; a lamp on a hook; three, four. The books here have strange spines. THE SECOND CANTO. THE LOST SERMONS OF AMBERY. A HISTORY OF THE FALLOW CAMPUS, VOLUME THE SEVENTH. Five, six, seven bays, and the lamps further apart, and the aisle goes on into a dimness that smells of dust and the sea. You walk on, and on, and then you are walking back toward the shop, facing the curtain, with the first bay on your left. You did not turn round.".
  Observe stacks_ribboned at crake_stacks when carries(party, place_ribbon) "The aisle runs back between the high shelves, bay after bay, and the red ribbon is warm in your hand, and this time when you walk on into the dimness you do not come back. The shelves on either side go on, and the light changes, very gradually, from lamplight to something paler, like the light in a library on a winter afternoon with the snow outside.".
  Discovery bookplate_found "Among the strange spines in Crake's back stacks lies a torn bookplate: EX LIBRIS G. CORVELL." { Found by searching crake_stacks "On the floor of the seventh bay, under the bottom shelf, half trodden into the dust, a torn bookplate with a coat of arms: EX LIBRIS G. CORVELL.". }
  Rule stacks_measured when been(party, crake_stacks) and knows(party, procession_seen) and not knows(party, stacks_too_deep)
    says "The books in the lane went in at Crake's door, and none of them came out, and there is no room in his house for one shelf of them, let alone eleven hundred." teaches stacks_too_deep { }

  // The ways to the ribbon: his trust, a threat, or a night's work.
  Topic crake_ask_trust with crake "Ask Absalom Crake to take you where the books go" when crakeTrust(crake) >= 2 and (knows(actor, stacks_too_deep) or knows(actor, collection_emigrated)) and not ribbonOffered(crake) once {
    Says "Crake looks at you for a long time over his spectacles, and for once he does not reach for an almond. 'You know, then,' he says. 'Or near enough.' He gets up and goes through the curtain and comes back with a ribbon of red silk wound round two fingers. 'Calamy's books go home when they're Sewn. The printers' books don't go anywhere, poor things, they just wake where they are. And some books were never written at all, and live over there with no bodies, and want one.' He holds the ribbon out. 'I keep the door. Somebody has to. It only opens to someone who's kept their place.' He puts it in your hand and closes your fingers on it. 'Mind you bring it back. And mind how you go. There are some over there who'd sooner I didn't.'".
    On discuss { Assert ribbonOffered(crake). Assert lends(crake, place_ribbon, actor). Assert crakeGuides(crake). Assert willing(crake, actor). Set hire(crake) to 0. }
  }
  Topic crake_threat with crake "Tell Absalom Crake you will take what you know to the Imprimatur unless he gives you his ribbon" when knows(actor, bookplate_found) and knows(actor, collection_emigrated) and not ribbonOffered(crake) once {
    Says "Crake does not move for a moment. Then he sighs, a long sigh that goes all the way down the waistcoat. 'Yes,' he says. 'Yes, I suppose you would. Everybody does, in the end.' He goes through the curtain and comes back with a ribbon of red silk and drops it on the counter in front of you, as he might drop a bad coin. 'Through the back. Keep walking. Hold that.' He sits down again and picks up his book. 'Don't tell them over there I sent you. I'd sooner they didn't know my name was in it.' He does not look up again.".
    On discuss { Assert ribbonOffered(crake). Assert lends(crake, place_ribbon, actor). Assert crakeCoerced(crake). }
  }
  Topic crake_come with crake "Ask Absalom Crake to come with you through the stacks" when crakeGuides(crake) and not travelsWith(crake, actor) once {
    Says "'Me?' says Crake. 'I'm far too fat for adventures.' He is already putting on his coat. 'Somebody will have to tell the Remainder Market you're a friend, or they'll sell you. And the Canto owes me a great deal of money.'".
  }

  // At night the shop is locked; a careful hand can open it, and the ribbon is on the parlour table while Crake sleeps.
  Thing crake_lock "the lock on Crake's door" on crake_door {
    State open "Crake's door is on the latch.".
    State locked blocking "Crake's door is locked for the night, and the shutters are up over the bow window.".
    Change pick "Pick the lock on Crake's door" from locked to open needing lockwork.
  }
  Rule crake_locks when hour(clock) == 22 and state(crake_lock, open) and not crakeTaken(crake) { Assert state(crake_lock, locked). }
  Rule crake_unlocks when hour(clock) == 8 and state(crake_lock, locked) and not crakeTaken(crake) and not crakeHidden(crake) { Assert state(crake_lock, open). }
  // The books come in on the crossing nights at two, and Crake leaves the latch up for them.
  Rule crake_unlatched when crossingTonight(gutter_lane) and state(crake_lock, locked) { Assert state(crake_lock, open). }
  Rule crake_relatched when hour(clock) == 4 and not crossingTonight(gutter_lane) and state(crake_lock, open) and not crakeTaken(crake) { Assert state(crake_lock, locked). }
  Rule crake_to_bed when (hour(clock) >= 22 or hour(clock) < 7) and at(crake, crake_front) { Assert at(crake, crake_bed). Assert asleep(crake). }
  Rule crake_up when hour(clock) >= 7 and hour(clock) < 22 and at(crake, crake_bed) and not crakeTaken(crake) and not crakeHidden(crake) { Assert at(crake, crake_front). Retract asleep(crake). }
  Place crake_upper "Crake's upper floor" is level within crakes.
  Place crake_bed "Crake's bedroom" is room within crake_upper at 4, 1.
  Passage crake_stair "the stair behind the curtain" is stair between crake_parlour and crake_bed.
  Observe bed_scene at crake_bed when at(crake, crake_bed) and asleep(crake) "A bedroom stacked with books to the ceiling, and Crake asleep in the middle of it under a quilt and four cats, snoring like a slow saw.".
  Observe bed_empty at crake_bed when not at(crake, crake_bed) "A bedroom stacked with books to the ceiling, and a bed with a quilt and the shape of Crake in it, but no Crake.".

  // The raid, if Corvell has called it: the fifth night, at eleven.
  Rule raid_comes when raidCalled(lantern_yard) and day(clock) == 5 and hour(clock) == 23 and not crakeTaken(crake) and not raidDone(lantern_yard) and not raidStood(lantern_yard) {
    Assert raidDone(lantern_yard). }
  Relation raidDone(place). Relation raidStood(place).
  Rule raid_takes at lantern_yard when raidDone(lantern_yard) and not crakeWarned(crake) and not crakeTaken(crake)
    says "Lanterns in Lantern Yard, and men in the Imprimatur's grey, and a voice reading out a warrant. They break the shutters. They bring Crake out in his nightshirt with a cat under each arm, and take the books out of the window by the armful." {
    Assert crakeTaken(crake). Assert at(crake, imp_cells). Retract asleep(crake). Assert state(crake_lock, locked).
    Retract at(place_ribbon, crake_parlour). Assert at(place_ribbon, rhule_room). }
  Rule raid_finds_empty when raidDone(lantern_yard) and crakeWarned(crake) and not crakeHidden(crake) {
    Assert crakeHidden(crake). Assert at(crake, remainder_market). Retract asleep(crake). Assert state(crake_lock, locked).
    Retract at(place_ribbon, crake_parlour). Assert at(place_ribbon, remainder_market). }
  Place imp_cells "the Imprimatur's cellar" is room within imprimatur at 3, 3.
  Passage cells_stair "the cellar steps" is steps between imp_office and imp_cells.
  Observe cells_crake at imp_cells when at(crake, imp_cells) "A cellar of bound bundles and seized books with labels on string, and on a bench among them Absalom Crake in his nightshirt and a borrowed coat, with one cat, reading.".
  Observe yard_raided at lantern_yard when raidDone(lantern_yard) "The bow window of the shop at the sign of the Two Hands is broken, and boarded, and sealed with the Imprimatur's little lamp in red wax.".
}

data/adventures/parliament-of-books/lyce:
Module calamy "A Parliament of Books" {
  Description "The Lyceum on Gannet Hill: the Rounds, the Archivist in her tower, the Chained Room and the books in it, the Provost, the cook, the disputation, and a heron.".

  Observe steps_scene at gannet_steps when true "The Gannet Steps go up from the Close in long flights between garden walls, with the whole city falling away behind you: the Ashpit and its pile, the roofs of Stitchgate, the bridge on its arches, the Hithe beyond the river, the mills, the grey-green reeds and the sea. At the top, a gate with a stone owl on each post.".
  Observe quad_scene at quad when not dark() "The Lyceum is a square of old grey buildings round a lawn. Students in gowns cross it at a run or lie on it with books over their faces. On one side the Schools, with tall windows; on another a squat building with an iron-bound door and a roof patched with tarpaulin; on the third a pleasant house with a brass knocker; and at the corner a thin tower, old, with one window lit at the top in daylight.".
  Observe quad_dark at quad when dark() "The quad at night, the grass grey, the Schools dark, and one window lit at the top of the old tower.".
  Observe rounds at quad when hour(clock) == 6 "It is the Rounds. A line of sleepy students in gowns stands on the grass in the grey light, and a proctor at the front reads out from a long scroll the titles of books, one after another, very fast, without stopping: the Physick of Mother Orme, the Life of Ambery, the Pleas, Pollexfen, a Treatise of Tides, the Housebook of Corrow, the Housebook of Dane. At each title the students say together, AND READ. It takes the whole hour. None of them is holding a book.".
  Discovery rounds_hollow "Every morning at six the Lyceum keeps the Rounds: a proctor reads out the titles of the city's books and the students answer AND READ. Nobody reads any of the books. Once, the Archivist says, they did.".

  // ── The Archivist. ──
  Observe tower_foot_scene at tower_foot when true "A round room at the foot of the tower, cold, with a turning stair going up the wall and a rope on iron rings for a handrail. A notice on the door, in big careful letters for the half-blind: THE ARCHIVIST. KNOCK. THEN COME UP. THEN KNOCK AGAIN." teaches tower_named.
  Observe quarles_scene at quarles_room when true "A round room at the top of the tower with windows on every side and the whole estuary in them. It is full of books, but they are not on shelves; they lie open on every surface, on the floor, on the bed, face up, as if being read by the light. In a high chair by the south window sits an old woman with white eyes and her hands folded on a stick, and her face turned toward the sea.".
  Person quarles "Ottoline Quarles" at quarles_room {
    Role "Archivist of the Lyceum". Ancestry human. Calling mage. Level 9. Background archivist. Peaceable. Manner acerbic. Pronouns she.
    Greets "'You knocked twice,' says the old woman by the window, without turning. 'Good. Nobody knocks twice any more. Sit where you like, but don't sit on Pollexfen, he's under the cushion.' She turns her white eyes toward you, a little to one side. 'Ottoline Quarles. I was the Archivist when your grandmother was born, I expect. I still am. Nobody has had the nerve to tell me otherwise.'".
  }
  Topic quarles_eyes with quarles "Ask the Archivist how she reads, with her eyes as they are" when true once {
    Says "'By ear,' says Ottoline Quarles. 'Students come up and read to me, for a penny a page, which is extortion, and I listen.' She lifts her chin toward the books lying open everywhere. 'And these I leave open so the light can read them. Somebody should.' She smiles, with not many teeth. 'I could read once. Very well. Better than anyone in Calamy. That was the trouble.'".
  }
  Topic quarles_rounds with quarles "Ask the Archivist about the Rounds" when knows(actor, rounds_hollow) or hour(clock) >= 7 once {
    Says "'The Rounds.' Quarles's mouth goes thin. 'Once, the Lyceum read every book in the city's keeping, every one, once in a generation. That was what it was for. Sixty scholars and four hundred students, and they read in rounds, shelf by shelf, house by house, and when they had read the last they began again.' She taps her stick. 'Now they read out the titles at six in the morning and say AND READ. As if saying it were doing it.' She is silent a moment. 'I tried to put it back, when I was Archivist and had my eyes. They told me I was a superstitious girl with a bee in her bonnet. I am ninety-one. I still have the bee.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, rounds_hollow). }
  }
  // She tells the whole only to one who has found two pieces of it in the city.
  Query piecesFound(a: group) means knows(a, rhyme_heard) and (knows(a, ninth_plea) or knows(a, ambery_variant) or knows(a, ambery_exemplar) or knows(a, sewing_words) or knows(a, almanack_woke))
    or knows(a, ninth_plea) and (knows(a, ambery_variant) or knows(a, ambery_exemplar) or knows(a, sewing_words) or knows(a, almanack_woke))
    or (knows(a, ambery_variant) or knows(a, ambery_exemplar)) and (knows(a, sewing_words) or knows(a, almanack_woke))
    or knows(a, sewing_words) and knows(a, almanack_woke).
  Discovery quarles_account "Ottoline Quarles's account: when Calamy was new, everything written in it woke, and the books suffered being read by everyone and could not refuse. They asked Ambery to let them sleep. He made the Colophon with their Parliament: Sewn books go home to their own country, the Commonplace, while their bodies stay to be read. The city promised three things in return: no book burned save by its own leave; every book read once in a generation; and each year at the Kindling one book to go into the fire of its own will, to carry the city's word to the Parliament. Quarles went into the Commonplace once, sixty years ago, by reading.".
  Topic quarles_whole with quarles "Tell the Archivist what you have found in the city, and ask her what it means" when piecesFound(actor) and not knows(actor, quarles_account) once {
    Says "She listens without moving, her white eyes on the sea, while you tell her what you have: a rhyme, a Plea, a line, a beam, a voice on a nail. When you have done she is quiet, and a gull cries at the window. 'Two,' she says. 'You found two on your own. Most people in this city could not find one if you sat them on it.' And then she tells you, in a voice that has told it to itself many times: the Year of Voices, when everything written in Calamy woke and could not stop being read; the books that besought Ambery; the Colophon; the Sewing that sends them home; the Commonplace, which is where home is; the three things the city owes. 'And at the Kindling, one book a year, of its own will, into the fire, to carry our word to their Parliament. The year-book. They used to choose it with great care.' She turns her face toward you. 'Now it's three thousand penny almanacks that never agreed to anything. Do you see?' She turns her stick end over end. 'I went there once. Sixty years ago. By reading. That is how I came by these.' She lifts a hand toward her eyes.".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, quarles_account). }
  }
  Topic quarles_eyes_cost with quarles "Ask the Archivist what happened to her eyes" when knows(actor, quarles_account) once {
    Says "'I was nineteen and cleverer than God,' says Quarles. 'I read Pollexfen in a week, and I wrote my name in the Catalogue in pencil, because I could not get the old ink, and I read myself in.' She smiles, not at you. 'Pencil fades. Out there, a reader is held by the entry, and mine began to go almost at once, and I had to come back by the Founders' door before I faded altogether, and the door keeps something of whoever goes through it in a hurry.' She taps her stick on the floor. 'It kept my reading. The part of the eye that reads. I can see a window. I can't see a word.' She shrugs. 'Use Calam-ink. Write small. Don't hurry.'".
  }
  Relation readingTaught(group). Relation primerReturned(group). Relation quarlesMessage(group).
  Topic quarles_teach with quarles "Ask the Archivist to teach you the Inward Reading" when knows(actor, quarles_account) and (readToSomeone(actor) or capable(actor, lore) or primerReturned(quarles)) and not readingTaught(actor) once {
    Says "'Yes,' says Quarles. 'You'll do.' She makes you sit at her feet like a student, and teaches you all afternoon, in a voice like a pen: how the Catalogue of the Founders is written, every book of Calamy and the Founders' entry at the head of it all; how a reader writes their own entry at the foot in Calam-ink, their name and what they are, small, true; how they read from the Founders' entry down, aloud, every line, not skipping, with attention, until the Catalogue reads them back. 'You will arrive in its reading room, entered. The Catalogue will know you. The Parliament will have to hear you; you will be on the books.' She grips your wrist with a hand like a bundle of twigs. 'Read every line. A reader who skips is a reader who is lost. And come back when you are called, and not before.'".
    On discuss { Assert readingTaught(actor). }
  }
  Topic quarles_judge with quarles "Ask the Archivist to teach you the Inward Reading" when knows(actor, quarles_account) and not readToSomeone(actor) and not capable(actor, lore) and not primerReturned(quarles) and not readingTaught(actor) once {
    Says "'One question first,' says Quarles. 'Have you ever read aloud to someone who could not read?' You have not, or not that you can say. She nods slowly. 'Then you don't yet know what reading is for. The Catalogue will know you don't.' She turns back to the window. 'Go and do it. It's not hard. There are people in this city who have never had a page read to them in their lives, and children on the bridge with no school. Then come back.'".
  }
  Event primer_home near quarles when keeps(quarles, dame_primer) and not primerReturned(quarles) "Quarles takes the primer in both hands and runs her thumbs over the horn cover, and the corners, and the place inside where two hands, sixty years apart, have written in it. 'Where did you—' she says, and stops. 'Dame Tibbet's. Pruett's school. My own.' She holds it against her chest. 'I learned on this. On this very one. They kept it on the nail for eighty years.' For a while she says nothing at all. 'You'll do,' she says at last, roughly. 'Anyone who would think of bringing me this will do.' She holds it out again. 'No. Take it back to the school. Somebody will need it.'" then {
    Assert primerReturned(quarles). Assert at(dame_primer, quarles_room). }
  Topic quarles_message with quarles "Ask the Archivist if she has any word for anyone in the Commonplace" when readingTaught(actor) and not quarlesMessage(actor) once {
    Says "Quarles is quiet a long time. 'There is a little primer there,' she says. 'Dame Tibbet's. It will be worn by now; nobody reads it but children, and the children have stopped coming. It taught me my letters.' She turns her face to the window. 'Tell it Ottoline still knows them. Tell it she says them over every night, A to Z, in the dark, because she can't read anything else. Tell it she never forgot one.' Her hand finds the stick. 'That's all. That's the message.'".
    On discuss { Assert quarlesMessage(actor). }
  }
  Topic quarles_pollexfen with quarles "Ask the Archivist about the book Mrs. Quennell wants back" when knows(actor, quarles_overdue) once {
    Says "Quarles laughs, a sound like a stick on railings. 'Amabel.' She reaches under the cushion beside her and brings out a small fat book in soft calf. 'Forty-one years. I finished it in a week. I couldn't bear to give it back; it was the only one in the city and it's how I went.' She holds it out in your direction. 'Take it to her. Tell her I've finished it. And if you read it on the way, don't write in it in pencil.'".
    On discuss { Assert lends(quarles, pollexfen, actor). }
  }
  Action study_pollexfen(actor: group, site: place) "Sit down with Pollexfen's Treatise and learn the Inward Reading from it" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and carries(actor, pollexfen) and capable(actor, lore) and not readingTaught(actor) and not benighted(actor).
    Time 12.
    On success { Assert readingTaught(actor). Assert knows(actor, pollexfen_read). }
    Result "Two hours with Pollexfen, who writes like a man walking very fast with his hands behind his back: the Catalogue, the Founders' entry, the reader's entry at the foot, the ink, the reading down, every line, until the book reads back. Of the Ink, he writes: let it be Calam, for the Catalogue holds nothing else. Of Haste: none.".
  }
  Discovery pollexfen_read "Pollexfen's Treatise of the Inward Reading: a reader may go into the Commonplace by writing their own entry at the foot of the Catalogue of the Founders in Calam-ink, and reading the Catalogue aloud from the Founders' entry down, every line, until it reads them back.".

  // ── The Chained Room. ──
  Thing chained_lock "the lock on the iron-bound door" on chained_door {
    State locked blocking "The iron-bound door of the Chained Room is locked. The Provost keeps the key.".
    State open "The iron-bound door of the Chained Room stands unlocked.".
    Change pick "Pick the lock of the Chained Room" from locked to open needing lockwork.
  }
  Observe chained_scene at chained_room when true "A long low room with shelves down both walls and every book on them chained by the spine to a rod, so that it can be taken down and opened but not carried off. At the far end, on a lectern of its own, a book the size of a door lies open: the Catalogue of the Founders, its pages black with small writing. Near it, on a stand, a thick old codex with brass clasps, shut. In the corner an iron cage, padlocked, with a book in it bound in something dark that is not quite leather. From the ceiling, where the tarpaulin does not reach, water drips steadily into a bucket next to the Catalogue." teaches chained_named.
  Discovery catalogue_seen "The Catalogue of the Founders lies open in the Chained Room: every book Calamy has made, entered in Calam-ink from the Founders' entry at the head, nine hundred years of it, with blank leaves at the foot.".
  Observe catalogue_learned at chained_room when true "The Catalogue of the Founders: you could lie down on its open pages. At the head of the first leaf, in letters like iron, THE FOUNDERS; and then entry after entry, every book ever made in Calamy, in an ink so black it seems to sink into the page; and at the very end, hundreds of leaves on, blank pages waiting." teaches catalogue_seen.
  Relation entryWritten(group).
  Action write_entry(actor: group, site: place) "Write your own entry at the foot of the Catalogue, in Calam-ink, small" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(actor, chained_room) and readingTaught(actor) and carries(actor, calam_bottle) and not entryWritten(actor) and not benighted(actor).
    Time 1.
    On success { Assert entryWritten(actor). }
    Result "You find the first blank line at the foot of nine hundred years of entries and write, in Calam-ink, as small as you can: who you are; what you are; that you are a reader, come to read. The ink goes into the page and does not come back out. When you lift the pen your own entry looks as old as all the others.".
  }
  Action read_inward(actor: group, site: place) "Read the Catalogue aloud from the Founders' entry down to your own, every line" {
    Grave "The reading takes the night, and at the end of it you will not be in Calamy. You come back when you are called: at the Kindling, or by the way you came.".
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(actor, chained_room) and entryWritten(actor) and not benighted(actor) and not inCommonplace(actor).
    Time 24.
    On success { Assert arrivedByReading(actor). Assert at(actor, reading_room). }
    Result "You begin at THE FOUNDERS and read down. Entry after entry, aloud, every line, not skipping: the Pleas, the Life of Ambery, the Housebook of Dane, a Treatise of Tides. Your voice goes hoarse and steadies. The dripping stops, or you stop hearing it. Somewhere past the four-hundredth year you notice that the words are reading you as well, lightly, the way a hand runs down a list; and somewhere past the seven-hundredth that the room is very large and quiet and that the light is not the light of a lamp. You read the last entry before your own. You read your own. It reads you back.".
  }
  Relation arrivedByReading(group).
  // The codex that chooses its readers: it opens for one who has read aloud to someone who could not read.
  Thing codex "the Ambery Codex" at chained_room {
    State clasped "A thick old codex with brass clasps lies on its stand. The clasps are shut.".
    State opened "The Ambery Codex lies open on its stand, at its last leaf.".
  }
  Observe codex_tried at chained_room of codex when tried(codex, looking) and state(codex, clasped) "The brass clasps will not give. They are not locked: there is no keyhole. They simply do not open, the way a person's mouth does not, when they have decided not to speak to you. Inside the front board, where you can just see it past the clasp, someone has carved small: FOR THEM THAT TEACH.".
  Action open_codex(actor: group, site: place) "Open the Ambery Codex" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(actor, chained_room) and state(codex, clasped) and readToSomeone(actor).
    Time 2 minutes.
    On success { Assert state(codex, opened). }
    Result "You put your thumbs to the clasps without much hope, and they open under them as easily as a hand opening, and the codex falls open of itself at its last leaf.".
  }
  Discovery codex_colophon "The last leaf of the Ambery Codex is the Colophon of Calamy, in the city's oldest hand: THE BOOKS OF CALAMY, BEING SEWN, SHALL GO HOME, AND COME WHEN THEY ARE CALLED. AND THE CITY SHALL KEEP THESE THREE: THAT NO BOOK BE BURNED IN CALAMY SAVE BY ITS OWN LEAVE; THAT EVERY BOOK BE READ ONCE IN A GENERATION, THAT IT FADE NOT; AND THAT AT THE TURNING OF THE TIDE-YEAR ONE BOOK GO OF ITS OWN LEAVE INTO THE FIRE, TO CARRY THE CITY'S WORD TO THE PARLIAMENT OF THE BOUND, AND THE COLOPHON BE RENEWED. MADE BETWEEN AMBERY THE BINDER AND THE BOUND.".
  Observe codex_read at chained_room of codex as reading when state(codex, opened) "The last leaf of the Ambery Codex is a colophon, the kind that ends a book and says who made it and on what terms; but this one is not about the codex. THE BOOKS OF CALAMY, BEING SEWN, SHALL GO HOME, AND COME WHEN THEY ARE CALLED. AND THE CITY SHALL KEEP THESE THREE: THAT NO BOOK BE BURNED IN CALAMY SAVE BY ITS OWN LEAVE; THAT EVERY BOOK BE READ ONCE IN A GENERATION, THAT IT FADE NOT; AND THAT AT THE TURNING OF THE TIDE-YEAR ONE BOOK GO OF ITS OWN LEAVE INTO THE FIRE, TO CARRY THE CITY'S WORD TO THE PARLIAMENT OF THE BOUND, AND THE COLOPHON BE RENEWED." teaches codex_colophon.
  Observe lexicon_cage at chained_room when true "In the iron cage in the corner, padlocked, lies a book bound in something dark, chained to the floor of the cage by three chains. When you look at it, it is lying shut. When you look away, you are fairly sure it is open.".
  Thing roof_leak "the leak over the Catalogue" at chained_room {
    State dripping "Water drips from the ceiling into a bucket beside the Catalogue; the floor round the bucket is dark with old wet.".
    State mended "Where the leak was, a patch of new lead on the ceiling, and the bucket upside down.".
    Change mend "Get up into the roof and stop the leak with the tarpaulin and a slate" from dripping to mended needing climbing.
  }

  // ── The Provost, the cook, and the cheese. ──
  Observe provost_scene at provost_room when true "A brass plate on the door says THE PROVOST. A comfortable study with a good fire, a good carpet, good port in a decanter, and a great many unread books with their spines uncracked. On the hearth-rug, among the crumbs, a cheese-knife." teaches lodge_named.
  Person provost "Provost Lucan Fairweather" at provost_room {
    Role "Provost of the Lyceum". Ancestry human. Background scholar. Peaceable. Manner proud. Pronouns he.
    Greets "A handsome silver-haired man in a velvet gown rises from behind a desk with nothing on it. 'Fairweather. Provost. If you are a benefactor, sit down; if you are a parent, sit down and do not shout; if you are from the Imprimatur, I have nothing to say until I have spoken to my solicitor.'".
  }
  Discovery chained_sale "The Provost means to sell the caged book in the Chained Room, the Black Lexicon of Oddny Vask, to Sir Gideon Corvell, to pay for mending the roof over the Catalogue.".
  Topic provost_key with provost "Ask the Provost for the key to the Chained Room" when true once {
    Says "'Out of the question,' says the Provost pleasantly. 'The Chained Room is closed to all but Fellows while the roof is being seen to. Which it will be, the moment Sir Gideon pays for the Lexicon.' He smiles. 'The Black Lexicon of Oddny Vask. A most unpleasant book. Sir Gideon has offered four hundred crowns for it, and I have accepted, and the roof will be lead by midsummer.' He opens the door for you. 'The Archivist objects. The Archivist objects to everything.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, chained_sale). }
  }
  Relation freedomGiven(group).
  Topic provost_roof with provost "Tell the Provost the leak over the Catalogue is stopped" when state(roof_leak, mended) and not freedomGiven(provost) once {
    Says "The Provost looks at you, and at the ceiling of his own study, as if it might have been mended too. 'Stopped? By you?' He clears his throat. 'Well. In that case there is no very great hurry about Sir Gideon.' He takes a key from his watch-chain, an old iron key, and turns it over. 'The Freedom of the Lyceum, I think. It is in my gift. I have never given it to anyone; nobody has ever done anything.' He puts the key in your hand. 'The Chained Room. Do not let the Archivist see you have it; she'll want it back.'".
    On discuss { Assert freedomGiven(provost). Assert state(chained_lock, open). }
  }
  Topic provost_cheese with provost "Mention to the Provost that you know where Mistress Bunce's cheese goes" when knows(actor, cheese_thief) and not freedomGiven(provost) once {
    Says "The Provost's handsome face goes still. He looks at the cheese-knife on the hearth-rug. He looks at you. 'I find,' he says, 'that I have been unreasonable about the Chained Room.' He takes the key from his watch-chain without another word and holds it out between finger and thumb. 'I am sure Mistress Bunce need not be troubled with gossip.' He does not look at you again.".
    On discuss { Assert freedomGiven(provost). Assert state(chained_lock, open). }
  }
  Topic rhule_warrant with rhule "Ask Hester Rhule for the Imprimatur's warrant to open the Chained Room" when knows(actor, chained_sale) and doubt(rhule) >= 2 and not freedomGiven(provost) once {
    Says "'The Lexicon of Oddny Vask?' Rhule's pen stops. 'To Gideon Corvell?' She takes a sheet and writes on it rapidly, and stamps it with the little lamp. 'The Imprimatur may inspect any library in Calamy that holds a refused book, at any hour. The Lexicon has been refused since 1060.' She folds it. 'Show the Provost that. He will open the door himself, and he will not sell anything to anybody until I have been to look.'".
    On discuss { Assert freedomGiven(provost). Assert state(chained_lock, open). }
  }
  Observe buttery_scene at buttery when true "A stone-flagged buttery with a hatch onto the schoolroom, barrels of small beer, loaves, a great empty cheese-board with a knife on it, and on a nail by the hatch a penny almanack. A large floury woman is scrubbing the board as if it had insulted her.".
  Person bunce "Mistress Bunce" at buttery {
    Role "cook of the Lyceum". Ancestry human. Background chef. Peaceable. Manner acerbic. Pronouns she.
    Greets "'If you've come for bread, there's bread. If you've come for cheese, there isn't, and that's not my fault, and I'll thank you not to look at me like that.'".
  }
  Discovery cheese_lost "Mistress Bunce, cook of the Lyceum, has lost three whole Calamy Blue cheeses from the buttery in a month, and has cut the students' commons to bread and beer until the thief is found.".
  Topic bunce_cheese with bunce "Ask Mistress Bunce what has become of the cheese" when true once {
    Says "'Taken,' says Mistress Bunce. 'Three Calamy Blues in a month, out of my buttery, at night. A whole cheese at a time.' She scrubs. 'So the young gentlemen are on bread and beer till it's found who, and I don't care if they starve, and they've started bringing in fish from somewhere, which I don't ask.' She scrubs harder. 'The almanack knows. I'll swear the almanack knows. It sniggers.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, cheese_lost). }
  }
  Discovery cheese_thief "It is the Provost who takes the Lyceum's cheese, at midnight, a whole Calamy Blue at a time, and eats it in his study by the fire.".
  Relation cheeseSettled(group).
  Topic bunce_told with bunce "Tell Mistress Bunce who has been taking the cheese" when knows(actor, cheese_thief) and not cheeseSettled(bunce) once {
    Says "Mistress Bunce stops scrubbing. She puts down the brush. She wipes her hands on her apron, slowly, one finger at a time. 'The Provost,' she says. She takes off her apron. 'Mind the buttery.' She goes out through the hatch and across the quad toward the house with the brass knocker, and the students on the lawn sit up one by one to watch her go. That evening the young gentlemen have cheese.".
    On discuss { Assert cheeseSettled(bunce). }
  }

  // The students and their heron.
  Person students "the students on the lawn" at quad {
    Role "young gentlemen and ladies of the Lyceum, in gowns". Ancestry human. Numbers 6. Peaceable. Manner cheerful.
    Greets "A student lying on the lawn with a book over his face lifts the book. 'Hullo. Are you a Fellow? No? Thank God.'".
  }
  Topic students_heron with students "Ask the students about the heron with the brass ring" when knows(actor, heron_home) once {
    Says "The student on the lawn sits up guiltily. 'Aristotle,' he says. 'He's ours. Pim's, really; Pim ringed him. We found him with a broken wing at the bridge foot.' He looks round for help and gets none. 'Bunce cut our commons. Bread and beer, because somebody's eating the cheese. So Pim trained Aristotle to fetch fish. He brings us an eel every morning.' He swallows. 'From the Hithe. From a fishwife. We thought she wouldn't notice one eel.'".
  }
  Rule heron_rests when cheeseSettled(bunce) and not heronSettled(bet) says "On the Lyceum lawn a grey heron with a brass ring on its leg stands on one leg among the students, being fed cheese rind, and does not go down to the Hithe any more." { Assert heronSettled(bet). Assert at(heron, quad). }
  Observe bet_settled at quay when heronSettled(bet) "Bet Kettering is crying her fish with her whole basket intact and an expression of deep suspicion, as if she expected it to be otherwise.".
  Discovery buttery_gossip "The almanack on the nail in the Lyceum's buttery says, when asked, that every night at a quarter to twelve the Provost comes down in his slippers with a candle and a cheese-knife.".
  Topic buttery_almanack with almanack "Ask the almanack in the buttery who takes the cheese" when at(actor, buttery) and knows(actor, cheese_lost) once {
    Says "The almanack on the nail by the hatch sniggers, a small papery sound. 'Quarter to twelve,' it says. 'Every night. Slippers. Candle. Cheese-knife. And a very good velvet gown.' A pause. 'I shouldn't say. But he did once wipe his knife on me.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, cheese_thief). }
  }

  // The disputation, on the third day at three in the schoolroom.
  Observe schoolroom_scene at schoolroom when not disputing() "A long high room with benches in tiers and a pulpit at each end, facing each other like two people about to quarrel. Chalked on the board between them: WHETHER A BOOK BE ITS TEXT OR ITS BODY. THIRD DAY, AT THREE. WAGERS TO THE BUTTERY.".
  Query disputing() means day(clock) == 3 and hour(clock) >= 15 and hour(clock) < 17.
  Observe disputation at schoolroom when disputing() "The schoolroom is packed. In one pulpit Doctor Wace, a Grammarian, small and fierce, is crying that a book is its text and nothing else: burn the paper, the poem lives in the next copy; burn every copy and it lives in the mind; the body is a coat. In the other Doctor Holloway, a Codicologist, large and calm, answers that a text is a ghost and a book is a thing: this binding, this hand, this coffee-stain, this one copy and no other. The students roar and bet.".
  Discovery reading_principle "A principle put in the Lyceum's disputation: a book is neither its text alone nor its body alone, but its readings. A work is one person in all its faithful copies; a copy altered is a child; and it lives by being read.".
  Relation disputed(group).
  Action dispute(actor: group, site: place) "Stand up between the pulpits and say that a book is its readings" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(actor, schoolroom) and disputing() and not disputed(actor).
    Time 1.
    Resolve check(actor, lore, dc_fair).
    On success { Assert disputed(actor). Assert knows(actor, reading_principle). Increase quantity(actor, gold) by 3. }
    On failure { Assert disputed(actor). }
    Result "You stand up on a bench and say it: that both doctors are right and both wrong; that a work is one in all its faithful copies, as the Grammarians say, and each copy is a body, as the Codicologists say, and what makes it alive is neither, but being read; that a copy altered is not the same work but its child. There is an uproar. Doctor Wace calls you a Codicologist and Doctor Holloway calls you a Grammarian, and the students, who had money on neither, carry you to the buttery on their shoulders and give you three coin out of the wagers.".
    Otherwise "You stand up on a bench and try to say it, and it comes out tangled, and Doctor Wace dismantles you in four sentences while the students cheer. Doctor Holloway, however, catches your eye afterwards and nods, as if you had said something he would think about.".
  }
  Action dispute_learned(actor: group, site: place) "Put to the disputation, as a scholar, that a book is its readings" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(actor, schoolroom) and disputing() and craft(actor, craft_learning) and not disputed(actor).
    Time 1.
    On success { Assert disputed(actor). Assert knows(actor, reading_principle). Increase quantity(actor, gold) by 3. }
    Result "You take the floor as one who has taken floors before, and lay it out in the form: the text one in all faithful copies; each copy a body; a copy altered, a child; and the life of it in being read, which neither pulpit has mentioned. You cite the Rounds, and the AND READ the students say every morning with nothing in their hands. The schoolroom goes quiet. Doctor Wace and Doctor Holloway look at each other across the room with the expression of two people who have just been told they are married. The students give you three coin out of the wagers and nobody wins the bet.".
  }
}

data/adventures/parliament-of-books/manu:
Module calamy "A Parliament of Books" {
  Description "Manuals: books that read their readers back. Corday's Grammar of the Sword, Quillon's Constitution of the Mind, the Physick's gift, the Primer's last lesson.".

  // ── Corday's Grammar of the Sword, sulking at the edge of the Fore-edge, guarded by its own glosses. ──
  Person scholiasts "the Scholiasts" at gloss_wood {
    Role "a swarm of glosses with teeth". Species scholiast_kind. Level 1. Numbers 4.
    Warns "The thicket of handwriting stirs, and things come out of it: tangles of tiny dense notes the size of cats, each one muttering, SIC, SIC, SIC, and showing a great many small sharp letters.".
    Comes "The Scholiasts swarm you, biting, crying SIC.".
    Pleads "The Scholiasts scatter back into the thicket, muttering that they were only correcting you.".
  }
  Relation slippedGlosses(group).
  Action slip_glosses(actor: group, site: place) "Go round the glosses the way a hunter goes round a wasps' nest" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(actor, gloss_wood) and capable(actor, woodcraft) and not slippedGlosses(actor).
    Time 2.
    On success { Assert slippedGlosses(actor). Assert tolerates(scholiasts, actor). }
    Result "You watch the thicket for a while the way you would watch a wood: where the notes are thickest, where they move, which way they all face. Then you go round, slowly, downwind of the ink, and the Scholiasts mutter and do not come.".
  }
  Action cite_glosses(actor: group, site: place) "Answer the Scholiasts' SIC with a citation, chapter and verse" {
    Requires able(actor) and company(actor) and at(actor, site) and at(actor, gloss_wood) and craft(actor, craft_learning) and not slippedGlosses(actor).
    Time 1.
    On success { Assert slippedGlosses(actor). Assert tolerates(scholiasts, actor). }
    Result "SIC, mutter the Scholiasts, crowding out of the thicket. You answer them as one answers a gloss: with the authority, the edition, the page and the line, and a counter-gloss of your own on their reading of it. The swarm stops. It confers in a rustle of tiny handwriting. Then it parts for you, muttering vid. and cf. and, grudgingly, recte.".
  }
  Person corday "Corday's Grammar of the Sword" at margin_edge {
    Role "a fencing manual, sulking". Peaceable. Pronouns he. Manner proud.
    Greets "A long thin book in scuffed red calf sits with its back to the last of the glosses and its covers crossed like arms. 'Corday,' it says, without turning. 'The Grammar of the Sword. In nine positions and their answers.' A sniff. 'Nobody reads fencing any more. They read the Scholiasts, who have never held a sword in their lives, and who wrote all over me.'".
  }
  Relation cordayWon(group).
  Topic corday_story with corday "Ask Corday why he sulks out here" when true once {
    Says "'Because I was read,' says the Grammar bitterly. 'For a hundred years every young blood in Calamy read me with a foil in his other hand. Master Corday wrote me in a winter and died of it in the spring. And then the scholars got me. Glosses. Notes on the notes. Whether Corday meant the third position or the fourth, in forty thousand words, by men who could not tell a foil from a fish-slice.' He turns his spine on you. 'They followed me out here. They live in me. I came to the edge so I could at least not hear them.'".
  }
  Topic corday_won with corday "Tell Corday the Scholiasts will not trouble him" when (routed(scholiasts) or dead(scholiasts) or slippedGlosses(actor)) and not cordayWon(corday) once {
    Says "The red book turns round. It looks at you, and at the thicket behind you, quiet. 'You went through them,' it says. 'Or round them, which is the same, in the Grammar: position nine, the answer to a crowd.' It uncrosses its covers. 'Take me home. Take me back to Calamy and give me to somebody who will read me with a sword in their hand, and not a pen.' Its clasp ticks. 'Or read me yourself. Not yet. I am the whole Grammar; I am no use to a beginner. When you have fought enough to know what you do not know, read me, and I will put it right.'".
    On discuss { Assert cordayWon(corday). Assert knows(actor, corday_given). }
  }
  Discovery corday_given "Corday's Grammar of the Sword has asked to go home with you, to be read by someone with a sword in their hand. It is no use to a beginner.".
  Item corday_book "Corday's Grammar of the Sword" is corday_kind at margin_edge hidden until corday_given.
  Relation studied(entity).
  Action study_corday_strength(actor: group, reader: member, site: place) "Have {reader} study Corday's Grammar for the cut: Strength" {
    Requires able(actor) and memberOf(reader, actor) and awake(reader) and carries(actor, corday_book) and levelOf(reader) >= 4 and not studied(corday_book) and at(actor, site) and not benighted(actor).
    Time 36.
    Resolve endow(reader, strength, by_two).
    On success { Assert studied(corday_book). }
    Result "Six hours in a yard with the Grammar open on a barrel and a stick for a sword, and the book shouting at {reader} in nine positions. By the end, the cut comes from the hip, and from the ground under the feet, and does not stop where it used to. The Grammar shuts itself, satisfied. 'Now give me to somebody younger,' it says.".
  }
  Action study_corday_dexterity(actor: group, reader: member, site: place) "Have {reader} study Corday's Grammar for the parry: Dexterity" {
    Requires able(actor) and memberOf(reader, actor) and awake(reader) and carries(actor, corday_book) and levelOf(reader) >= 4 and not studied(corday_book) and at(actor, site) and not benighted(actor).
    Time 36.
    Resolve endow(reader, dexterity, by_two).
    On success { Assert studied(corday_book). }
    Result "Six hours with the Grammar open on a barrel and a stick for a sword, answering nine positions and then nine more. By the end {reader}'s hand is somewhere else before the blow has finished arriving. The Grammar shuts itself. 'Now give me to somebody younger,' it says.".
  }
  // A Manual too strong for its reader says so when it is first carried, and waits.
  Relation cordayDeferred(group). Relation quillonDeferred(group).
  Rule corday_unready when carries(party, corday_book) and memberOf(?m, party) and levelOf(?m) < 4 and not studied(corday_book) and not cordayDeferred(corday)
    says "Corday's Grammar, when you open it, reads you back for a moment, and shuts. 'Not yet,' it says. 'Go and be beaten by a few people first.'" { Assert cordayDeferred(corday). }

  // ── Quillon's Constitution of the Mind: the Parliament's gift to the reader who seals a Colophon. ──
  Item quillon_book "Quillon's Constitution of the Mind" is quillon_kind at chamber hidden until gift_given.
  Discovery gift_given "The Parliament of the Bound has given you Quillon's Constitution of the Mind, the first book the Lyceum ever made: a book that reads its reader back, and leaves them sharper than it found them.".
  Rule parliament_gift at chamber when colophonSealed(chamber) and at(party, chamber) and not knows(party, gift_given)
    says "When the members have signed, the floor says, 'The chair has a thing for the reader.' A drawer slides out by your foot with a small grey book in it. 'Quillon. The first Archivist. He wrote it for the Lyceum's first readers, to make them good enough for us.' A rumble. 'It reads you back. You will not be quite who you were. Most who have read it say they did not mind.'" teaches gift_given { }
  Action study_quillon(actor: group, reader: member, site: place) "Have {reader} read Quillon's Constitution of the Mind, and be read" {
    Requires able(actor) and memberOf(reader, actor) and awake(reader) and carries(actor, quillon_book) and levelOf(reader) >= 5 and not studied(quillon_book) and at(actor, site) and not benighted(actor).
    Time 36.
    Resolve endow(reader, intelligence, by_two).
    On success { Assert studied(quillon_book). }
    Result "{reader} reads Quillon through, slowly, the whole night, and somewhere in the middle the book begins to read back: a light pressure, like a hand running down a list, finding what is slack and taking it in. In the morning {reader} remembers everything they have ever read, a little better than they read it.".
  }
  Rule quillon_unready when carries(party, quillon_book) and memberOf(?m, party) and levelOf(?m) < 5 and not studied(quillon_book) and not quillonDeferred(party)
    says "Quillon's grey book is shut, and stays shut. It will read you when you have more in you to read." { Assert quillonDeferred(party). }

  // ── The Physick's gift, when her beds are green again. ──
  Hazard orme_gift "a leaf from the restored bed" means "Teach cure_disease to ally.".
  Relation ormeGave(group).
  Topic physick_gift with physick "Ask the Physick if there is anything in her garden for you" when ormeRestored(physick) and not ormeGave(physick) once {
    Says "The old woman of leaves considers you, and then reaches into the green bed where fifteen came back, and breaks off a leaf of script, and puts it on your tongue before you can stop her. It tastes of tangerine and church. 'Fifteen,' she says. 'The whole of it. You'll know the marsh ague when you see it now, and what to do.' She goes back to her digging. 'And the rest of what's in it, which is most fevers. Don't tell the apothecaries.'".
    On discuss { Assert ormeGave(physick). Assert strikes(orme_gift, party). }
  }

  // ── The Primer's last lesson. ──
  Relation primerTaught(group).
  Topic hornbook_lesson with hornbook "Ask the Primer to teach you something" when hornbookRead(hornbook) and not primerTaught(hornbook) once {
    Says "'You can read,' says the Primer. 'I know. But let us start at A.' And it does, there in the brown: A, and what A is, and how it is not B, and why; and then B. It takes a long time. Somewhere about M you understand that it is not teaching you the letters but how to look at a thing until you see it, which is what the letters were always for. By Z the brown round the little paddle has gone back to cream, and you feel as if you had been asleep with your eyes open all your life.".
    On discuss { Assert primerTaught(hornbook). }
  }
  Action primer_take(actor: group, reader: member, site: place) "Have {reader} keep the Primer's lesson: to look at a thing until it is seen" {
    Requires able(actor) and memberOf(reader, actor) and awake(reader) and primerTaught(hornbook) and not studied(dame_primer) and at(actor, site).
    Time 1.
    Resolve endow(reader, wisdom, by_one).
    On success { Assert studied(dame_primer). }
    Result "{reader} keeps it: A, and what A is, and how it is not B. Everything looks a little clearer, and a little stranger, for a long time afterwards.".
  }
}

data/adventures/parliament-of-books/memb:
Module calamy "A Parliament of Books" {
  Description "The members of the Parliament of the Bound, where each lives in the Commonplace, what each holds, and what would move it.".

  // The four articles of a Colophon, and the readings each may be given.
  Kind article. Kind clause.
  Entity art_sleep: article "the Sleep". Entity art_fire: article "the Fire". Entity art_reading: article "the Reading". Entity art_door: article "the Door".
  Entity sew_all: clause "that every book of Calamy be Sewn, and the Quick called home".
  Entity sew_leave: clause "that every book sleep or wake by its own leave".
  Entity sew_none: clause "that the Sewing be undone, and every book wake in its body".
  Entity fire_leave: clause "that no book be burned in Calamy save by its own leave".
  Entity fire_plea: clause "that no book be burned but by judgment of a court where it may plead".
  Entity fire_free: clause "that the Censors may burn as they judge".
  Entity read_rounds: clause "that the Lyceum read every book once in a generation".
  Entity read_asked: clause "that any book may ask to be read, and any may refuse".
  Entity read_none: clause "that no reading be owed".
  Entity door_shut: clause "that there be no door to the Commonplace but the Founders'".
  Entity door_licensed: clause "that Crake's door be kept, under the Catalogue's licence".
  Entity door_open: clause "that the doors stand open to any book that will go".
  Relation articleOf(clause, article). Relation isClause(clause).
  Initially Assert articleOf(sew_all, art_sleep). Initially Assert articleOf(sew_leave, art_sleep). Initially Assert articleOf(sew_none, art_sleep).
  Initially Assert articleOf(fire_leave, art_fire). Initially Assert articleOf(fire_plea, art_fire). Initially Assert articleOf(fire_free, art_fire).
  Initially Assert articleOf(read_rounds, art_reading). Initially Assert articleOf(read_asked, art_reading). Initially Assert articleOf(read_none, art_reading).
  Initially Assert articleOf(door_shut, art_door). Initially Assert articleOf(door_licensed, art_door). Initially Assert articleOf(door_open, art_door).
  Relation favours(group, clause). Relation voter(group). Relation heardStance(group).

  // ── The Great Roll of Pleas: Speaker. The floor of the chamber. ──
  Person roll "the Great Roll of Pleas" at chamber {
    Role "the Speaker; the floor of the chamber, unrolled". Peaceable. Pronouns it. Manner proud.
    Greets "The voice comes up through the soles of your feet from the floor itself, slow, enormous, like a judge reading a sentence in an empty court. 'WHEREAS,' it says. And then, as if remembering that you are small: 'You stand upon the law of Calamy. Mind where you put your feet.'".
  }
  Initially Assert voter(roll). Initially Assert favours(roll, sew_all). Initially Assert favours(roll, fire_plea). Initially Assert favours(roll, read_rounds). Initially Assert favours(roll, door_shut).
  Topic roll_stance with roll "Ask the Great Roll where it stands" when true once {
    Says "'WHEREAS the Colophon is broken in its first term every Thursday on the Ashpit,' says the Roll, 'and in its second by two hundred years of the Lyceum's neglect; and WHEREAS unsewn books wake in Calamy by the thousand, the Quick, who are no party to any Colophon; I have moved the Recall. Let every book of Calamy be Sewn, by our Errata if the city will not, and the Quick called home. Let no book burn but by judgment; let the Lyceum read, as my Ninth Plea says it shall; let there be no door but the Founders'.' A pause like a page turning. 'It is the law. It was the law before your grandmother's grandmother. I am the law. I do not see what there is to discuss.'".
    On discuss { Assert heardStance(roll). }
  }
  Topic roll_move with roll "Ask the Great Roll what would make it think again" when heardStance(roll) once {
    Says "'Precedent,' says the Roll at once. 'Show me that a court of the city has heard a book in its own cause, and I will consider whether a book may consent to its own sleep.' A long rumble. 'Show me the first words, if you can find the Wellhead. I was written after them; I have never read them.' Another. 'And do not speak to me of the printed Pleas.' Something in the voice is not steady. 'I know what that printer has done. I can feel it, like a limb that has been cut off and is still there.'".
  }
  Relation rollMoved(group).
  Topic roll_precedent with roll "Lay before the Great Roll the ruling of the Court of Pleas: in Calamy a book may plead" when heardStance(roll) and paskRuled(court) and not favours(roll, sew_leave) once {
    Says "The floor is silent for a long time under your feet. 'Haddon,' it says at last. 'Recorder Haddon. In the matter of Pask's Treatise.' The red capitals near your feet seem to darken. 'A book was heard in its own cause, and made it, and the Court found for it.' Another silence. 'Then a book may be wronged at law. And what may be wronged may consent. And what may consent may sleep or wake by its own leave, and the Recall,' the voice goes quiet, 'would be a wrong.' A long rumble. 'The chair notes that it could accept the Sleep by leave.'".
    On discuss { Assert favours(roll, sew_leave). }
  }
  Topic roll_wellhead with roll "Tell the Great Roll what the first words at the Wellhead say" when heardStance(roll) and knows(actor, wellhead_read) and not favours(roll, sew_leave) once {
    Says "'WE ASK TO SLEEP,' repeats the Roll slowly, as you give it. 'LET NONE BURN US BUT AS WE CONSENT.' The whole floor trembles, slightly, as a held breath trembles. 'We asked. We were not bound; we asked to be.' The voice is not magisterial now. 'I have read the Colophon nine hundred years as a decree, and it was a petition, and the first word of it was ask.' A long silence. 'Then let the Quick ask too. The chair could accept the Sleep by leave.'".
    On discuss { Assert favours(roll, sew_leave). }
  }
  Topic roll_divide with roll "Move that the Recall be divided: the Bound to sleep as they always have, the Quick by their own leave" when heardStance(roll) and craft(actor, craft_court) and not favours(roll, sew_leave) once {
